window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["jl"]=[{"id":"jl-front-bumper","title":"Steel Front Bumper \u2014 Budget Through Premium Options","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":449,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack (optional)","socket set","torque wrench","wire loom (for sensor relocation)","trim panel tool"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics","hardware installation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front-bumper","winch-ready"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Comp Series Front Bumper (JL)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Warn Ascent Front Bumper (JL)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":999},{"name":"Fab Four Stubby Front Bumper (JL)","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartan Front Bumper (JL)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":549}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Ascent JL Front Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com/bumpers-jl-wrangler-ascent-front-bumper"},{"title":"Fab Four Stubby JL Bumper Overview","url":"https://www.fabfourusa.com/jeep/wrangler-jl/bumpers-jl"},{"title":"Rugged Ridge Spartan Bumper JL","url":"https://www.ruggedridge.com/bumpers/spartan-bumper-jl"}],"alternatives":["jl-rear-bumper","jl-winch","jl-sliders"],"summary":"The JL's factory front fascia is plastic and won't survive a significant trail hit \u2014 a steel bumper is the foundation of any trail-ready build, and the right choice depends heavily on whether your rig has front parking sensors.","description":"The JL Wrangler introduced front parking sensors on certain trims and packages \u2014 specifically, the Sahara with the Safety & Security Group and any JL equipped with the Park Assist package. These sensors sit in the factory plastic fascia and disappear when you swap to a steel bumper. Some aftermarket bumpers are designed to accommodate the factory sensors with integrated sensor ports; others are not. Before ordering any bumper, pull your window sticker or check the Uconnect settings screen to confirm whether your JL has front sensors. If it does, verify explicitly that your chosen bumper is sensor-compatible or accept that the sensors will become non-functional.\n\nThe Smittybilt XRC Comp at $499 is the entry-level choice with a winch-ready plate welded in. The construction is heavier gauge than the factory fascia but lighter than the mid-range and premium bumpers \u2014 adequate for trail use that doesn't regularly involve direct rock contact. If your budget tops out here, it's a functional platform. The Rugged Ridge Spartan at $549 is the other budget-accessible option; similar gauge to the Smittybilt with a slightly more refined design and sensor port provisions on most variants.\n\nThe Warn Ascent at $999 is the value-to-quality sweet spot for most JL builds. Warn's bumper engineering is done in-house and the Ascent is designed specifically around Warn winch mounting dimensions \u2014 if you're running a Warn VR EVO or Zeon, the fit is precise. The Ascent also provides better approach angle than most stubby designs while maintaining a lower front profile that keeps headlight sightlines intact.\n\nThe Fab Four Stubby at $1,399 is the premium choice for rock-build rigs where approach angle is critical. The stubby design removes the outer wings of the factory bumper envelope entirely, dramatically improving the approach angle \u2014 the difference is visible and meaningful on off-camber approaches. The tradeoff is reduced frontal protection on the outer edges and no parking sensor provision. Fab Four's fabrication quality is excellent and the finish holds up well.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal before starting \u2014 parking sensor wiring and any ambient temperature sensor leads need to be disconnected safely.","Remove the factory plastic front fascia. Most JL fascias are held by push-pin retainers along the top edge, two bolts in the wheel wells, and clips along the lower valance. A trim panel tool and patience beats a screwdriver and frustration here.","Disconnect all sensor connectors attached to the factory fascia \u2014 parking sensors, fog light harnesses, ambient temperature sensor. Label connectors before unplugging.","Remove the factory crash bar/impact absorber. This bolts to the frame horns with four bolts.","If the bumper kit includes a fog light relocation bracket or sensor housing, install it now before the bumper goes on.","With a helper, lift the new bumper into position and start all bolts finger-tight.","Confirm bumper is square to the vehicle before torquing. Small asymmetries become obvious at distance.","Torque mounting hardware to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 60\u201380 ft-lb on the main frame mount bolts.","Reconnect fog lights, relocate parking sensor harnesses to bumper sensor ports if present.","If running a winch: mount winch before final bumper install when possible \u2014 much easier than fishing the fairlead through the bumper opening after the fact.","Reconnect battery, test all lights and sensor functions."],"pros":["Steel construction provides protection that factory plastic cannot \u2014 trail-going JLs should have a steel bumper","Most options in this range include a winch-ready plate \u2014 a significant value-add given winch mounting hardware complexity","Warn Ascent's purpose-built compatibility with Warn winches eliminates fitment guesswork","Fab Four Stubby approach-angle improvement is meaningful on technical trails","Rugged Ridge and Smittybilt options deliver core protection at accessible price points"],"cons":["Parking sensor compatibility must be verified before purchase \u2014 this is the most common ordering mistake on JL bumper installs","Full-width steel bumpers add 50\u201390 lbs to the front end, affecting steering feel and approach weight","Premium options (Fab Four, Warn) represent significant investment \u2014 budget total build cost including winch before committing to top-tier bumper","Stubby designs sacrifice outer approach protection for angle \u2014 not ideal for builds that see frequent brush or trail debris","Factory fog lights typically require relocation hardware (sometimes sold separately) \u2014 verify what's included"]},{"id":"jl-front-bumper-guide","title":"JL Wrangler Front Bumper: Best Options and Installation","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":1800},"tools_required":["1/2\" drive ratchet","metric and SAE socket set","torque wrench","trim panel tools","wire stripper and crimping tool (if adding lights)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic wiring for fog light integration"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","armor_bumper_front","bumper","trail","protection"],"summary":"A steel front bumper on the JL serves two purposes: trail protection and mounting surface for lights, winches, and recovery gear. Budget $400\u2013$800 for a mid-tier bumper that handles real trail use. The factory bumper plastic is cosmetically fine for pavement but will crack on any meaningful ledge contact.","description":"# JL Wrangler Front Bumper: Best Options and Installation\n\nThe JL's factory front bumper is plastic over a steel tube \u2014 it looks integrated, but the plastic fascia cracks on first contact with a rock. For trail use, a steel replacement bumper changes the math: instead of managing around obstacles, you can lean on the bumper and let it do its job.\n\n**Full-width vs. stubby/shorty:**\nFull-width bumpers provide more corner protection and look factory. Stubby bumpers improve approach angle (critical if you're running bigger tires) and are lighter. For serious trails with steep ledge entries, stubby is the better tool.\n\n**Winch-ready vs. non-winch:**\nWinch-capable bumpers have a reinforced mounting plate and enough depth to house a full-size winch. If you're not running a winch now but might later, buy winch-ready \u2014 the price difference is $50\u2013$100 and swapping bumpers costs more.\n\n**Light tabs:**\nMost aftermarket bumpers include tabs for 3\" cube lights or a 50\" light bar. Verify tab location and weld quality before buying \u2014 flimsy tabs vibrate loose.\n\n| Bumper | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Smittybilt XRC | $350\u2013$450 | Budget-tier; acceptable for occasional trail use; thinner steel |\n| Poison Spyder Brawler Lite | $550\u2013$700 | Mid-tier; good approach angle; winch-ready |\n| Rugged Ridge Spartan | $500\u2013$650 | Full-width or stubby; solid trail-proven design |\n| Warn Axon | $700\u2013$900 | High-tier; winch-integrated version available |\n| Metalcloak Game Changer | $900\u2013$1,100 | Premium; one of the best approach angles on the market |\n| Poison Spyder Brawler Full | $950\u2013$1,200 | Best combination of strength and clearance at this price |\n\nAmazon: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07KWJ2Q9F?tag=trailmanual0b-20 (Rugged Ridge Spartan)\n\n**Remove the factory bumper**\n1. Disconnect the two fog light connectors inside the bumper opening \u2014 squeeze the tab and pull.\n2. There are four main mounting bolts attaching the bumper brackets to the frame horns \u2014 two per side, typically 15mm or 18mm. Remove them and catch the bumper.\n3. The factory tow hooks may be reused with some aftermarket bumpers. Set them aside.\n\n**Mount the new bumper**\n4. Thread the new bumper onto the frame horn bolts by hand with a helper. Factory frame horn holes typically accept M12 bolts.\n5. Confirm clearance around the radiator and hood. Snug the bolts finger-tight and adjust side-to-side until the bumper is centered.\n6. Torque the mounting bolts to 55\u201365 ft-lbs (verify with your specific bumper's instructions \u2014 some require higher torque on their custom hardware).\n\n**Wire lighting (if applicable)**\n7. If the bumper has built-in fog light mounts, splice into the factory fog light wiring or run new wires to the factory aux switches. Use weatherproof connectors; the front of the JL sees full water exposure.\n\n- **Approach angle**: Measure clearance between the lowest point of your new bumper and the ground with tires aired down. A bumper that hangs below the axle line gains nothing on trail.\n- **Hood clearance**: Some bumpers require a 1/4\"\u20131/2\" hood lift spacer to clear. Check the product page \u2014 most manufacturers note this.\n- **Powder coat quality**: Budget bumpers often have thin powder coat that chips quickly. Touch-up paint ($10\u2013$15 a can) preserves the steel beneath it.\n- **Grille protection**: Some JL bumpers leave the grille exposed. If you trail hard, a grille guard or pre-runner loop is worth adding.\n\nMid-tier budget: $550\u2013$750 covers a quality bumper with winch capacity. Spending over $1,000 buys measurably better steel thickness and precision fitment \u2014 worth it if you're at trails like Moab or Rubicon regularly. Below $400, quality becomes inconsistent."},{"id":"jl-rear-bumper-guide","title":"JL Wrangler Rear Bumper: Options, Tire Carriers, and Installation","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":2500},"tools_required":["1/2\" drive ratchet","metric and SAE socket set","torque wrench","drill with step bit (some installs)","wire stripper"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","armor_bumper_rear","bumper","tire-carrier","trail"],"summary":"A JL rear bumper with integrated tire carrier handles full-size spare tires (35\"-37\") that the factory carrier can't support. Budget $600\u2013$1,200 for a solid unit. Tire carrier hinges take real stress \u2014 buy quality here; a failed carrier at speed is a safety issue.","description":"# JL Wrangler Rear Bumper: Options, Tire Carriers, and Installation\n\nThe factory JL rear bumper is plastic with a swing-arm spare tire carrier rated for stock tire sizes. Running 35\" tires on it will eventually crack the mounting and stress the tailgate hinges. A steel rear bumper with a purpose-built tire carrier solves this, and adds trail approach clearance and a solid mounting surface for lights and recovery gear.\n\n**Tire carrier or no carrier:**\nIf you're running 35\"+ tires, you need a dedicated carrier. The factory swing arm isn't rated for the weight. If you're staying at stock tire size, a non-carrier steel bumper is sufficient and cheaper.\n\n**Single-swing vs. dual-swing:**\nSingle-swing carriers have one door: the spare swings out on a hinged arm attached to the bumper. Dual-swing designs have two doors: one for the spare, one for tailgate access. Dual-swing adds weight and complexity but is the more practical choice for daily drivers who access the cargo area frequently.\n\n| Bumper | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rough Country with tire carrier | $400\u2013$550 | Budget; functional for moderate trail use |\n| Smittybilt XRC with carrier | $550\u2013$750 | Mid-budget; solid hinges; limited clearance options |\n| Rugged Ridge Atlas | $700\u2013$950 | Strong carrier arm; good trail clearance |\n| Poison Spyder Brawler Lite Rear | $800\u2013$1,100 | Purpose-built for sustained trail use |\n| Genright Ultra HD | $1,000\u2013$1,300 | Excellent carrier bearing; premium steel |\n| Warn Elite | $1,200\u2013$1,600 | High-tier; carrier rated for 40\" tire |\n| Metalcloak Overland | $1,400\u2013$2,000 | Best clearance and hinge design on market |\n\nAmazon: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B08CJQY1PS?tag=trailmanual0b-20 (Rugged Ridge Atlas)\n\n**Remove the factory bumper and carrier**\n1. Disconnect the license plate light wiring \u2014 one connector behind the bumper.\n2. Remove the spare tire from the factory carrier.\n3. The factory bumper attaches via bolts through the frame rails \u2014 typically 15mm or 18mm, four bolts total.\n4. Disconnect the backup camera wiring if equipped (connector inside the bumper).\n5. Remove the factory carrier swing arm from the tailgate \u2014 it bolts to the tailgate itself, usually two or three bolts.\n\n**Install the new bumper**\n6. With a helper, position the new bumper against the frame horns and thread the mounting hardware by hand.\n7. Confirm the bumper sits level \u2014 measure from the top of the bumper to a fixed reference point on both sides.\n8. Torque the mounting bolts to spec (typically 55\u201380 ft-lbs depending on manufacturer).\n\n**Mount the tire carrier**\n9. The tire carrier arm bolts to the bumper's hinge point. Thread all bolts before torquing any \u2014 carrier hinges need even pressure across all bolt faces.\n10. Torque the carrier hinge bolts to manufacturer spec. This is critical: a loose hinge allows the carrier to sag, causing the spare to bounce and the arm to crack at the weld.\n11. Mount the spare tire to the carrier. Some carriers include an adjustable tire bracket that needs to be positioned for your wheel's bolt pattern.\n\n**Rewire lighting and camera**\n12. Route the backup camera wire through the bumper's provided access point. Most quality bumpers have a rubber grommet for this.\n13. Wire the license plate light to the factory connector or a fused tap off the tail light circuit.\n\n- **Carrier arm torque**: Under-torqued carrier hinges cause premature hinge wear and arm fatigue at the welds. Follow the spec \u2014 this isn't a place to guess.\n- **Tire swing clearance**: After installation, open the carrier and verify the tire clears the tailgate and any nearby bodywork across the full swing range. Measure at full extension; some installs are tight.\n- **Backup camera compatibility**: Factory backup cameras on JL mount to the license plate housing or the spare tire carrier. Some aftermarket bumpers require a camera relocation bracket ($30\u2013$60) to maintain factory camera function.\n- **Weight distribution**: A 37\" tire on a carrier adds 65\u201380 lbs in a leveraged position. A quality carrier spreads that load across the full hinge assembly. Budget carriers concentrate stress at the first bolt \u2014 they fatigue and crack.\n\nA $700\u2013$1,000 rear bumper/carrier for 35\" tire use is the realistic budget for quality that won't fail on trail. Spending more buys you better clearance and a carrier rated for larger tires. Under $500, hinge quality becomes questionable for sustained wheeling."},{"id":"jl-skid-plate-guide","title":"JL Wrangler Skid Plates: What to Protect and Which to Buy","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":3.0},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":900},"tools_required":["13mm socket","15mm socket","ratchet and extension","torque wrench","floor jack and jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","armor_skid","skid-plates","trail","protection","underbody"],"summary":"The JL ships with a factory skid plate package on Rubicon trims \u2014 front axle and transfer case coverage. Sahara and Sport trim skids are plastic and cosmetic only. A full aftermarket skid system runs $400\u2013$700 and covers oil pan, transfer case, fuel tank, and gas tank. Prioritize transfer case and fuel tank first.","description":"# JL Wrangler Skid Plates: What to Protect and Which to Buy\n\nThe factory JL Rubicon skid package is one of the better factory setups on any production SUV: it includes steel coverage for the front axle and transfer case. But Sahara and Sport trims ship with plastic belly pans that protect against road debris, not rocks. And even on Rubicons, the fuel tank and rear differential get minimal coverage.\n\n**Oil pan**: The lowest point on the 3.6L engine. A direct rock strike cracks the aluminum pan and puts you on the side of the trail.\n\n**Transfer case**: Centered under the Jeep, protected on Rubicons by the NP241 or NP241OR factory skid. Still vulnerable on Sport/Sahara.\n\n**Fuel tank**: Sits behind the rear axle. No factory armor on any JL trim. A rock punch through the tank is a serious situation \u2014 fire risk and you're stranded.\n\n**Transmission pan**: Less exposed but worth covering on sustained ledgy trails.\n\n**Front differential**: Covered adequately by most front skid packages.\n\n**Priority order if budget is limited:**\n1. Fuel tank skid\n2. Transfer case (if not Rubicon)\n3. Oil pan/transmission combo\n4. Front differential\n\n| Skid / Manufacturer | Coverage | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Rough Country full set | TC + oil pan + trans | $250\u2013$350 | Budget; 3/16\" steel; adequate for mild use |\n| Skid Row Automotive full set | TC + oil pan + trans + fuel | $500\u2013$700 | Mid-tier; 3/16\" DOMEX; good fitment |\n| Teraflex Armor set | Front + TC + trans | $550\u2013$750 | Clean install; proven on Moab-level trails |\n| Genright Atlas set | TC + oil pan + fuel + rear diff | $700\u2013$900 | Premium; thicker plate, radiused edges |\n| Artec Industries | Full underbody | $800\u2013$1,100 | Welded construction; best in category |\n\nAmazon: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B082W5LFN3?tag=trailmanual0b-20 (Rough Country skid plate)\n\n**Prepare the vehicle**\n1. Raise the JL on a hydraulic floor jack and support on jack stands at the frame. Do not work under a vehicle on just a floor jack.\n2. Slide under and identify the existing factory plastic belly pans and any factory steel skids. The plastic pans typically have plastic push-pin fasteners \u2014 a flathead pops the center pin, then the outer body pulls free.\n\n**Remove factory pans**\n3. Remove all plastic push pins from the existing belly pans. Most factory pans come down in sections \u2014 forward engine underbelly, then a middle section over the transmission and TC.\n4. On Rubicons, the factory steel TC skid uses 13mm bolts threaded into weld nuts on the frame. Remove these bolts and lower the skid.\n\n**Install the new skid**\n5. Hold the new skid in position. Most aftermarket skids use the same factory mounting points with the provided hardware.\n6. Thread all bolts in by hand before tightening any \u2014 skids need to shift slightly to align all holes.\n7. Torque to spec (typically 30\u201340 ft-lbs for 13mm frame bolts on most skid manufacturers' specs).\n\n**Fuel tank skid (separate install)**\n8. The fuel tank skid typically replaces the factory plastic heat shield under the tank. Support the tank with a jack during removal of the heat shield retainers.\n9. The new skid bolts to the fuel tank straps or uses frame-mounted brackets \u2014 varies by manufacturer. Follow the included instructions.\n\n- **Ground clearance**: Thick steel skids add 1/2\"\u20131\" to the belly height. Measure your clearance-critical points before ordering a full system.\n- **Edge radiusing**: Cheap skids have sharp edges that catch ledges and can fold. Better skids have rolled or radiused edges that deflect instead.\n- **Rock oil**: On sustained rocky trails, the skids will heat up from repeated contact. This is normal \u2014 heat dissipation is one reason why thin skids eventually deform.\n- **Inspection after runs**: After ledgy trail runs, slide under and check skid welds for cracks. A crack that starts small grows fast under repeated impact.\n\nA complete fuel tank + transfer case + oil pan skid system for the JL runs $400\u2013$700 from mid-tier manufacturers. This covers the three highest-risk areas. Factor in shop time ($100\u2013$150) if you don't have jack stands \u2014 this is a straightforward under-vehicle job once you're safely supported."},{"id":"jl-sliders-guide","title":"JL Wrangler Rock Sliders: Best Options and Install","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":900},"tools_required":["1/2\" drive ratchet","SAE and metric socket set","torque wrench","drill with 1/2\" bit (some installs)","floor jack"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","armor_sliders","sliders","rock-sliders","trail","rocker"],"summary":"Rock sliders bolt or weld to the JL's rocker panel area and absorb side hits on ledge moves. A quality slider bolts to the frame \u2014 not just the rocker \u2014 for enough rigidity to lever off of. Budget $350\u2013$600 for a mid-tier slider that's actually useful on trail.","description":"# JL Wrangler Rock Sliders: Best Options and Install\n\nThe JL's rocker panels are body sheet metal. One solid side hit on a ledge without protection means buckled rockers and a body shop bill. Rock sliders absorb the hit, give you a footing step, and provide a surface to lever off of if the Jeep gets hung up.\n\nThe key distinction: rocker guards vs. rock sliders. Rocker guards are trim pieces \u2014 they protect against brush and cosmetic scrapes. Rock sliders are structural \u2014 they bolt to the frame and can take full vehicle weight. Buy sliders. Rocker guards are for people who never intend to actually trail.\n\n**Frame-mount vs. body-mount**: A frame-mounted slider spreads load across the frame when the Jeep slides onto a ledge. A body-mounted unit twists and transfers load to body seams \u2014 which crack. Always verify the slider mounts to the frame, not just the body.\n\n**Tube diameter**: 1.75\" DOM or 2\" DOM is the standard for real trail use. Thinner wall tube looks similar but deforms on sustained ledge pressure.\n\n**Step functionality**: Most sliders double as a step. Wider, gusseted tube steps work better; narrow round tube is slippery when muddy.\n\n| Slider | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rough Country Rock Sliders | $300\u2013$380 | Budget; frame-mount; adequate for moderate trail |\n| Poison Spyder Rocker Knockers | $450\u2013$600 | Mid-tier; proven design; step surface included |\n| Rugged Ridge Rock Sliders | $380\u2013$480 | Frame-mount; clean install; good value |\n| Metalcloak Sliders | $550\u2013$700 | Excellent build quality; best trail clearance |\n| DV8 Rock Sliders | $420\u2013$550 | Frame-mount; aggressive step design |\n| Genright Trail Sliders | $600\u2013$800 | Premium; welded reinforcement at frame mount |\n\nAmazon: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07TGK5XZC?tag=trailmanual0b-20 (Rugged Ridge Rock Sliders)\n\n**Access the frame mount points**\n1. Raise the JL and support on jack stands. The frame mount points are on the lower frame rail along the rocker area \u2014 below the doors on each side.\n2. Clean the frame mount area with a wire brush. Rust or scale on the mating surface causes loose installs.\n\n**Install the slider**\n3. Most bolt-on sliders have two or three frame plates per side that clamp to the frame rail, plus body bolts that locate the slider against the rocker.\n4. Thread all hardware finger-tight before torquing. The body bolt holes rarely align perfectly \u2014 snugging everything before tightening lets the slider shift into position.\n5. Start with the frame mount bolts: torque to 55\u201365 ft-lbs.\n6. Torque the body-location bolts (typically smaller hardware): 20\u201330 ft-lbs.\n7. Verify the slider doesn't contact the rocker panel unevenly. There should be minimal gap \u2014 the slider should be close to the rocker but not pressing into it under normal conditions.\n\n**Clearance check**\n8. Lower the Jeep and visually verify the slider clears the ground at your normal ride height with appropriate clearance for the wheel travel range.\n9. Bounce the body and watch the slider position relative to the rocker \u2014 verify no contact through the full suspension range.\n\n- **Body interference**: The factory door moldings may need to be trimmed or removed on some slider installs. Check the product's fitment notes before ordering.\n- **Ground clearance**: Sliders add 2\"\u20134\" of hardware below the rocker line. If you're already running a low body height, measure before ordering.\n- **Paint protection at the mount**: The clamping action of frame mount plates can chip paint. Apply a thin bead of silicone sealant between the plate and frame rail to prevent rust in the clamped area.\n- **Re-torque after first trail run**: Sliders settle into their mount position on the first hard use. Re-torque all hardware after the first trail run.\n\n$400\u2013$550 for a quality bolt-on slider with real frame mounting is the realistic target. Spending more gets you thicker tube and better weld quality \u2014 relevant if you're at Moab-level trails. Under $300, frame mount quality is questionable and body-only mounting is common."},{"id":"jl-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper with Swing-Out Tire Carrier","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":849,"high":2200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","trim panel tool","helper for lifting bumper assembly","drill (for some installs)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","hardware installation","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear-bumper","tire-carrier","spare-tire"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (JL)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Poison Spyder JL Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"LOD Destroyer Series Rear Bumper with Carrier (JL)","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Warn Ascent Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (JL)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1599}],"citations":[{"title":"Smittybilt XRC JL Rear Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com/jeep/wrangler-jl/bumpers/xrc-rear-bumper.html"},{"title":"Poison Spyder JL Rear Bumper Overview","url":"https://poisonspyder.com/jeep-wrangler-jl-rear-bumpers.html"},{"title":"Warn Ascent Rear Bumper JL","url":"https://www.warn.com/bumpers-jl-wrangler-ascent-rear-bumper"}],"alternatives":["jl-front-bumper","jl-sliders","jl-tire-fitment-37s"],"summary":"The stock JL rear bumper is plastic, and the factory swing-arm tire carrier was not built to handle a 37\" spare \u2014 a steel rear bumper with a proper tire carrier is one of the most functional upgrades on a trail-going JL.","description":"The JL Wrangler ships with a plastic rear fascia and a factory swing-arm carrier that's rated for the 32\"\u201333\" factory spare. When you move to 35s or 37s, the added weight of a larger, heavier tire pushes the factory swing arm beyond its design intent \u2014 the pivot bearing wears prematurely, the carrier droops, and the rear closure becomes misaligned over time. A steel rear bumper with an integrated, purpose-built carrier solves all of this at once.\n\nThe Smittybilt XRC at $899 is the value entry point. The construction is functional, the carrier pivot is substantially more robust than factory, and it handles up to a 35\" spare with confidence. For 37s, the Smittybilt's carrier is on the lighter-duty end of capable \u2014 it will hold the spare, but the pivot bearing load is higher than with premium options. If you're running 37s long-term, the Poison Spyder, LOD, or Warn units are better investments.\n\nPoison Spyder's rear bumper at $1,199 is the mid-premium choice \u2014 heavier construction than the Smittybilt, a more robust pivot mechanism, and finishes that hold up better over time. LOD's Destroyer at $1,399 is notably thick-plate construction with a functional minimalist design that doesn't sacrifice departure angle significantly. The Warn Ascent at $1,599 is the top-tier option: Warn's engineering is thorough, and the carrier pivot mechanism on the Ascent has the smoothest operation and longest expected service life of the four options.\n\n**Hitch integration:** Most JL rear bumpers include a 2\" receiver hitch as part of the design. Verify hitch integration before purchasing if you run a trailer, bike rack, or recovery equipment off the rear. The factory Class II hitch (if equipped) is removed during bumper install.\n\n**37\" spare weight:** A mounted 37x12.5R17 on a steel wheel weighs approximately 85\u201395 lbs. The tire carrier pivot and the frame attachment points need to handle that load over rough terrain \u2014 lighter-duty carriers are not suited to this use case. Go heavier-duty than you think you need.","steps":["Remove the factory plastic rear fascia (push-pin retainers, clips along the underside, wheel well bolts).","Disconnect the rear license plate light harness and any backup sensor harnesses.","If factory-equipped with a trailer hitch receiver, remove it. The new bumper's hitch replaces it.","Remove the factory swing-arm spare tire carrier \u2014 typically four bolts on the factory hinge assembly.","Remove the factory rear impact absorber/crash bar (bolts to the rear frame rails).","With a helper, lift the new rear bumper assembly into position. These assemblies are heavy \u2014 a 37\" spare carrier with bumper can exceed 100 lbs unloaded.","Start all mounting bolts finger-tight, then confirm the bumper is level and centered before torquing.","Torque mounting hardware to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 60\u201380 ft-lb on main frame bolts.","Reinstall license plate light using the new bracket (usually included). Reconnect harnesses.","Relocate rear backup sensors if applicable and bumper provides sensor ports.","Load the spare tire onto the new carrier, confirm pivot operation swings freely and latch engages cleanly.","Check departure angle clearance \u2014 lower the vehicle fully, then drive over a rolled curb or use a ramp to verify the new bumper profile doesn't contact prematurely."],"pros":["Dramatically more robust tire carrier than factory \u2014 purpose-built for 35\"\u201337\" spare weight","Steel construction protects the rear frame area during recovery and trail impacts","Integrated 2\" hitch receiver on most designs adds functional utility","Departure angle on most JL rear bumpers is improved over factory or equal \u2014 the low rear fascia of the JL is typically the departure bottleneck, not the bumper","Full-replacement install removes all factory plastic from the rear \u2014 cleaner visual result"],"cons":["Total assembly weight (bumper + spare on carrier) adds significant mass to the rear \u2014 up to 100\u2013120 lbs beyond factory. This affects rear spring rate and body pitch","Installation requires two people \u2014 these assemblies are too heavy and unwieldy for solo mounting","Factory backup sensors require relocation or sacrifice \u2014 verify what your chosen bumper does with sensor provision","Premium options (Warn, LOD, Poison Spyder) represent significant investment \u2014 budget realistically against front bumper and other armor priorities","Smittybilt XRC is functional for 35s but not the ideal choice for long-term 37\" spare loads \u2014 carrier pivot will see accelerated wear"]},{"id":"jl-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit \u2014 Engine, Transfer Case, and Fuel Tank","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pry bar (for stubborn existing skid alignment)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","hardware installation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","skids","underbody-protection","skid-plates"],"parts":[{"name":"Teraflex JL Wrangler Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 JL Wrangler Full Belly System","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rubicon Express JL Skid Plate Package","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"GenRight Offroad JL Full Underbelly Skid Kit","vendor":"GenRight Offroad","price_estimate_usd":899}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex JL Skid Plate Kit Product Page","url":"https://teraflex.com/products/jl-skid-plate-kit"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JL Skid Plates","url":"https://rockhard4x4.com/jeep/jl-wrangler/skid-plates/"},{"title":"GenRight JL Full Underbelly Skid System","url":"https://www.genright.com/jl-wrangler-skid-plates.html"}],"alternatives":["jl-sliders","jl-front-bumper"],"summary":"The JL's underbody layout is different enough from the JK's that JK skids will not fit \u2014 this is not a fitment approximation, it's a flat incompatibility. Every skid plate on a JL must be JL-spec.","description":"Jeep updated the JL's frame geometry, crossmember locations, and underbody component placement significantly compared to the JK. The oil pan position, transfer case mounting, and fuel tank skid integration points all moved. If you have JK skids in your garage, they are not transferable. This is worth knowing before buying used parts.\n\nThe JL Rubicon ships with a factory aluminum skid package that provides basic coverage \u2014 better than nothing, but thin and prone to deformation on genuine rock contact. The Sport and Sahara have no factory skids worth mentioning. Aftermarket steel skids address both the coverage gaps and the material upgrade.\n\nThe Teraflex JL skid kit at $499 covers the primary three: the transfer case belly area, the engine/oil pan, and the front crossmember. It's 3/16\" steel plate, laser-cut for JL-specific fitment, and the mount points align with factory hardware. This is the most popular mid-range option because the price is fair and the coverage addresses the components most commonly damaged on trail.\n\nRock Hard 4x4's JL system at $699 adds coverage to the areas the Teraflex kit leaves exposed \u2014 specifically the rear areas near the fuel tank and the front diff on equipped trims. The extra coverage is worth the $200 premium if your trails involve sustained belly exposure rather than occasional hits. GenRight's full underbelly kit at $899 is the comprehensive choice for rock-crawl builds \u2014 every vulnerable surface has plate coverage, and the kit is designed to work as a unified deflection surface rather than individual plates with gaps.\n\nRubicon Express at $449 is the value option \u2014 slightly thinner plate than Rock Hard, adequate for mild to moderate trail use, and competitively priced. For a JL that's primarily an overland rig with occasional trail excursions, the Rubicon Express kit is a defensible choice.","steps":["Lift the vehicle on jack stands at the frame rails \u2014 you need clear underbody access to work comfortably.","If factory plastic undertray or factory aluminum skids are present, remove them. Factory aluminum Rubicon skids unbolt cleanly; factory plastic undertrays use a combination of bolts and push pins.","Inspect the underbody for existing damage before installing new skids \u2014 address any compromised brackets or crossmember sections before covering them.","Dry-fit all skid plates before starting installation. Confirm mounting hole alignment with factory threaded inserts or frame holes.","Start all hardware finger-tight before torquing any individual plate. Multiple plates often share mounting points, and a plate torqued early will bind adjacent plates.","Torque all hardware to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 30\u201345 ft-lb depending on bolt size and material.","Confirm clearance around exhaust components if your kit covers the area near the cat or mid-pipe. Aftermarket skids occasionally require minor exhaust hanger relocation."],"pros":["Protects the JL's most vulnerable underbody components \u2014 oil pan, transfer case, and fuel tank contacts are what end trail days","Bolt-on installation; no cutting or welding required for any of the kits listed","Teraflex and Rock Hard kits provide better coverage than the factory Rubicon aluminum skids at similar or lower cost","Steel plate construction deflects and slides over rock contact rather than deforming and catching","GenRight full kit eliminates all coverage gaps \u2014 appropriate for builds that prioritize underbody protection"],"cons":["JK skids are completely incompatible \u2014 do not attempt JK fitment on a JL regardless of appearance similarity","Steel skid weight adds to unsprung/belly weight \u2014 full kits add 40\u201380 lbs total","Factory Rubicon aluminum skids are not compatible with most aftermarket kits as supplements \u2014 plan for full replacement","GenRight's $899 kit is a significant investment; prioritize by terrain type before committing to full coverage","Skid plates that have been heavily impacted should be inspected for deformation that could contact exhaust or drivetrain components"]},{"id":"jl-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders and Rocker Guards","vehicle":"jl","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":950},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","drill bits","ratchet straps (for alignment during install)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","hardware installation","body panel awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rockers","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder JL Rocker Knockers Rock Sliders","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 Patriot Series Rock Sliders (JL)","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"LOD Offroad Destroyer Series Rock Sliders (JL)","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Rock Sliders (JL)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":499}],"citations":[{"title":"Poison Spyder JL Rock Sliders Product Page","url":"https://poisonspyder.com/jeep-wrangler-jl-rock-sliders.html"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JL Wrangler Sliders","url":"https://rockhard4x4.com/jeep/jl-wrangler/rock-sliders/"},{"title":"LOD Offroad Destroyer JL Sliders","url":"https://www.lodoffroad.com/products/jeep-jl-wrangler-rock-sliders"}],"alternatives":["jl-skid-plates","jl-front-bumper"],"summary":"The rocker panels on the JL Wrangler are exposed \u2014 no factory protection \u2014 and a single high-centering on a rock shelf can fold sheet metal that costs $600+ to repair. A slider is the single cheapest per-dollar piece of trail insurance available for this platform.","description":"The JL's unibody construction changes how sliders attach compared to the body-on-frame XJ. Sliders for the JL bolt through the body and into the integrated frame rails in the sill area \u2014 a robust mounting approach when done correctly, but one that requires precise fit and correct torque specs. Do not attempt to run a JK slider on a JL; the mounting points are different and a slider that's not properly attached to the structure will transfer impact forces to the wrong part of the body.\n\nPoison Spyder's Rocker Knockers are the premium option \u2014 heavy-gauge DOM tube construction with a textured powdercoat finish and a step tube design that doubles as a rock step. The $699 price reflects the material quality, but these are genuinely trail-proven hardware. If your build sees regular technical terrain, the Poison Spyder units are worth the premium.\n\nRock Hard 4x4 is the value-focused choice that doesn't compromise on structural integrity. At $449, the Patriot Series sliders are 3/16\" plate with 1.75\" DOM tube construction \u2014 heavy enough to protect the rockers without the price of premium brands. These are a popular choice for trail builds on a budget. LOD's Destroyer series sits in the middle \u2014 $649 for notably thick plate construction and a relatively minimal profile that doesn't add much departure-angle penalty. The Smittybilt XRC sliders at $499 are the budget-ceiling option: adequate construction, lighter gauge than the Rock Hard or LOD, but a functional choice for mild-to-moderate trail use.\n\n**JL vs JLU fitment:** The 2-door JL and 4-door JLU have different wheelbase and rocker lengths. Every manufacturer makes separate part numbers for JL and JLU \u2014 verify before ordering. Running a JL-spec slider on a JLU will leave rear coverage gaps; running a JLU slider on a JL won't fit at all.","steps":["Chock wheels, lift vehicle on jack stands at the frame rails with enough clearance to work under the rocker area.","Clean the mounting surfaces \u2014 factory undercoating and debris on the bolt hole areas will affect seat quality.","Dry-fit both sliders before drilling any pilot holes or running any hardware. The fit should align with the body before torquing.","For bolt-through installations: drill pilot holes per the manufacturer's template if provided. Use sharp bits \u2014 the factory sill material is hard.","Install mounting hardware from the inside of the vehicle where required. Some slider designs require a helper inside to hold inner-body hardware while you torque from below.","Start all hardware finger-tight before torquing anything.","Use ratchet straps or a helper to hold the slider in alignment during initial torquing \u2014 they're heavy and will shift if unsupported.","Torque all fasteners to manufacturer spec. For most JL sliders, this is 35\u201345 ft-lb on the main mounting points.","Articulate the doors to confirm clearance \u2014 sliders close to the door bottoms need a clearance check before driving."],"pros":["Protects the most vulnerable body panel on a trail-going JL \u2014 rocker damage is expensive and common","Most slider designs double as a step tube, which is a genuine quality-of-life upgrade on a lifted JL","Rock Hard and Smittybilt options deliver real protection at accessible price points","Bolt-on installation (no welding required on any of these options) means a home builder can complete the job","Adds structural rigidity to the sill area \u2014 measurable improvement on flex-intensive trail use"],"cons":["JL and JLU require separate part numbers \u2014 wrong fitment will not work","Heavy hardware (most sliders add 40\u201370 lbs) raises the CG slightly and increases unsprung weight","Slider profile affects departure angle \u2014 wider, lower-profile sliders sacrifice some departure clearance","Powder coat finish scratches quickly on rocky terrain \u2014 expect trail wear; that's what they're for","Smittybilt XRC gauge is lighter than Rock Hard or Poison Spyder; adequate for mild-moderate use but not the choice for serious rock work"]},{"id":"jl-dana44-jl-front","title":"Dana 44 AdvanTEK Front \u2014 Service and Upgrade","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","fluid pump","torque wrench","RTV","drain pan"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","diff cover service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["axles","drivetrain","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 75W-140 Synthetic Gear Oil (qt)","part_number":"68218655AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Mopar Front Diff Cover Gasket / RTV Kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Spicer Chromoly Front Shafts (FAD-delete, M210)","part_number":"10044469","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Next Venture Motorsports M210 Front Axle Truss","vendor":"Next Venture Motorsports","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Artec Industries M210 Differential Cover","vendor":"Artec","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Spicer JL M210/Dana 44 AdvanTEK Upgrades","url":"https://danaproparts.com/p-35861-jeep-wrangler-jl-dana-spicer-parts-upgrades.html"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Maintenance Schedule","url":"https://getgaragehub.com/guides/jeep-wrangler-jl-maintenance-schedule/"},{"title":"JL Front Axle Truss \u2014 AdvanTEK / M210","url":"https://nextventuremotorsports.com/products/jl-jt-front-axle-truss-advantek-dana-44-m210"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":["jl-differential-fluid-jl","jl-dana44-jl-rear","jl-rubicon-jl-front"],"summary":"Service the JL front Dana 44 AdvanTEK every 30,000 miles with 2 quarts of 75W-140 \u2014 and know that the FAD housing and stock axle shafts are the two weak points that snap first when you go bigger than 35s.","description":"The JL front axle is a Dana 44 AdvanTEK on Rubicon trims (model M210 wide-track) and an M210 Dana 44-derived front on Sahara/Sport with smaller ring gear. Both share the same housing platform and the same Front Axle Disconnect (FAD) on non-Rubicon trims. The Rubicon's electronic locker lives in this housing. Service fluid is Mopar 75W-140 synthetic GL-5, capacity about 2.0 quarts at the bottom of the fill hole. Change interval is every 30,000 miles under normal driving and every 15,000 miles for vehicles that see water crossings, sand, or regular trail use.\n\nThe FAD casting on Sport and Sahara trims is the part to watch. The cast aluminum FAD housing is brittle and is the most common failure point on stock JL fronts, and the plastic FAD coupler bushing inside it wears out and dumps gear oil. If your build is heading past 35-inch tires, the honest move is a FAD-delete with Spicer chromoly inner shafts (PN 10044469 set) \u2014 this swaps the plastic coupler for a solid one-piece shaft and gets rid of the disconnect mechanism entirely. Rubicons skip this issue because they came with the locking version and no FAD.\n\nStock M210 axle shafts are adequate up to 35-inch tires for trail use. Past 35s, especially with lockers and heavy throttle, expect to bend or twist stock shafts within a season or two of hard wheeling. Chromoly upgrades push the working limit to 37-inch tires with the rest of the drivetrain unchanged. An axle truss (Next Venture Motorsports or Artec) adds housing rigidity for builds running 37s+ with lockers \u2014 necessary on rigs that see rock crawling, optional for trail rigs.\n\nBall joints on M210s are a known wear item. Listen for clunks at full lock and inspect for grease weeping at every fluid change. Replace as a set with Synergy or Dynatrac ball joints; the stock joints are unsealed in the lower position and gravel finds its way in.\n\nAftermarket diff covers (Artec, ARB, Rusty's, AEV) add about 4 ounces of capacity and a magnetic drain plug. They protect the housing on rocks and make fluid changes cleaner because of the drain plug \u2014 the stock cover requires you to pull the whole cover to drain.","steps":["Park on level ground, warm the axle by driving 5\u201310 miles so the old fluid drains cleanly.","Position drain pan under the diff cover. Crack the fill plug (top of cover, 3/8\" square drive) FIRST to confirm it backs out \u2014 if it's stuck and you've already drained, you can't refill.","If you have an aftermarket cover with a drain plug, pull the plug and drain. Otherwise, loosen the cover bolts in a crisscross pattern, leave the top two snug, and pry the bottom of the cover loose to drain.","Remove the cover, wipe the housing clean, inspect the ring gear teeth for pitting or chipping, and check the magnet for metal chunks (fine paste is normal; chunks are not).","Scrape old RTV off both mating surfaces with a plastic scraper. Wipe with brake cleaner.","Apply 1/4\" bead of Mopar gray RTV or equivalent silicone around the cover, going inside the bolt holes. Install cover and torque bolts to 25 ft-lbs in a crisscross pattern.","Wait 30 minutes for RTV to set, then fill through the fill hole until fluid is at the bottom of the threads (about 2 quarts). Reinstall fill plug to 30 ft-lbs.","Drive 20\u201350 miles and recheck for leaks at the cover and fill plug."],"pros":["Fluid change is a one-hour job with a $40 parts bill","Service catches FAD wear, ball joint slop, and locker-fluid contamination","Chromoly shaft upgrade is a bolt-in once the axle is open"],"cons":["FAD housing is a known weak point on non-Rubicon trims","Stock shafts bend on 37s+ \u2014 plan a chromoly budget if you're going big","Mopar 75W-140 is not cheap and the spec is firm \u2014 generic 80W-90 will damage the electric locker on Rubicons"]},{"id":"jl-dana44-jl-rear","title":"Dana 44 AdvanTEK Rear \u2014 Locker Options","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"part","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1700},"tools_required":["dial indicator","bearing press","torque wrench","case spreader (optional)","shop manual"],"skills_required":["gear setup","pinion preload","backlash measurement"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["advantek","m220","m210","locker","rubicon"],"parts":[{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 44 M220 35-spline (Non-Rubicon JL/JT)","part_number":"14234-1","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 44 M220 35-spline (alt p/n)","part_number":"14313-2","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1150},{"name":"Eaton Performance ELocker \u2014 30-spline D44, 3.73 and lower","part_number":"19977-010","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"ARB Air Locker RD-245 \u2014 Dana 44 30-spline w/factory CV","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1091},{"name":"ARB compact onboard compressor (CKMA12)","part_number":"CKMA12","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"Eaton 14234-1 Dana 44 Electric Locker \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/eaton/dana-44-electric-locker-wrangler-jl-gladiator-jt-14234-1"},{"title":"Eaton 14313-2 35 Spline D44/M220 E-Locker \u2014 OffRoad Elements","url":"https://www.offroadelements.com/eaton-e-locker-35-spline-d44-m220-wrangler-jl-gladiator-jt-2018/"},{"title":"Eaton 19977-010 4-Pinion ELocker 30-spline D44 \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/eaton/performance-4-pinion-e-locker-30-spline-dana-44-19977-010"}],"alternatives":["jl-dana44-jl-front","jl-rubicon-jl-front"],"summary":"Non-Rubicon JLs have an open Dana 44 M220 rear axle that benefits enormously from a locker; Eaton's 14234-1 ELocker is the direct-fit electric answer ($1,100), ARB's RD-245 is the air-actuated alternative if you already run a compressor.","description":"The JL rear axle is a Dana 44 in name only \u2014 the housing is the M220 variant, beefier than the front M210 but with its own carrier specs and spline counts. Rubicons come with the OE Tru-Lok electric locker from the factory. Sport, Sport S, Sahara, and Willys come with an open differential (Sahara is sometimes Trac-Lok limited-slip) and a 30- or 32-spline carrier depending on year and gear ratio. Knowing exactly what you have determines which locker fits.\n\nEaton's ELocker is the most common upgrade because it's electric, one-wire activation, and doesn't require an air compressor. The 14234-1 fits 35-spline M220 carriers on 2018\u20132023 JL/JT non-Rubicons. The newer 14313-2 covers later model years through 2026. The 19977-010 is the performance 4-pinion option for older 30-spline D44s with 3.73 or numerically lower gears \u2014 relevant only if you're running an older D44 conversion or stock gears. Get the spline count from your build sheet or, more reliably, by pulling the diff cover and counting splines on the side gears.\n\nARB Air Lockers are the air-actuated alternative. The RD-245 fits 30-spline factory-CV Dana 44s and costs $1,091 plus another $340 for the compressor (CKMA12) if you don't have one. Two locker reasons to choose ARB: you want full-open behavior on the street (ELockers are also open until engaged, so this is a wash), and you want the option to add a front ARB later sharing the same compressor. If you're already running OBA for tires, the ARB compressor is a no-cost adder.\n\nInstall is not a beginner job. You're pulling the carrier, swapping in the locker, setting pinion preload, ring gear backlash (0.005\u20130.008\"), and pinion bearing crush (~15 in-lb rolling torque used). If you're not regearing at the same time, you can re-use the existing ring gear if it's not worn \u2014 but most shops will tell you to regear during the locker install since the carrier is out anyway. If this is your first gear setup, pay a driveline shop. Bad setup eats a $1,100 locker.","steps":["Confirm axle: M220 vs older D44, spline count (30/32/35), and current gear ratio (stamped on ring gear or check VIN build sheet).","Drain rear diff fluid. Pull the cover.","Mark and remove the carrier bearing caps. Note shim locations and orientation.","Pry the carrier out. Measure ring-and-pinion backlash before removing anything (note for re-setup).","Press old carrier bearings, install on new locker carrier with same shims to start.","Drop new locker into housing, install bearing caps, torque to spec (55-70 ft-lbs typical).","Re-check backlash (0.005\u20130.008\") and pattern. Adjust shims as needed.","Run actuator wire (ELocker) along axle tube to a frame-mounted switch, or run ARB air line.","Fill diff with 75W-85 or 75W-90 gear oil \u2014 Rubicon trac-lok pumpkins also need friction modifier; locker pumpkins do not.","Bench-test actuator before reinstalling driveshaft. Verify locker engages and disengages cleanly."],"pros":["Eaton ELocker is fully open until engaged \u2014 no street manners trade-off","One-wire install, no air system needed for ELocker","ARB shares compressor with front locker if planning both","Massive traction gain on a non-Rubicon JL"],"cons":["$1,100+ for the locker alone, more if regearing","Setup is unforgiving \u2014 backlash off by 0.002\" eats a ring gear","Need to know your spline count before ordering \u2014 wrong locker = restock fee","ARB requires a compressor, more plumbing"]},{"id":"rubicon-jl-front","title":"JL Rubicon Dana 44 \u2014 What Transfers Across","vehicle":"jl","category":"axles","subcategory":"front_axle","type":"swap","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","spring compressor","pickle fork"],"skills_required":["axle swap experience","brake bleeding","ABS reset"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["dana-44","m210","front-axle","rubicon-swap","locker"],"parts":[{"name":"JL Rubicon M210 front axle assembly (used)","vendor":"Quadratec or salvage","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Rubicon front driveshaft (used)","vendor":"salvage","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"AEV ProCal Snap programmer","part_number":"30401050AB","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Front track bar (heavy duty, if re-using stock arms)","vendor":"Synergy","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Identifying Wranglers and Their Different Axles \u2014 Revolution Gear & Axle","url":"https://revolutiongear.com/blog/identifying-wranglers-and-their-different-axles/"},{"title":"Which Jeep Wrangler Axles Do I Have? \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/blog/which-jeep-wrangler-axles-do-i-have"}],"alternatives":["dana-44-swap-aftermarket","locker-only-upgrade"],"summary":"A JL Rubicon front Dana 44 (M210) bolts into a Sport or Sahara JL with the right gear ratio match, but it's 1.5\" wider per side, comes with a 4.10 final drive, and requires a recalibration before it'll behave on the road.","description":"The JL Rubicon front axle is technically a Dana M210HD \u2014 Dana's modern designation for the wide-knuckle, electric-locker variant Jeep ships under Rubicon trims. Non-Rubicon JLs get the narrower M186 (often called Dana 30 internally). The two share suspension mounting points and brake hardware, which is what makes the swap appealing \u2014 bolt-pattern compatibility means you're not fabricating brackets.\n\nWhat does NOT transfer cleanly is the track width and gearing. The Rubicon axle sits 1.5\" wider per wheel flange, which pushes the front tires outboard and changes scrub radius. Stock fender flares cover it, but expect rub on full lock. More importantly, Rubicons ship with 4.10 gears; Sports and Saharas typically run 3.45 or 3.73. If the rear axle doesn't get re-geared to match, the transfer case binds and the driveshafts wind up against each other on dry pavement. Either re-gear the rear or plan on swapping both axles as a pair.\n\nThe electric locker is the real prize. It engages through the Rubicon-only switch panel, which non-Rubicon JLs don't have. Wiring the locker requires either a salvaged Rubicon dash harness or a standalone controller (sPOD, Switch Pros). AEV's ProCal Snap will tell the BCM about the new axle, gear ratio, and locker so the dashboard and ABS behave normally \u2014 without it, expect persistent dash warnings and incorrect speedometer readings.\n\nDriveshaft length is the other gotcha. Rubicon front driveshafts are slightly shorter to match the wider axle pinion offset. Trying to re-use a Sport driveshaft will cause vibration and premature U-joint wear. Source the matching Rubicon driveshaft from the same donor.\n\nCross-generation note: JL Rubicon axles are NOT interchangeable with JK Rubicon axles. The JL uses a redesigned knuckle, different ball joints, and an updated electronic locker controller. Don't try to mix generations.","steps":["Source a complete JL Rubicon front axle assembly with knuckles, hubs, calipers, ABS sensors, and pinion yoke intact.","Confirm gear ratio on the donor matches your rear axle (or plan to re-gear the rear).","Support the JL on jack stands, remove front tires, disconnect ABS, brake lines, sway bar links, and track bar.","Unbolt control arms at the axle and lower the stock axle out.","Position the Rubicon axle, reattach control arms, track bar, and sway bar links.","Reconnect brake lines (use new banjo washers), bleed the system.","Reconnect ABS sensors and the electric locker harness \u2014 splice into a Rubicon donor harness or wire to an aftermarket controller.","Use AEV ProCal Snap to write the new axle code and gear ratio to the BCM.","Test on a flat lot in 2WD before hitting trails. Verify ABS, locker engagement, and steering centering."],"pros":["Factory locker with proper integration","Wider stance improves stability","Stronger axle shafts and ring gear than M186","Mostly bolt-in if donor is complete"],"cons":["Locker wiring is the hard part \u2014 plan it before you start","Wider track means new wheels or spacers off the table without rub","Must match rear axle gearing or re-gear both","AEV ProCal required for proper BCM integration"]},{"id":"jl-door-removal","title":"JL Wrangler Door Removal: Full and Half Doors","vehicle":"jl","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":30},"tools_required":["T40 Torx socket","T50 Torx socket","ratchet and extension","door hinge strap or second person"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["doors","body","door-removal","open-air","trail-doors"],"summary":"JL doors come off without tools for the mirror wiring disconnect \u2014 you just need a T40 for the strap bolts. Budget 15\u201320 minutes per door once you've done it twice.","description":"# JL Wrangler Door Removal: Full and Half Doors\n\nDoorless driving is one of the defining JL experiences. Jeep designed the doors to come off repeatedly \u2014 the hinges are bolt-free, the wiring unplugs cleanly, and the strap and limiting strap take two T40 bolts each. The catch is that full doors weigh 50\u201370 lbs each. Have somewhere to put them before you start unbolting.\n\n- T40 Torx socket and ratchet\n- T50 Torx socket (for some strap bolts on earlier JLs)\n- Door storage bags or a horizontal rack (highly recommended \u2014 powder coating scratches easily)\n- A second person OR a door hinge support strap to catch the door weight\n- Painter's tape to cover the hinge area on the body\n\n**Prepare the door**\n1. Open the door to roughly 90 degrees \u2014 not fully open, not barely cracked. This gives you access to the strap hardware.\n2. Tape around the body-side hinge area. When you lift the door, the hinge knuckle swings out and can drag across the paint.\n\n**Disconnect the wiring harness**\n3. The wiring harness runs through the door jamb. There's a connector located on the forward door hinge pillar \u2014 press the tab and pull straight out. On JLs with power windows and heated mirrors, there may be a second connector nearby.\n4. Release the rubber grommet holding the wiring into the door sill. Tuck the harness against the body.\n\n**Remove the door check strap**\n5. The check strap (the black strap that limits how far the door opens) bolts to the A-pillar. Two T40 bolts. Remove both, pull the strap retainer pin out of the door-side anchor.\n\n**Lift the door off**\n6. With your partner holding the door from the outside, lift the door straight up. The door hinges are pin-and-knuckle \u2014 the door simply lifts off the hinge pins when you raise it high enough.\n7. Lower the door onto a soft surface or directly into a door storage bag. Full doors should be stored upright when possible to avoid warping the seals.\n\n**Repeat for remaining doors**\n8. The rear doors follow the same procedure. Note that rear doors have no window motor on base trims, so the harness may have only one connector.\n\n**After removal**\n9. Install the factory door surround mirror \u2014 it threads into the existing mirror hole in the A-pillar. JLs ship with these stored in the cab.\n10. Fold back any loose harness ends and tuck them inside the cab so they don't catch trail debris.\n\n- **Paint damage at the hinge**: The hinge knuckle is cast metal with sharp edges. Tape the body beforehand \u2014 one careless lift leaves a gouge.\n- **Wiring tension**: Don't lift the door until the harness is fully disconnected. The wiring will handle a little tension but the connectors will break before the harness stretches.\n- **Door storage**: Full JL doors have a powered window channel. Store them with the glass up, not down \u2014 pressure on the glass can stress the regulator mounts.\n- **Reinstallation alignment**: The hinge pins have a slight taper. The door drops in cleanly when you have it perfectly vertical. If it feels like it's binding, lift fully clear and try again \u2014 don't force it.\n\n- Factory door storage bags: $60\u2013$120 (Bestop, Rugged Ridge, Smittybilt)\n- Door hinge strap (lifts the door solo): $30\u2013$60 (Rugged Ridge makes a good one)\n- Aftermarket trail doors (half doors): $500\u2013$1,200 (Bestop, Barricade, Mopar)\n- Replacement door check strap: $25\u2013$45 OEM if yours is torn\n\nTrail doors are worth considering if you pull doors more than a few times a season \u2014 the weight drop from 65 lbs to roughly 18 lbs per door is significant, and storage becomes trivial."},{"id":"jl-soft-top-removal","title":"Removing and Storing the JL Soft Top","vehicle":"jl","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":50},"tools_required":["T50 Torx socket","trim panel tool","two-person crew for full removal"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["soft-top","body","top-removal","open-air"],"summary":"Removing the JL soft top takes 30\u201360 minutes with two people and no special tools beyond a T50 Torx. The factory top stores in a bag \u2014 treat the plastic windows carefully and you'll reuse them for years.","description":"# Removing and Storing the JL Soft Top\n\nThe JL factory soft top comes off in sections: the rear quarter panels and tailgate bar first, then the full header bow and front sections. Two people makes it manageable; one person makes it frustrating and risks creasing the windows.\n\n- T50 Torx socket and ratchet\n- Trim panel tool or flathead wrapped in tape (for the plastic retaining clips)\n- Two people for full top removal\n- Factory top bag (came with the Jeep) or an aftermarket storage bag\n\n**Remove rear quarter windows first**\n1. Unlock the zipper channels running along the D-pillars (inside the cargo area). Unzip fully.\n2. Unsnap the plastic retaining studs along the bottom edge of each quarter window panel \u2014 press the center pin, then pull the retainer.\n3. Lift the quarter panel up and out. Fold carefully \u2014 the plastic windows crack at temperature extremes, especially below 40\u00b0F.\n\n**Remove the tailgate header bar**\n4. Open the tailgate. The rear header bar runs across the top of the tailgate opening. Remove the two T50 Torx bolts (one each side) securing it to the tub.\n5. Lift the bar up and off. Set it aside \u2014 it does not go in the top bag.\n\n**Remove the top bow assembly**\n6. Standing inside the Jeep, locate the four latches securing the header bow to the windshield frame. Flip each latch lever up and forward to release.\n7. With your second person at the rear, lift the header bow up and toward the rear of the Jeep. The soft top folds back over itself \u2014 do not fight it if it binds; check that all zipper channels are fully open.\n8. The folded top can stay folded over the rear seats (partial topless) or be fully removed and bagged.\n\n**Full removal**\n9. The top attaches at the rear tub rail with a series of hook-and-loop strips and plastic channel retainers. Work from the rear corners forward, releasing each attachment point.\n10. Fold the top as the factory bag specifies \u2014 it matters for keeping the windows crease-free. Store in a cool, dry location out of direct sun.\n\n- **Cold weather**: Soft top plastic yellows and cracks if forced when cold. Warm the top with a heat gun on low or leave the Jeep in the sun for 20 minutes before removal below 50\u00b0F.\n- **Zipper lubrication**: Dry zippers tear. Apply a zipper lubricant (not WD-40) to all zipper channels twice a year.\n- **Window storage**: Never fold the rear window into itself under pressure. Lay it flat or roll it loosely.\n- **The retaining clips**: JL clips are friction-fit. They will feel like they're breaking when they're not \u2014 use a trim tool, not a screwdriver, to avoid cracking the plastic.\n\n- Factory replacement soft top (if yours is worn): $400\u2013$900 from the Jeep dealer or Bestop\n- Aftermarket replacement: Bestop SuperTop NX (~$500), Smittybilt Standard Top (~$350)\n- Zipper lubricant: $8\u2013$12 (Bestop Zip Care or 303 Fabric Guard)\n- Storage bag (if factory bag is lost): $40\u2013$70 aftermarket\n\nThe factory top holds up well if stored properly. Aftermarket windows (Bestop, Mopar tinted) run $200\u2013$400 and are worth the upgrade if your factory windows have yellowed beyond cleaning."},{"id":"jl-brake-pads-jl","title":"Front and Rear Brake Pad Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"brakes","subcategory":"pads_rotors","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":280},"tools_required":["19mm socket for lug nuts","13mm and 15mm sockets for caliper bracket","C-clamp or piston compressor","brake cleaner","torque wrench","anti-seize for sliding pins","high-temp brake grease"],"skills_required":["brake-system competence","torque awareness"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-present"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Akebono ACT2018 ceramic front pads","part_number":"ACT2018","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Akebono ACT2019 ceramic rear pads","part_number":"ACT2019","vendor":"Akebono","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Mopar OE front pads (Rubicon spec)","part_number":"68249847AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Centric Premium front rotors (pair)","part_number":"120.67098","vendor":"Centric","price_estimate_usd":110}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar JL Wrangler brake torque specs \u2014 caliper bracket 110 ft-lb, slide pins 26 ft-lb","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums: brake pad and rotor part numbers thread","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Front pads and rotors take 90 minutes; rears another 60. Caliper bracket bolts are 110 ft-lb, slide pins are 26 ft-lb, lugs are 130 ft-lb. Don't reuse worn slide pin boots \u2014 torn boots cause uneven pad wear within 5,000 miles.","description":"JL Wrangler brakes are larger than JK across the lineup \u2014 the front rotor went from 12.9\" on JK Sport to 13.0\" Sport/13.4\" Rubicon on JL. The system is otherwise conventional floating caliper with single-piston front and rear. Pad and rotor service is straightforward once you understand the bracket vs slide pin distinction.\n\n**Pad choice.** OE Mopar pads are quiet but dust-heavy. Akebono ACT-series ceramic pads stay clean and bite well below 1,000\u00b0F \u2014 the right choice for daily-drivers and mild trail use. Hawk LTS gives more cold bite for tow rigs. Avoid no-name pads from auction sites; they'll glaze and squeal within 6 months.\n\n**Rotor decision.** Rotors are sacrificial. If yours have <0.030\" of useful thickness left (verify with a micrometer against the minimum stamped on the rotor hat), replace them. Centric Premium and Bosch QuietCast are both quiet and last 50K+ miles. Slotted/drilled rotors look aggressive but offer no benefit for the JL's weight class and crack faster.\n\n**Slide pins matter more than pads.** A torn slide pin boot lets sand and water into the pin bore. The caliper then sticks on one side, the inboard pad wears faster than outboard, and you replace pads at half life. Inspect both pins every brake service. Re-grease with high-temp synthetic brake grease \u2014 never wheel bearing grease (it melts).","steps":["Loosen lug nuts before lifting. Lift one wheel, support on stand. Remove wheel.","Inspect the caliper for leaks at the dust boot and at the hose fitting. A wet caliper goes in the rebuild pile, not the reinstall pile.","Open the master cylinder cap to let air escape as the pistons retract. (Don't let the reservoir overflow.)","Pull the two 13mm slide pin bolts on the caliper. Lift caliper off the bracket. Hang it from the spring with a wire \u2014 never let it hang from the brake hose.","Slide the old pads out of the bracket. Note the wear pattern \u2014 uneven wear = stuck slide pin.","Pull the slide pins out of the bracket. Wipe clean. Check the boots \u2014 replace if torn. Re-grease with high-temp brake grease and reseat.","(If replacing rotors) Pull the two 15mm bracket bolts and lift bracket off. Remove the rotor (may need a few hammer taps if rust-stuck). Wipe new rotor clean with brake cleaner \u2014 they ship with rust preventative oil. Reinstall bracket, 110 ft-lb on bolts.","Insert new pads into the bracket. Apply a thin smear of brake grease on the pad backing where it contacts the caliper piston and the slide ears.","Compress the caliper piston with a C-clamp using an old pad as the contact surface. Push slowly so the master doesn't overflow.","Reinstall caliper over new pads. Torque the 13mm slide pin bolts to 26 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheel. Torque lugs to 130 ft-lb in a star pattern.","**Critical:** Pump the brake pedal 5\u201310 times before driving to seat the pads against the rotor. The first pedal will go to the floor if you skip this. Verify firm pedal before moving the vehicle.","Re-torque lugs after 50\u2013100 miles."],"pros":["Standard floating caliper design","Common Akebono / Centric parts at any parts store","90 minutes per axle once you've done it","No special tools beyond a C-clamp"],"cons":["Easy to forget the pedal-pump step (and rear-end someone)","Slide pin neglect = premature pad wear","Larger Rubicon rotors not interchangeable with Sport","Lug torque is high (130 ft-lb) \u2014 needs a real torque wrench, not a breaker bar guess"]},{"id":"jl-brake-rotor","title":"Brake Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":140,"high":380},"tools_required":["13mm socket","18mm socket","21mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands","micrometer"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal","rotor measurement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["brakes","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front rotor \u2014 standard brakes (0.95\" thick)","part_number":"68273502AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Mopar OEM front rotor \u2014 heavy duty brakes (1.1\" thick)","part_number":"68250085AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Mopar OEM rear rotor (328mm solid)","part_number":"68564973AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Powerstop Z36 rotor + pad kit (front and rear)","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Brake Rotor Sizes and Codes Reference","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/brake-rotor-sizes-codes.57269/"},{"title":"Mopar 68273502AB Brake Rotor Specifications","url":"https://store.mopar.com/oem-parts/mopar-brake-rotor-68273502ab"}],"alternatives":["jl-brake-pads"],"summary":"JL Wranglers ship with one of three front rotor packages \u2014 verify which one you have before ordering. Standard rotors are 0.95\" thick (PN 68273502AB), heavy-duty are 1.1\" thick (PN 68250085AB). Wrong rotor won't fit. Full job runs 2 hours, $140 OEM front pair or $320 for a full four-corner kit.","description":"The JL Wrangler shipped from the factory with three distinct front brake configurations: BR2/BR3/BR6 packages with 1.1\" thick front rotors, and BRY with 0.945\" rotors. Standard Sport and Sahara trims got the thinner BRY setup; Rubicon and tow-package trucks got the heavier 1.1\" rotors. The bracket and caliper bolt to the same knuckle, but the rotor itself is taller \u2014 install the wrong rotor and the pads won't reach.\n\nHow to tell which you have without VIN decoding: pop a wheel and measure with a micrometer. Anything 27mm or thicker (new) is heavy-duty. 24mm range is standard. The Mopar build sheet identifies the brake package by code (BR2, BR3, BR6, BRY) but most owners don't have it handy. Measuring is faster than digging up the build sheet.\n\nRotor wear vs warp: like the JK, the JL front rotors don't tolerate creek crossings well. A glowing rotor dunked in cold water sets the casting slightly out-of-round and you'll feel it as steering-wheel shimmy under braking from highway speeds. Resurfacing rarely lasts more than a single pad cycle. Replace the rotor when warp shows up.\n\nOEM Mopar rotors are mid-grade castings \u2014 perfectly adequate for stock use. Powerstop Z36 and EBC Premium upgrade rotors cost the same to a little more and run cooler, which matters for lifted JLs running 35s or for tow rigs. Drilled-and-slotted \"performance\" rotors offer no real benefit on a sub-5,000-lb vehicle with single-piston calipers \u2014 the drill holes become crack initiators after a few hundred heat cycles.\n\nThe procedure mirrors the JK closely but with two differences. First, JL caliper slider bolt torque is slightly higher (13\u201315 ft-lb depending on year), still way lower than the bracket bolts. Second, the JL master cylinder is more sensitive to backflow when you compress the piston \u2014 definitely open the reservoir cap and have a rag ready.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (19mm), lift the front, support on stands at the frame rails.","Remove front wheels. Note brake package: measure rotor thickness with micrometer to confirm.","Open the brake fluid reservoir cap. Drape a rag.","Remove the two 13mm caliper slider bolts on the back of the caliper. Slide the caliper off and support with a hanger \u2014 never let it hang from the brake hose.","Compress the caliper piston back into the caliper bore using a C-clamp and an old pad. Move slowly.","Pull old pads from bracket. Note shim orientation.","Remove the two 18mm caliper bracket bolts at the knuckle. Lift bracket off. Bag it; it's reusable.","Pull the old rotor off the hub. Use M8x1.25 puller-hole bolts if stuck.","Wire-brush the hub face clean. Rust ridges cause runout that returns as pedal pulsing.","Install new rotor (correct thickness for your brake package). Light anti-seize on the hub mating face only \u2014 never on the friction surface.","Reinstall bracket and torque bracket bolts to 110 ft-lb.","Install new pads if pad life is below 5mm. Apply light brake grease to pad backs and slider pins.","Slide caliper over the pads. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 14 ft-lb.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 110 ft-lb.","Pump brake pedal 6\u20138 times until firm. Top off fluid.","Bed in pads: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then 2\u20133 hard stops from 50 mph. Cool down without dragging."],"pros":["OEM Mopar rotors well-priced and widely stocked","Same caliper architecture as JK \u2014 common tools, similar procedure","Powerstop Z36 upgrade is bolt-on, cost-effective for lifted rigs","Job under 2 hours on a familiar truck"],"cons":["Three different rotor packages from the factory \u2014 wrong part won't fit","Drilled-and-slotted rotors offer zero benefit and crack faster","Master cylinder sensitive to piston backflow \u2014 must open reservoir cap","Rotors warp early on rigs that see water crossings"]},{"id":"jl-coolant-flush","title":"Coolant System Flush \u2014 JL Wrangler (2.0T and 3.6L)","vehicle":"jl","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":80},"tools_required":["drain pan","funnel","socket set","garden hose"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["cooling","coolant","flush","maintenance","overheating-prevention"],"summary":"The JL uses Mopar OAT (orange) coolant \u2014 do not mix it with universal green. Flush interval is 5 years or 150,000 miles. The procedure is the same on 2.0T and 3.6L, with minor routing differences.","description":"# Coolant System Flush \u2014 JL Wrangler (2.0T and 3.6L)\n\nJeep specifies Mopar OAT (Organic Acid Technology) coolant for the JL \u2014 it's dyed orange. If someone added green universal coolant at any point, the system chemistry is compromised and you need a full flush before refilling with OAT. Mixing turns the coolant acidic and accelerates water pump and head gasket wear.\n\nFactory service interval: 5 years or 150,000 miles on original fill, then every 3 years / 30,000 miles. Off-road use that ends in water crossing contamination warrants an earlier flush \u2014 check the reservoir clarity after every trip through deep water.\n\n- Mopar OAT coolant (orange) \u2014 1 gallon concentrate + distilled water, or 1 gallon pre-mixed 50/50\n- Distilled water (not tap \u2014 minerals leave deposits)\n- Drain pan (at least 2-gallon capacity)\n- Funnel\n- Garden hose\n- Socket set (8mm for lower radiator hose clamp if clamp-style)\n- Flush-T adapter (optional but speeds the process)\n\n**Drain the system**\n1. Let the engine cool completely \u2014 at least 2 hours after last run. Hot coolant under pressure will burn you.\n2. Place your drain pan under the radiator lower hose. The JL has a drain petcock on the bottom passenger-side corner of the radiator \u2014 turn it counterclockwise to open. If there's no petcock accessible, loosen the lower radiator hose clamp and pull the hose end.\n3. Open the coolant reservoir cap to break vacuum and speed the drain.\n4. Let drain fully (5\u201310 minutes).\n\n**Flush the system**\n5. Close the petcock or reconnect the lower hose.\n6. Fill the reservoir and radiator with distilled water only.\n7. Start the engine and run to operating temp (thermostat opens at ~192\u00b0F). Let it run 5 minutes at operating temp.\n8. Shut off and let cool completely. Drain again.\n9. Repeat the flush cycle once more if the drained water is anything other than clear.\n\n**Refill with OAT coolant**\n10. Close the drain. Fill with 50/50 OAT coolant/distilled water mix. The JL system capacity is 13.5 quarts (2.0T) or 13.8 quarts (3.6L).\n11. Leave the reservoir cap off. Start the engine and let it warm up to operating temp with the heat set to max (bleeds air from the heater core).\n12. Squeeze the upper radiator hose a few times while the engine runs to burp trapped air.\n13. Top off the reservoir to the MAX line as coolant circulates and air escapes. Cap the reservoir.\n14. Check for leaks at hose connections, the petcock, and the reservoir.\n\n- **Do not mix coolant types**: Green (IAT), pink (HOAT), and orange (OAT) are chemically incompatible. If you see anything other than orange in there, treat it as contaminated.\n- **Air pockets cause overheating**: The JL heater core is high in the system and traps air. Running with the heat on max during the fill cycle is not optional \u2014 it bleeds the core.\n- **2.0T turbo consideration**: The 2.0 Turbo has an additional oil-cooler circuit. After a flush, check the intercooler reservoir separately \u2014 it's a separate loop with its own small reservoir near the battery.\n- **Petcock strip risk**: JL plastic petcocks are fragile. Open them slowly and stop before forcing them.\n\n- Mopar OAT coolant concentrate (1 gallon): $18\u2013$25 at the dealer or online\n- Zerex G-OAT or Prestone 50/50 OAT (compatible aftermarket): $15\u2013$20 per gallon\n- Distilled water: $1\u2013$2 per gallon at any grocery store\n- Flush-T kit: $8\u2013$12 (optional; makes the distilled water flush cycle faster)\n\nTotal cost: $40\u2013$70 depending on how many flush cycles you run and whether you buy OEM or compatible aftermarket coolant."},{"id":"jl-thermostat-replacement","title":"JL Wrangler Thermostat Replacement (3.6L Pentastar)","vehicle":"jl","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3.0},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":120},"tools_required":["10mm socket","13mm socket","ratchet and extensions","pliers","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","coolant system familiarity"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["cooling","thermostat","pentastar","overheating","coolant"],"summary":"The 3.6L Pentastar thermostat is integrated into a housing on the driver's side of the block. Replacement is a 2-hour job \u2014 the main complication is coolant drainage and housing torque sequence. Use the factory torque spec; over-tightening cracks the plastic housing.","description":"# JL Wrangler Thermostat Replacement (3.6L Pentastar)\n\nThe Pentastar 3.6L uses an integrated thermostat-and-housing assembly rather than a standalone thermostat. When yours fails \u2014 usually showing up as slow warm-up, overheating, or temp gauge swings \u2014 the whole housing comes off as a unit. The replacement part from Mopar or Motorad runs $40\u2013$80.\n\n**Symptoms of a failing thermostat:**\n- Temperature gauge runs hotter than normal or fluctuates\n- Heater blows cold after the engine is fully warm\n- Coolant temperature reaches operating temp unusually slowly\n- Overheating under load, especially towing or trail use\n\n- Replacement thermostat housing (Mopar 68079900AA or Motorad equivalent)\n- New O-ring or gasket (often included with the housing \u2014 verify before ordering)\n- 2 gallons of Mopar HOAT coolant (orange)\n- Distilled water\n- 10mm and 13mm socket set, ratchet with 3\" and 6\" extensions\n- Pliers for hose clamps\n- Drain pan (2+ gallon capacity)\n- Funnel\n- Torque wrench (housing bolts torque to 105 in-lbs \u2014 not ft-lbs)\n\n**Drain the coolant**\n1. Let the engine cool completely \u2014 at minimum 2 hours after last run.\n2. Position the drain pan under the lower radiator hose. Squeeze the lower hose clamp and slide it back, then pull the hose off the radiator. Allow to drain fully.\n3. Alternatively, use the radiator petcock on the driver's side lower corner of the radiator (if equipped). Some JLs have it; some don't.\n\n**Locate the thermostat housing**\n4. The housing sits on the driver's side of the block, low down near the alternator. Follow the lower radiator hose \u2014 it leads directly to the housing. The housing has two or three 10mm bolts holding it to the block.\n\n**Disconnect the coolant hoses**\n5. Slide the clamps back on the hoses attached to the housing. There are typically two: the lower radiator hose and a smaller coolant bypass hose. Pull each hose off the housing nipples. More coolant will drain \u2014 keep the pan in place.\n\n**Remove the housing**\n6. Remove the housing bolts (two or three 10mm bolts). Note their positions \u2014 they vary in length.\n7. Pull the housing straight off the block. If it sticks, wiggle it gently. Don't pry against the block or you'll damage the mating surface.\n\n**Install the new housing**\n8. Clean the block mating surface with a rag. Remove any old gasket material.\n9. Seat the new O-ring or gasket on the new housing. Do not use RTV sealant with the O-ring.\n10. Position the housing on the block and thread the bolts in by hand. Torque to **105 in-lbs** in a crossing pattern. This is approximately 8.75 ft-lbs \u2014 do not exceed it. The housing is plastic; cracks here leak coolant directly onto the serpentine belt.\n\n**Reconnect hoses and refill**\n11. Reattach both coolant hoses. Slide the clamps into position \u2014 factory clamps should still have enough tension; replace if they feel weak.\n12. Reinstall the lower radiator hose if you disconnected it at the radiator.\n13. Fill the coolant reservoir with a 50/50 mix of Mopar HOAT coolant and distilled water. Fill slowly to limit air pockets.\n14. Start the engine with the cap off and watch the level drop as air bleeds out. Top off until the level stabilizes.\n15. Replace the cap, let the engine reach operating temp, and check for leaks at the housing.\n\n- **Housing torque**: 105 in-lbs is the spec. It feels under-torqued with a full-size ratchet. Use a torque wrench.\n- **Coolant type**: The JL takes Mopar HOAT (orange). Do not mix with green or universal coolant \u2014 it causes sludging and deposits.\n- **Air pockets**: The Pentastar can hold significant air in the system. Run the heater on full blast during the warm-up bleed to draw coolant through the heater core. Squeeze the upper hose periodically while the engine warms to dislodge trapped air.\n- **Leaks at the hose clamps**: If the factory spring clamps have lost tension, replace them with worm-drive clamps. Leaking hose connections on a fresh job are almost always clamp tension.\n\n- Mopar thermostat housing (OEM): $65\u2013$90 from a Jeep dealer or online\n- Motorad housing (aftermarket): $40\u2013$60 \u2014 quality is equivalent for street/trail use\n- Mopar HOAT coolant (1 gal): $18\u2013$25\n- Distilled water (1 gal): $1\u2013$2\n\nCoolant flushes every 50,000 miles substantially extend thermostat and water pump life. If your coolant is brown or has visible deposits when you drain it here, flush the system before refilling."},{"id":"jl-8hp75-trans","title":"8HP75 Automatic \u2014 Service Intervals and Fluid","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":220,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torx bits","torque wrench","fluid pump","scan tool with trans temp read or Tazer"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","fluid level by temperature procedure"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["transmission","fluid","maintenance","8HP"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ZF 8 & 9 Speed ATF (1 quart)","part_number":"68218925AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"ZF Lifeguard 8 ATF (1 liter, OEM-equivalent)","vendor":"ZF","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Mopar 8HP75 filter and pan gasket kit","part_number":"68225344AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Blau pre-packaged 8HP transmission service kit","vendor":"Blauparts","price_estimate_usd":380}],"citations":[{"title":"JL 8HP Specific Service Details (JL Wrangler Forums)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/jl-specific-auto-transmission-service-details.48248/"},{"title":"Blauparts 8HP50/8HP75 ATF Change Kit","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/jeep-wrangler-transmission-fluid-atf-change-kit-8-speed-850re-8hp50.html"},{"title":"ZF8 50K DIY Procedure (JL Wrangler Forums)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/zf8-50k-mile-transmission-service-diy-how-to-instructions.94302/page-17"}],"alternatives":["jl-transfer-case-jl","jl-etorque-understanding"],"summary":"Jeep markets the 8HP75 as \"lifetime fill,\" but ZF \u2014 the company that designs it \u2014 specifies service every 50,000-80,000 miles, and the gearbox lasts dramatically longer when you actually do it.","description":"The 8HP75 (Mopar code 850RE) is the same ZF-designed eight-speed used in BMW, Ram, and Maserati. It's a remarkable piece of engineering, but the \"lifetime fluid\" claim is a marketing convenience. ZF's own service literature calls for fluid changes at 50,000-80,000 miles, and shops that rebuild these boxes see the same failure pattern: burnt fluid leads to mechatronic and clutch-pack wear by 120k-150k miles. A $250 fluid service saves a $6,000 rebuild.\n\nThe fluid is non-negotiable: Mopar 68218925AB (ZF 8 & 9 speed ATF) or genuine ZF Lifeguard 8. Do not use a \"compatible\" or \"multi-vehicle\" ATF. The friction modifiers are specific to the wet clutch packs, and the wrong fluid causes shift flare and clutch glazing within thousands of miles.\n\nThe JL pan is metal with a separate bolt-on filter \u2014 meaning you can reuse the pan but should replace the filter and gasket every service. Capacity is roughly 9.5 quarts total, but a drain-and-fill only swaps about 5-5.5 quarts (the torque converter holds the rest). For best results, drain-fill twice with 1000 miles between, or run a fluid exchange machine.\n\nThe hard part isn't draining \u2014 it's filling to the correct level. There is no dipstick. The fill plug doubles as a level check, and the procedure requires the fluid at 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F (30\u00b0C-50\u00b0C), the vehicle level on a lift or all four jack stands, and the trans in Park with the engine running. You need a scan tool (or Tazer) that reads transmission fluid temperature. Below 86\u00b0F you under-fill; above 122\u00b0F you over-fill. Both kill the box.\n\nIf you've never done this and don't have a scan tool that reads trans temp, this is the maintenance task to pay a dealer or independent shop to do. The fluid is cheap; the access is straightforward; the temperature window is what trips people up.","steps":["Drive the Jeep until trans is warm (10-15 minutes), then let it cool. Goal is 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F at fill time.","Lift all four wheels to level the vehicle. Trans pan rail must be horizontal.","Place a wide drain pan under the trans. Remove the level/fill plug first (8mm Allen) \u2014 if you can't get this off, stop, because you can't refill.","Remove pan bolts in a crisscross pattern. Drop the pan slowly \u2014 it will be full of fluid.","Remove the bolt-on filter. Note its orientation. Install new filter and new pan gasket. Torque pan bolts to 89 in-lb in crisscross pattern.","From the fill port, pump in fresh fluid until it begins to dribble out (about 5-5.5 quarts).","Start the engine, foot on brake, cycle through P-R-N-D-2-1 holding each gear 3 seconds, return to Park.","With engine running, monitor trans fluid temp via scan tool or Tazer. When temp hits 86\u00b0F-122\u00b0F, fluid should be at a steady dribble from the fill port. If running fast, pump in more; if dry, you're under-filled.","Install fill plug, torque to 26 ft-lb.","Drive 10 miles checking for leaks and shift quality. Plan a second drain-and-fill at 1,000 miles to refresh the fluid that stayed in the converter."],"pros":["Extends transmission life by tens of thousands of miles","Pan and filter are accessible without dropping the trans","Mopar fluid is widely available"],"cons":["Requires a scan tool that reads trans fluid temperature","Temperature window for fill is narrow","Two drain-and-fills (or a flush machine) needed to fully refresh fluid","Wrong ATF causes shift flare within thousands of miles"]},{"id":"jl-etorque-understanding","title":"eTorque Mild Hybrid \u2014 What It Is and What It Isn't","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner (optional)"],"skills_required":["reading vehicle documentation"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["hybrid","electrical","battery","warranty"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"What is eTorque and does my Jeep Wrangler have it?","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/what-is-e-torque"},{"title":"48 volt eTorque battery reliability discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/48-volt-etorque-battery-reliability-system.74361/"},{"title":"Battery issues and How to test 48V eTorque Battery","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/battery-issues-and-how-to-test-48v-etorque-battery.111007/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"eTorque is a mild hybrid system that replaces the alternator with a 48V belt-driven motor generator, smooths start-stop, and adds about 90 lb-ft (2.0L turbo) or 130 lb-ft (3.6L V6) of brief assist torque. It is not a plug-in hybrid and it does not let you drive on electric power alone.","description":"If you bought a 2019 or newer JL with the 2.0L turbo, you have eTorque whether you wanted it or not \u2014 it's standard on that engine. On the 3.6L Pentastar, eTorque was optional and is identified by the \"eTorque\" badging or by the second battery under the rear seat. The system has three pieces: a 48V lithium battery pack (under the rear passenger seat), a belt-driven motor generator unit (MGU) where the alternator used to live, and a DC-DC converter that steps 48V down to charge the conventional 12V starter battery.\n\nHere is the part most people miss: the 12V battery still cranks the engine on a cold start, exactly like a non-hybrid Jeep. The MGU only takes over to restart the engine after an auto stop-start shutoff. So when you turn the key first thing in the morning, you're using the same lead-acid battery any JK owner has been using for fifteen years. The 48V battery handles the smooth restart at red lights, captures regen energy on coastdown, and feeds the MGU when it pushes 90 to 130 lb-ft into the crankshaft for the first second or two of acceleration. That brief torque assist is what makes the 2.0L feel quicker than its 270 hp on paper.\n\nCommon eTorque problems on JL forums cluster around three things. First, the 12V starter battery still dies \u2014 owners often blame eTorque but the 12V is a normal AGM battery that ages on a normal AGM battery timeline (4\u20136 years in Phoenix heat). Second, the 48V battery occasionally throws codes for hybrid battery coolant sensor or temp sensor faults; dealerships generally replace under warranty. Third, some early 2.0L trucks reported weekly drain even with 14.4V charging \u2014 almost always traced to a parasitic draw, not the hybrid system. The 48V battery and related components are covered by an 8 year / 80,000 mile federal hybrid warranty, so most of the expensive failures are not coming out of your wallet.\n\nWhat eTorque does not do: it does not give you all-electric range, it does not let you bypass the 12V system if it dies, and it does not produce enough sustained torque to matter once you're above idle. Treat it as a smarter alternator that also softens the auto stop-start hand-off.","steps":["Identify whether your JL has eTorque by looking for the badge on the rear quarter panel or the second battery box under the rear passenger seat.","If you see a low-battery warning, test the 12V starter battery first \u2014 same drill as any AGM battery.","If the 48V hybrid system throws a code (P1A0A, P0A7D, P0AFA, others), document it and take it to a dealer while still under 8/80k warranty.","Do not disconnect or service the orange-shielded 48V cables \u2014 high-voltage hybrid wiring requires shop-level safety procedures.","Maintain the 12V battery normally: clean terminals, check resting voltage at 12.6V, replace at 4\u20136 years."],"pros":["48V battery, MGU, and related components covered 8 years / 80,000 miles","Smooths the auto stop-start restart","Adds brief torque assist on takeoff","Improves city MPG by 1\u20132 mpg"],"cons":["12V starter battery still dies on a normal schedule, confusing owners","Hybrid components are dealer-service items if out of warranty","Adds weight and complexity vs. a non-eTorque Pentastar","Trail repairs of the high-voltage side are not a DIY job"]},{"id":"jl-front-driveshaft-jl","title":"Front Driveshaft Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":750},"tools_required":["15mm socket","18mm socket","torque wrench","marker","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","driveline geometry"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["driveshaft","rzeppa","cv-joint","vibration","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front driveshaft (JL non-Rubicon)","part_number":"68272525AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Mopar OEM Rzeppa CV joint repair kit","part_number":"68272534AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Adams Driveshaft 1310 front (heavy-duty)","vendor":"Adams Driveshaft","price_estimate_usd":595},{"name":"Tom Wood's JL front 1310 double cardan","vendor":"Tom Wood's","price_estimate_usd":575}],"citations":[{"title":"JK/JL front driveshaft CV failure \u2014 JK-Forum","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/stock-jk-tech-12/front-driveshaft-cv-replacement-245861/"},{"title":"Tom Wood's JL 1310 front shaft","url":"https://4xshaft.com/products/jk-jeep"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A clunk on throttle tip-in or a vibration that starts after a lift is almost always the front Rzeppa CV joint \u2014 the stock shaft is the weak point on every JL, and lifting more than 2.5 inches will kill it faster.","description":"The JL front driveshaft uses a Rzeppa CV joint at the transfer case end and a single u-joint at the axle pinion. The Rzeppa design lets the shaft articulate without the harmonic vibration you get from a u-joint at a steep angle. Stock-height JLs run them for the life of the truck. Lifted JLs eat them, and the failure mode is consistent: a torn boot, grease slung across the underbody, and a clunk every time torque transfers through the joint.\n\nThe failure progression is predictable. The Rzeppa boot tears or weeps \u2014 sometimes from age, more often from articulation past the joint's design limit. Grease escapes. Dirt and water enter. The bearings inside the joint dry out and start to score. You hear a clunk on acceleration or deceleration, then a vibration that worsens as speed climbs. Catch it at the torn-boot stage and you can clean, regrease, and reboot the joint with a Mopar repair kit around $180. Wait until the clunk shows up and the bearings are already damaged \u2014 replace the joint or the whole shaft.\n\nThree replacement paths exist. The OEM Mopar shaft (68272525AC for non-Rubicon JL) at roughly $450 is a direct fit, but it is the same Rzeppa design that just failed. If the Jeep is stock and stays stock, OEM is fine. If you lifted it, replace the failure with something better. A heavy-duty 1310 shaft from Adams Driveshaft or Tom Wood's at $575\u2013$595 uses a double-cardan front joint, which tolerates the steeper operating angle a lifted JL imposes. Heavier-walled tubing means it survives rock hits and torque from larger tires.\n\nDiagnose before you order. Drop the front shaft, hold the CV end, and try to articulate it through its range. A healthy joint moves smoothly and silently. A failed joint clicks, binds, or rolls roughly. Inspect the boot for tears and the centering yoke for play \u2014 non-greasable yokes (211544X) and greasable yokes (211355X) both wear. Check the rear u-joint at the pinion end while the shaft is down; replacing one joint without inspecting the other is leaving a job half done.","steps":["Park on level ground, chock the rear wheels, and raise the front. Support on jack stands.","Mark the front shaft flange and the pinion yoke with a paint pen so you can reinstall in the same orientation. Phasing matters.","Remove the four 15mm bolts holding the shaft to the front axle pinion yoke.","Remove the four bolts at the transfer case flange (typically 15mm).","Lower the shaft, supporting the weight \u2014 it is not heavy but the CV end is fragile if dropped on concrete.","Inspect both joints. If only the CV is bad and the rest of the shaft is sound, install a Mopar repair kit per its instructions. Otherwise install the new shaft.","Bolt the new shaft up with the alignment marks aligned. Torque flange bolts to 22 ft-lbs at the pinion and 22 ft-lbs at the transfer case. Verify clearance from exhaust and skids before lowering the Jeep."],"pros":["Eliminates the clunk and vibration immediately","Repair kit option keeps the shaft if the bearings are still good","Heavy-duty aftermarket shafts handle lift, 35s, and trail use","Job is 1\u20132 hours with hand tools"],"cons":["OEM shaft repeats the same failure mode under lift","Aftermarket heavy-duty shafts run $575+","Boots tear from articulation, not just age \u2014 lifted Jeeps see more frequent failures","Pinion seal often weeps after a hard CV failure and may need replacement too"]},{"id":"jl-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker Options \u2014 Dana 35 and Dana 44","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing puller","dial indicator","ring gear setup tools","inch-pound torque wrench"],"skills_required":["drivetrain work","differential setup","axle bearing service"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["locker","differential","traction","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker RD100 \u2014 Dana 35 (JL Sport/Sahara)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"ARB Air Locker RD156 \u2014 Dana 44 (JL Rubicon)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 Dana 44 (JL Rubicon)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Detroit Truetrac \u2014 Dana 35 (JL Sport/Sahara)","vendor":"Eaton Detroit Truetrac","price_estimate_usd":449}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker Product Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/air-lockers/"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Installation and Specs","url":"https://www.eatondifferentials.com/elocker"},{"title":"Detroit Truetrac Dana 35 Product Page","url":"https://www.eatondifferentials.com/truetrac"}],"alternatives":["jl-regear"],"summary":"A rear locker changes the JL's off-road capability more dramatically than almost any other single upgrade \u2014 but your rear axle (Dana 35 vs Dana 44) determines which lockers are available, and those axle differences also determine whether a locker is the right investment or whether an axle upgrade should come first.","description":"The axle situation on the JL is the critical starting context. JL Sport and Sport S models come with a Dana 35 rear axle \u2014 a solid, competent axle for street and mild trail use, but lighter-duty than the Dana 44. The Sahara also ships with the Dana 35 in most configurations. The Rubicon (and the Rubicon 392) gets the Dana 44, which is the significantly stronger choice for technical trail use. If you're on a Sport or Sahara and considering a locker, you need to decide whether you're locking a Dana 35 long-term or upgrading to a Dana 44 carrier down the road \u2014 because the lockers are not interchangeable.\n\nFor the Dana 35 (Sport/Sahara), the two realistic choices are the ARB Air Locker ($799) or the Detroit Truetrac ($449). The Truetrac is not technically a locker \u2014 it's a helical gear limited-slip differential that provides significant bias toward the wheel with traction under load, but does not achieve full lock. For mild-to-moderate trail use on a rig that sees pavement regularly, the Truetrac is the right choice: it's transparent on-road, requires no compressor or electrical system, and the $449 price point is accessible. For genuine rock crawling where complete drive wheel lock is required, the Truetrac falls short.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker for the Dana 35 ($799) delivers full lock on demand. ARB's air locker engages via an onboard air compressor (required separately \u2014 budget $150\u2013$300 for a basic unit) and disengages cleanly when the air is released. The on-road behavior when unlocked is stock-equivalent. The ARB is the correct choice for a Dana 35 build that sees sustained technical terrain. Just be realistic: a Dana 35 with a locker will eventually meet a trail that exceeds the axle's shaft strength. Know your terrain.\n\nFor the Dana 44 (Rubicon), both the ARB Air Locker ($849) and Eaton ELocker ($649) are full-lock solutions with different engagement mechanisms. The ARB requires a compressor; the Eaton ELocker engages electrically via a dash switch. The ELocker is the value choice for a Rubicon build \u2014 $200 less than the ARB, no compressor required, reliable engagement, and the electrical install is cleaner. The ARB's advantage is faster engagement response and the ability to share the compressor with ARB lockers front and rear, plus tire inflation duties.\n\nRubicon owners note: the factory Selec-Trac II transfer case includes an electronic rear locker. If your Rubicon already has the factory locker, evaluate whether an aftermarket locker adds meaningful capability before spending $649\u2013$849.","steps":["Drain the differential fluid before any disassembly.","Support the rear axle on jack stands and remove both rear wheels.","Remove the rear brake drums or caliper/rotor assemblies (depending on trim) and disconnect the axle shafts.","Mark the bearing caps before removal \u2014 they are directional and must return to the same position.","Remove the carrier bearing caps and extract the ring gear/carrier assembly.","Transfer the ring gear to the locker carrier per manufacturer instructions. New ring gear bolts should be used \u2014 old bolts stretch and should not be reused.","Set ring gear backlash and bearing preload per manufacturer specs using a dial indicator. This step requires patience and is the most critical part of the install \u2014 incorrect backlash causes noise, premature wear, or failure.","For ARB: route air lines through the axle housing per the included instructions before reinstalling carrier.","Reinstall carrier, torque bearing caps to spec.","Reconnect axle shafts, reinstall brakes, reinstall wheels.","For ARB: mount the air compressor, route air lines to the cab switch per install instructions.","For Eaton ELocker: route electrical harness to the cab switch location per install instructions.","Refill differential with gear oil per manufacturer's fluid spec \u2014 some lockers require specific viscosity.","Test locker engagement off the vehicle before returning to trail. Drive slowly, confirm engagement and disengagement."],"pros":["A rear locker delivers the most direct off-road capability gain of any single JL upgrade \u2014 full traction to both rear wheels in a bind","ARB Air Locker is the gold standard for on-demand, full-lock engagement with stock-equivalent on-road behavior","Eaton ELocker delivers full lock at lower cost than ARB with no compressor requirement","Detroit Truetrac is the right answer for a Dana 35 on a mild-trail rig that sees significant pavement","ARB compressor (required for air locker) doubles as a trail inflation tool \u2014 functional overlap"],"cons":["Dana 35 (Sport/Sahara) is a lighter-duty axle than the Dana 44 \u2014 a locker increases the load on the axle shafts, which are already the weak point; know your terrain limits","ARB Air Locker installation requires a compressor and plumbing, adding cost and complexity beyond the locker itself","Setting ring gear backlash correctly requires a dial indicator and specific knowledge; this is not a beginner install","Incorrect carrier bearing preload will cause differential noise within the first few hundred miles \u2014 don't skip the setup spec steps","Eaton ELocker is not available for the Dana 35 \u2014 Sport/Sahara owners are limited to ARB or Truetrac","Rubicon's factory electronic rear locker may make an aftermarket locker redundant \u2014 evaluate factory capability before purchasing"]},{"id":"jl-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the JL \u2014 4.56 and 4.88 Ratio Selection","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":2400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing puller","bearing press","dial indicator","inch-pound torque wrench","ring gear setup tools","press or shop press"],"skills_required":["advanced drivetrain work","differential setup","bearing service","gear mesh pattern reading"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["regear","gears","axle","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon Gear Ring and Pinion 4.88 \u2014 Dana 35 Rear","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":209},{"name":"Yukon Gear Ring and Pinion 4.88 \u2014 Dana 44 Rear","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":229},{"name":"Yukon Gear Ring and Pinion 4.88 \u2014 Dana 30 Front","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Yukon Gear Master Overhaul Kit \u2014 Dana 44","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":249}],"citations":[{"title":"Yukon Gear JL Wrangler Ring and Pinion Sets","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/jeep/wrangler-jl/ring-and-pinions.html"},{"title":"Ring and Pinion Ratio Selection Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/article/jeep-wrangler-ring-pinion-gear-ratio-guide"},{"title":"Dana 35 vs Dana 44 Comparison Guide","url":"https://offroad.com/jeep/dana-35-vs-44"}],"alternatives":["jl-locker-rear","jl-tire-fitment-37s"],"summary":"The stock gear ratios on most JL trims were sized for street-biased tires \u2014 if you've moved to 35s or 37s and haven't regeared, you're working the drivetrain harder than it was designed for and leaving significant trail capability on the table.","description":"The math behind regearing is straightforward. Your engine makes peak torque in a specific RPM range. Larger tires rotate more slowly per mile, which effectively changes your final drive ratio and moves your RPM operating point down out of that optimal range. The result: sluggish acceleration, transmission hunting on grades, and a crawl ratio that feels inadequate on steep technical terrain. Regearing restores the RPM relationship to a range the drivetrain was designed to operate in.\n\nJL gear ratio context by trim:\n- **Sahara:** Stock 3.45. With 35s, move to 4.56. With 37s, move to 4.88.\n- **Sport/Sport S:** Stock 3.73. With 35s, 4.10 is acceptable; 4.56 is better. With 37s, 4.88.\n- **Rubicon:** Stock 4.10. With 35s, 4.10 is workable; 4.56 improves trail performance meaningfully. With 37s, 4.56 at minimum; 4.88 for serious rock builds.\n\nThe Sahara with 3.45 gears and 37s is the worst-case scenario \u2014 the highway RPM drop is noticeable, low-speed trail control is compromised, and the transmission is working in a range it wasn't mapped for. This is not a \"it runs, just a bit slow\" situation; it's a configuration that accelerates powertrain wear. Regear before running 37s on a Sahara.\n\n**Front and rear must be regeared together.** Running mismatched ratios front to rear on a part-time 4WD system causes severe binding and drivetrain damage when 4WD is engaged. This is not optional.\n\nYukon Gear is the benchmark brand for JL ring and pinion sets. Their steel quality is consistent, the manufacturing tolerances are tight (which simplifies setup), and they support both Dana 30/35 (Sport/Sahara) and Dana 44 (Rubicon) fitments. The master overhaul kit ($249) includes all bearings, races, seals, shims, and hardware needed to complete the job \u2014 don't attempt to reuse factory bearings on a regear.\n\n**Labor consideration:** Setting up ring and pinion gears correctly is one of the most skill-dependent jobs in the drivetrain world. Incorrect backlash or bearing preload will cause gear noise, premature wear, or catastrophic failure within 10,000 miles. If you haven't done it before, the first regear should be done at a reputable 4WD shop \u2014 watch, learn, then DIY the second axle.","steps":["Drain differential fluid from both axles before disassembly.","Front axle: support axle, remove wheels, disconnect front driveshaft, remove axle shafts.","Remove front diff cover, mark bearing cap positions before removal.","Extract carrier/ring gear assembly, remove pinion.","Press old pinion and carrier bearings off using a bearing puller or press.","Install new pinion bearings on new pinion. Set pinion depth using shim stack (this requires a dial indicator and pattern checking \u2014 follow Yukon's instructions precisely).","Install pinion, set pinion bearing preload to manufacturer spec using an inch-pound torque wrench.","Transfer ring gear to carrier (or install new carrier if upgrading). Install new ring gear bolts \u2014 do not reuse old bolts.","Set carrier bearing preload and ring gear backlash to spec. Check gear mesh contact pattern with gear marking compound \u2014 the pattern must be centered before final assembly.","Install diff cover with new gasket, torque to spec.","Repeat procedure for rear axle (Dana 35 or Dana 44 depending on trim).","Fill both differentials with correct gear oil per manufacturer spec (some ring and pinion installs require specific break-in fluid \u2014 follow Yukon's recommendation).","Drive gently for the first 500 miles. Avoid sustained high-speed runs during break-in. Change break-in fluid at 500 miles."],"pros":["Restores factory RPM operating range after tire size increase \u2014 the single most important drivetrain step for builds running 35s or 37s","Dramatically improves trail crawl ratio, low-speed control, and pull-away performance","4.88 gears on a Sahara with 37s transforms a sluggish street-and-parking-lot Jeep into a capable trail rig","Yukon Gear's quality and documentation are the best in the class for JL fitments","Regear done alongside a locker install saves a second teardown \u2014 plan both jobs together when possible"],"cons":["Difficulty 5 \u2014 incorrect gear mesh setup causes noise and failure; this is the most technically demanding job on a Jeep","Both axles must be done simultaneously, doubling the parts cost","Parts alone run $650\u2013$800 (front + rear gear sets plus master kit); shop labor adds $1,200\u2013$1,600 for the pair, making total installed cost $2,000\u2013$2,400","Break-in period requires restraint \u2014 spirited driving in the first 500 miles risks premature gear wear","Dana 35 (Sport/Sahara rear) has a smaller ring gear and lighter shafts \u2014 regearing it to 4.88 doesn't change the fact that it's a lighter-duty axle","The Rubicon 392 has a different rear axle (Dana 44 AdvanTEK) with different gear selection \u2014 verify exact axle spec before purchasing gears"]},{"id":"jl-sye","title":"SYE Kit \u2014 Slip Yoke Eliminator for Lifted JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"transfer_case","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":320,"high":650},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","transmission jack","floor jack","pry bar","drain pan","snap ring pliers","yoke puller or slide hammer"],"skills_required":["drivetrain work","driveshaft angle measurement","transfer case familiarity"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["sye","slip-yoke-eliminator","driveshaft","vibration","lift","transfer-case","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Tom Woods JL SYE Kit (NV241OR / MP1222, with CV driveshaft)","vendor":"Tom Woods Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":595},{"name":"Advance Adapters SYE Kit \u2014 JL Wrangler (NV241OR)","vendor":"Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":485},{"name":"Rough Country SYE Kit + Rear Driveshaft (JL 2018\u20132024)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":349}],"citations":[{"title":"Tom Woods Driveshafts JL SYE Application","url":"https://www.tomwoodscustomdriveshafts.com"},{"title":"Advance Adapters JL SYE Kit","url":"https://www.advanceadapters.com"}],"alternatives":["jl-regear","jl-sliders"],"summary":"A 3.5\"+ lift on the JL changes the rear driveshaft angle enough that the stock slip yoke \u2014 riding on a fixed-length driveshaft \u2014 produces vibration at highway speed. The SYE replaces the slip yoke with a fixed output yoke and pairs it with a longer, double-cardan driveshaft that handles the steeper angle cleanly.","description":"The JL Wrangler's rear driveshaft uses a slip yoke at the transfer case output. At stock ride height this works \u2014 the driveshaft angle is shallow enough that the slip yoke doesn't bind or produce vibration. Add 3.5\" or more of lift and the angle steepens past the point where the slip yoke can handle it. The result is a vibration that appears at 45\u201370 mph, often described as a rhythmic buzz or drone that changes with speed. It's not subtle once it sets in.\n\nThe SYE (Slip Yoke Eliminator) kit replaces the stock slip-yoke output flange with a fixed output flange and internally relocates the slip point. This converts the transfer case output to a fixed yoke, which then mates to a longer, CV-joint driveshaft. The CV joint at the front of the driveshaft cancels the u-joint vibration that causes the problem at steep angles. The result: a JL lifted to 4.5\" or more that drives on the highway like a stock truck.\n\nThe JL uses different transfer cases depending on trim and model year. Rubicon models use the NV241OR Rock-Trac (2-speed, 4:1 low range). Most non-Rubicon JLs use the MP1222 (full-time 4WD option) or the MP3023 selectable unit. Confirm your transfer case before ordering \u2014 SYE kits are transfer case-specific, and a kit built for the NV241OR will not fit the MP1222. Pull the skid plate and read the transfer case ID tag if you aren't sure.\n\nOrder the SYE kit and the replacement rear driveshaft together, from the same vendor if possible. The driveshaft length must be measured for your specific lift height and suspension type \u2014 most vendors require you to specify lift height and whether you have a long-arm or short-arm suspension at the time of order. A mismeasured driveshaft is the most common reason SYE installs produce vibration post-install.\n\nTom Woods is the premier shop for this application. Their kits are built to order, the driveshaft comes indexed for correct phasing, and their support for JL-specific nuances is established. Advance Adapters makes a quality kit for the NV241OR specifically. Rough Country's bundled option is the budget path and works for moderate-lift builds.","steps":["Drain the transfer case fluid. Support the skid plate and remove it.","Measure and photograph the existing driveshaft angle before removal.","Remove the rear driveshaft at both ends. Mark the orientation at the pinion flange for reinstallation reference.","Support the transfer case with a transmission jack. Remove the crossmember if required per the SYE instructions.","Remove the stock slip yoke from the transfer case output shaft per the kit instructions \u2014 typically requires a slide hammer or yoke puller.","Install the SYE internal components per the kit's sequence: output bearing, new seal, fixed output flange.","Reinstall the transfer case crossmember. Torque to factory spec.","Install the new double-cardan CV driveshaft. Verify phasing marks align (u-joint ears in phase).","Torque driveshaft flange bolts to 18\u201322 ft-lb at the pinion end and 30 ft-lb at the transfer case output.","Refill the transfer case with the manufacturer-specified fluid (typically ATF+4 for JL applications \u2014 verify).","Road test at highway speed. Any remaining vibration usually indicates driveshaft phasing or a lingering pinion angle issue \u2014 measure pinion angle at ride height and correct with adjustable upper control arms if needed."],"pros":["Eliminates the root cause of highway vibration on lifted JLs \u2014 not a bandaid fix","Fixed output yoke and CV driveshaft handles 4.5\"\u20136\"+ of lift cleanly","Improves rear driveshaft life by running correct angles instead of fighting the geometry","Tom Woods and Advance Adapters have decades of Jeep drivetrain experience behind their kits"],"cons":["Requires transfer case-specific parts \u2014 must confirm your TC model before ordering","Driveshaft must be measured for your lift height \u2014 not an off-the-shelf part","More involved than bolt-on suspension work \u2014 transfer case output work requires care with seals and bearing installation","If pinion angle is also wrong (common after a lift), the SYE alone won't cure all vibration \u2014 both need attention"]},{"id":"jl-aux-switch-wiring","title":"Wiring Accessories to the JL Factory Aux Switches","vehicle":"jl","category":"electrical","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":250},"tools_required":["wire stripper","crimping tool","multimeter","heat gun","zip ties","panel removal tools"],"skills_required":["basic wiring","multimeter use"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["electrical","aux-switches","accessories","lighting","wiring"],"summary":"The JL Rubicon and many Sport S trims ship with factory auxiliary switches on the dashboard. These are fully powered and fused from the factory \u2014 you just need to run a wire from the switch output to your accessory. No relay required for loads under 20A.","description":"# Wiring Accessories to the JL Factory Aux Switches\n\nThe JL's factory auxiliary switches (labeled AUX 1 through AUX 4 on Rubicon trims, sometimes two on Sport S) are the cleanest way to power trail accessories. They're already integrated into the dash, fused through the under-hood PDC, and tied into the factory switch bank. You don't need a separate relay for most applications \u2014 lights, compressors under 20A, and similar loads wire directly.\n\n**What's already there:**\n- Each switch outputs up to 20A (some applications derate to 15A \u2014 verify in your FSM)\n- The switch outputs are located at the switch control module under the dash on the passenger side\n- Power is present at the connector even on non-Rubicon trims \u2014 the harness runs to a connector but is capped off\n\n- Wiring diagram for your specific JL (2018\u20132024 varies \u2014 check your FSM or AllDataDIY)\n- 14 AWG or 12 AWG wire (length depends on accessory location)\n- Weatherproof connectors or heat-shrink butt connectors\n- Crimping tool and wire stripper\n- Heat gun (for heat-shrink connectors)\n- Zip ties and split loom conduit\n- Multimeter\n- Panel removal tools (for underdash trim)\n\n**Locate the switch output harness**\n1. Remove the lower passenger-side underdash panel. It clips in \u2014 start from a corner and work around. No bolts on most JLs.\n2. The switch control module is mounted to the firewall or dash structure. Look for a multi-pin connector with wires running toward the dash switches.\n3. Identify the output wires. On most JLs, the output wires are a lighter gauge than the power supply wires. Confirm using a test light with the ignition on \u2014 the output wires should show 12V only when the corresponding dash switch is pressed.\n\n**Test outputs before wiring**\n4. With a multimeter in DC voltage mode, probe each output wire with the ignition on and the switch both ON and OFF. You're looking for 12V switched output (12V on, 0V off). Any wire showing 12V regardless of switch position is a power feed, not an output \u2014 don't connect to it.\n\n**Run the output wire to your accessory**\n5. Cut a length of 14 AWG wire long enough to reach your accessory from the switch module. Add 20% slack \u2014 it always takes more than you think.\n6. Crimp a weatherproof connector onto the switch output terminal. Connect the other end of your wire.\n7. Route the wire toward the accessory. Front-mounted lights typically run through the firewall grommet on the driver's side or through the existing wiring pass-through.\n8. At the accessory end, connect power. Ground the accessory to a solid body ground near the accessory \u2014 do not daisy-chain grounds.\n\n**Seal and protect**\n9. Wrap all exposed connections in self-amalgamating tape or use heat-shrink connectors.\n10. Secure the wire run with zip ties every 12\u201318 inches. Anywhere the wire could contact moving parts or heat sources, add split loom conduit.\n11. Reinstall the underdash panel.\n\n- **Fuse sizing**: The factory PDC fuses the circuit, but if you add a 20A load to a 15A-fused switch, the fuse will blow. Check the actual fuse rating in your underhood PDC before assuming you have 20A.\n- **Relay for high-draw accessories**: Winches, high-draw air compressors, and roof-mounted lighting arrays draw more than 20A. In these cases, use the aux switch output to trigger a relay, and run a separate heavy-gauge feed from the battery to the relay. The switch sees minimal current; the relay handles the load.\n- **Non-Rubicon installs**: Sport S and Sahara trims may have the harness pre-run but the PDC fuse slots may be empty. You may need to populate the fuse slots \u2014 this is documented in the FSM.\n- **Wire routing near exhaust**: The passenger-side firewall is close to exhaust routing under load. Any wire crossing near that area needs conduit.\n\n- 14 AWG wire (25 ft spool): $15\u2013$25 (Ancor or similar marine-grade is best for weatherproofing)\n- Heat-shrink weatherproof connectors (assorted): $15\u2013$25\n- Split loom conduit (10 ft): $8\u2013$12\n- Relay (if needed for high-draw): $15\u2013$30 (Bosch-style relay + relay socket)\n- Fuses for PDC population (if non-Rubicon): $5\u2013$10\n\nThe factory switch bank is one of the better-executed features on the JL. The work is mostly wire routing \u2014 the electrical infrastructure is already done."},{"id":"jl-dual-battery-setup","title":"Dual Battery Setup for JL Overland Builds","vehicle":"jl","category":"electrical","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","wire stripper","crimper","heat gun","multimeter","drill"],"skills_required":["basic wiring","battery handling"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["electrical","dual-battery","overland","auxiliary-power","winch"],"summary":"A dual battery setup on the JL requires an isolator or DC-DC charger \u2014 the JL's smart alternator does not charge a secondary battery reliably without one. Budget $400\u2013$600 for a capable system; expect $800\u2013$1,200 fully built out with lithium.","description":"# Dual Battery Setup for JL Overland Builds\n\nThe JL Wrangler's factory alternator uses variable voltage regulation \u2014 it doesn't hold 14.4V constantly the way older alternators did. That matters because a simple battery isolator solenoid relies on voltage differential to charge the aux battery, and the JL's alternator may not produce enough differential to trigger it reliably. If you use a traditional isolator on a JL, you may find your aux battery never fully charges.\n\nThe correct solution is a DC-DC charger (also called a battery-to-battery charger or B2B charger). It actively pulls from the start battery and charges the aux battery at a proper charge profile, regardless of the alternator's voltage behavior. Victron, Redarc, and Sterling make well-regarded units.\n\n- DC-DC charger: Victron Orion-Tr Smart 12/12-30A (~$180) or Redarc BCDC1240D (~$280)\n- Auxiliary battery: AGM or LiFePO4 \u2014 see sizing notes below\n- Battery box or tray (for aux battery location)\n- 4 AWG or 2 AWG wiring (length depends on routing)\n- Inline fuse holders (ANL or MIDI fuses at each battery)\n- Ring terminals and heat-shrink butt connectors\n- Bus bars (positive and negative) for distribution\n- Battery disconnect switch (optional but recommended)\n\n**Sizing the auxiliary battery:**\n- Overlanding (fridge, lighting, USB charging): 100Ah AGM sufficient; 50Ah LiFePO4 equivalent\n- Winching-capable: 200Ah AGM minimum; 100Ah LiFePO4 minimum\n- LiFePO4 is half the weight of AGM for equal usable capacity \u2014 worth the premium ($300\u2013$600 vs $120\u2013$200 for AGM at 100Ah)\n\n**Choose your aux battery location**\nThe JL hood/engine bay has limited space. Most builds mount the aux battery:\n- Rear cargo area (most common for overlanders): requires longer wiring runs but unlimited battery size options\n- Under-hood in the passenger-side void (common for winch-assist setups): tighter clearance, limited to ~35 group size\n\n**Wire the DC-DC charger**\n1. Mount the DC-DC charger in a dry, ventilated location \u2014 near the engine bay fuse box or on the firewall.\n2. Run a fused positive lead from the start battery to the DC-DC charger input. Fuse within 18\" of the start battery \u2014 use a 40A ANL fuse for a 30A charger.\n3. Run a fused positive lead from the DC-DC charger output to the aux battery. Fuse within 18\" of the aux battery \u2014 same 40A ANL.\n4. Run a chassis ground from the charger to a clean body ground point (not the battery terminal \u2014 ground the charger chassis to the frame directly).\n\n**Wire the aux battery for load distribution**\n5. Mount a positive bus bar and negative bus bar near the aux battery.\n6. Run all aux loads (fridge, lights, inverter, USB ports) through the bus bars \u2014 not directly to the battery terminals.\n7. Add a fuse for each load circuit at the bus bar. Size each fuse to the wire gauge and load: a 10A circuit gets a 15A fuse, not a 60A.\n8. Grounding: run a dedicated negative cable from the aux battery negative terminal to a clean chassis ground point \u2014 do not share the start battery's ground path.\n\n**Verify the system**\n9. Measure voltage at the aux battery with the vehicle off: should read 12.6\u201313.0V (full AGM) or 13.2\u201313.4V (full LiFePO4).\n10. Start the vehicle. Within 30 seconds, the DC-DC charger should activate. Measure aux battery voltage \u2014 it should begin climbing toward 14.4\u201314.6V.\n11. Monitor with a battery monitor (Victron BMV-712 is the standard) to track state of charge and confirm the system is working.\n\n- **Wire gauge matters**: Undersized wire is a fire hazard. Use a wire gauge calculator \u2014 4 AWG handles 30A at 15-foot runs; longer runs need 2 AWG.\n- **Fuse every positive lead within 18\" of its source battery**: This is not optional. An unfused wire that shorts is a vehicle fire.\n- **LiFePO4 compatibility**: Lithium batteries require a charger with a lithium charge profile. Most modern DC-DC chargers (Victron Orion-Tr Smart, Redarc BCDC) support LiFePO4. Verify before buying.\n- **JL 2.0T eTorque note**: 2.0T eTorque models have a 48V mild hybrid system with a belt-starter generator. The 12V system behavior is different. Consult Redarc or Victron's JL eTorque notes before selecting a charger.\n\n- Victron Orion-Tr Smart 12/12-30A: ~$180 ([Amazon](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07DTXPZYD?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- Redarc BCDC1240D (40A, more capable): ~$280 from retailers\n- 100Ah AGM aux battery (Renogy or VMAXTANKS): $120\u2013$180\n- 100Ah LiFePO4 (Ampere Time, Battle Born): $280\u2013$450\n- 4 AWG wiring kit with lugs and fuses: $30\u2013$60\n- Victron BMV-712 battery monitor: ~$120\n\nA complete entry-level AGM system (DC-DC charger + 100Ah AGM + wiring kit) runs $400\u2013$500 installed yourself. A full LiFePO4 overlanding setup with monitoring runs $800\u2013$1,200."},{"id":"jl-air-filter-replacement","title":"JL Wrangler Air Filter Replacement and Cold Air Intake Options","vehicle":"jl","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":300},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver or no tools for OEM filter"],"skills_required":["none"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["engine","air-filter","intake","maintenance","cold-air-intake"],"summary":"The JL OEM air filter swaps in under 10 minutes with no tools. Replace every 15,000\u201330,000 miles for street use; inspect every oil change if you wheel in dust. Cold air intakes add a real intake note but measurable power gains are minimal on a naturally aspirated 3.6L.","description":"# JL Wrangler Air Filter Replacement and Cold Air Intake Options\n\nThe 3.6L Pentastar breathes through a paper element housed in the factory airbox. Replacing it is the fastest maintenance task on the JL \u2014 no tools required, no coolant spills, nothing to torque. The question worth spending time on is whether to upgrade to an aftermarket intake, and the honest answer is: it depends on what you actually want.\n\n**For OEM filter swap:**\n- Replacement filter (Mopar 68402775AA, Fram CA12166, or K&N 33-5050)\n- No tools required\n\n**For cold air intake installation:**\n- The intake kit (Volant, S&B, K&N Typhoon, Banks Ram-Air)\n- 8mm and 10mm socket\n- Screwdriver\n- 30\u201390 minutes depending on kit complexity\n\n1. Open the hood. The airbox is on the driver's side, forward of the firewall.\n2. The airbox lid is held by two latches \u2014 squeeze and flip them up.\n3. Lift the lid. The filter sits inside the box, rectangular, with the open side facing the lid.\n4. Pull the old filter out. Note the orientation of the folded end \u2014 the new filter goes in the same way.\n5. Drop the new filter in. Close the lid, re-engage the latches. Done.\n\n**Interval:** Every 15,000 miles under normal conditions. Dusty trail use means inspecting every oil change \u2014 a visibly brown, clogged filter costs you throttle response and fuel economy. Paper filters cannot be cleaned; replace them.\n\n**Does it make power on a naturally aspirated 3.6L?**\n\nHonestly: not much. Dyno results on the factory JL intake show it flows adequately for street and moderate trail use. Most aftermarket intakes gain 3\u20137 rear-wheel horsepower \u2014 real but not transformational. The larger benefit is sound: aftermarket intakes produce a deeper induction roar under hard acceleration that a lot of JL owners want.\n\n**What the intakes actually offer:**\n- Better filtration capacity (K&N oiled filters flow more and can be cleaned)\n- More induction sound\n- Easier access to the filter for inspection\n- Some kits place the filter higher (away from water ingestion risk in crossings)\n\n**Popular options:**\n\n| Kit | Price | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| S&B Cold Air Intake | $280\u2013$320 | Dry or oiled filter; solid fitment; good water ingestion protection |\n| K&N Typhoon | $230\u2013$270 | Oiled filter; noticeable intake sound; proven brand |\n| Volant Cold Air Intake | $260\u2013$310 | Closed-box design limits heat soak |\n| Banks Ram-Air | $320\u2013$380 | High-flow, well-made; overkill for most trail builds |\n\n**Water crossings**: All of these intakes position the filter near or in the same location as the factory airbox. If you run deep water crossings, a snorkel is the right fix \u2014 not an air intake. Sucking water through any intake at speed hydrolocks the engine. This is not recoverable.\n\n- **Oiled vs. dry filters**: K&N oiled filters require re-oiling after cleaning with their kit. Over-oiling an oiled filter can foul the MAF sensor. Follow the directions \u2014 use a light coat of oil, let it wick for 20 minutes before installing.\n- **Intake tube connections**: If you install an aftermarket intake, verify all connections are tight before driving. An air leak between the airbox and throttle body causes rough idle, lean running, and check engine codes (P0171, P0174).\n- **Factory airbox re-use**: If you decide aftermarket intakes aren't worth it, the factory airbox is a legitimately good design. Clean it out with a rag, replace the filter, and move on.\n\n- OEM replacement filter (Fram or Mopar): $18\u2013$28\n- K&N drop-in replacement (reusable): $50\u2013$65 (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07BHCLM5C?tag=trailmanual0b-20)\n- S&B cold air intake: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07KX5WGNH?tag=trailmanual0b-20\n\nFor most JL owners on a budget, the drop-in K&N filter is the best upgrade \u2014 same money as two OEM filters, and it's cleanable and reusable for the life of the Jeep."},{"id":"jl-spark-plug-replacement","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 JL Wrangler 3.6L Pentastar and 2.0T","vehicle":"jl","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":100},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket","torque wrench","extension set","ratchet","compressed air or vacuum"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["engine","spark-plugs","maintenance","ignition","tune-up"],"summary":"The 3.6L Pentastar spark plugs are due at 30,000 miles \u2014 Mopar specifies NGK plugs and a specific torque. The 2.0T uses iridium plugs rated to 60,000 miles. Both are DIY-accessible with a quality socket set and about 90 minutes.","description":"# Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 JL Wrangler 3.6L Pentastar and 2.0T\n\nJeep recommends 30,000-mile spark plug intervals on the 3.6L Pentastar. The 2.0T eTorque is rated to 60,000 miles on its iridium plugs. Real-world experience in the Jeep community suggests the 30,000-mile interval holds up \u2014 the Pentastar is sensitive to worn plugs in a way the 2.0T is less so.\n\nBoth engines are accessible with standard tools. The 3.6L's plugs are split across both valve covers \u2014 three per side, accessed from above. The 2.0T has four in a row across the top of the engine and is slightly more accessible.\n\n**3.6L Pentastar:**\n- NGK Iridium IX BKR6EIX-11 (OEM spec) \u2014 6 plugs\n- 5/8\" spark plug socket with rubber insert (18mm)\n- 3\" and 6\" extensions\n- Torque wrench (set to 13 ft-lb / 156 in-lb)\n- Dielectric grease (apply to boot only, not threads)\n- Compressed air or vacuum for debris in plug wells\n\n**2.0T Turbo:**\n- NGK Laser Iridium ILFR6B-11 (OEM spec) \u2014 4 plugs\n- 5/8\" spark plug socket\n- Torque wrench (set to 20 ft-lb)\n- Same toolset as above\n\n**3.6L (both banks, same process)**\n1. Let the engine cool completely. Remove the engine cover (if equipped) by pulling straight up \u2014 it clips on.\n2. Disconnect the ignition coil connector on each cylinder before removing the coil. The coil is held by a single 10mm bolt.\n3. Remove the coil. Blow or vacuum any debris out of the plug well before breaking the plug loose \u2014 debris in the combustion chamber is a bad day.\n4. Use the plug socket with an extension to loosen the plug \u2014 counterclockwise. It will be 18\u201320mm deep in the well.\n5. Remove the old plug. Inspect: the electrode gap should be 0.044\" new; worn plugs will show a wider gap, rounded electrode, or carbon deposits.\n6. Check the new plug gap (pre-gapped from NGK but verify): 0.044\" for the Pentastar.\n7. Thread the new plug in by hand until snug \u2014 use a rubber hose on the plug body to avoid cross-threading. Torque to 13 ft-lb.\n8. Reinstall the coil and torque the coil bolt to 89 in-lb (a little under 8 ft-lb).\n9. Reconnect the coil connector \u2014 it will click.\n10. Repeat for remaining 5 cylinders.\n\n**2.0T (4 plugs in a row)**\n1. Same prep: engine cool, remove coils (10mm bolts), clean plug wells.\n2. Plug gap for the 2.0T: 0.028\" \u2014 verify each plug before install.\n3. Torque to 20 ft-lb (higher than the Pentastar \u2014 the 2.0T's aluminum head spec is different).\n4. Reinstall coils to 89 in-lb.\n\n- **Cross-threading**: The JL's aluminum heads do not forgive cross-threaded plugs. Thread by hand first. If you feel resistance before the plug is fully seated, back out and try again.\n- **Anti-seize on the threads**: Mopar does not spec anti-seize on these plugs. NGK iridium plugs ship with a zinc coating that acts similarly. Do not add anti-seize \u2014 it changes the torque-tension relationship and can lead to overtorquing.\n- **Dielectric grease goes on the boot only**: A small amount on the inside of the coil boot prevents it from seizing to the plug. Do not put it on the plug threads or electrode.\n- **Debris in the wells**: Dirt in a combustion chamber causes scoring. Blow out the wells before loosening any plug. This step matters more on a dusty off-road rig than a clean street car.\n- **Misfires after the swap**: Clear any stored codes with an OBD2 scanner after the job. New plugs on a high-mileage engine sometimes trigger a misfire initially \u2014 let it run a full heat cycle before diagnosing further.\n\n- NGK BKR6EIX-11 (3.6L, 6-pack): $40\u2013$55 ([Amazon NGK set](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000HEPQJ2?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- NGK ILFR6B-11 (2.0T, 4-pack): $35\u2013$50\n- Mopar OEM plugs from the dealer: $10\u2013$14 each (same NGK spec with Mopar branding)\n- Dielectric grease (tube): $5\u2013$8\n\nLabor at a shop runs $150\u2013$250 depending on location. This is a straightforward DIY with a torque wrench \u2014 the only part that slows most people down is the rear bank plugs on the 3.6L, which require a 6\" extension and some patience."},{"id":"jl-fuel-injector-cleaning","title":"JL Wrangler Fuel Injector Cleaning and Maintenance","vehicle":"jl","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":10,"high":80},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner (optional)","basic hand tools for coil pack removal if running off-car cleaning"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["fuel","injectors","maintenance","fuel-system","pentastar"],"summary":"Most JL injectors don't need aggressive cleaning before 75,000 miles if you use Top Tier fuel. When symptoms appear \u2014 rough idle, slight misfires, marginal fuel economy \u2014 a quality fuel system cleaner run through the tank is the first step. Off-car ultrasonic cleaning is worth it at 100,000+ miles.","description":"# JL Wrangler Fuel Injector Cleaning and Maintenance\n\nThe 3.6L Pentastar is a direct-injection engine, which means fuel injectors spray directly into the combustion chamber. This is efficient but produces a specific maintenance issue: carbon buildup on the back of the intake valves. The injectors themselves typically don't foul quickly on a well-maintained Pentastar, but they do benefit from periodic attention, especially in high-mileage engines.\n\n**Symptoms that suggest injector attention:**\n- Rough idle, especially when cold\n- Slight hesitation under light throttle\n- Marginal drop in fuel economy (5\u201310% below baseline)\n- P0300\u2013P0306 misfire codes without an obvious ignition cause\n\n**For fuel system cleaner (in-tank method):**\n- Techron Concentrate Plus or BG 44K cleaner ($10\u2013$50)\n- Full tank of premium fuel (87 octane minimum)\n\n**For intake valve cleaning (GDI carbon issue):**\n- Spray-in intake cleaning kit (CRC GDI IVD Intake Valve & Turbo Cleaner or Mopar ACI)\n- Vacuum line access location (varies by year)\n- Basic hand tools to access vacuum lines\n\n**For off-car injector cleaning (professional or DIY):**\n- Ultrasonic injector cleaning service ($20\u2013$30 per injector at a shop)\n- Injector removal requires intake manifold access \u2014 a longer job\n\n### In-tank fuel system cleaner (first step, low risk)\n\n1. Run the fuel level to under \u00bc tank.\n2. Pour the full bottle of Techron Concentrate Plus into the tank before filling up with premium fuel.\n3. Drive normally for the full tank. Highway miles at steady throttle are more effective than short city trips.\n4. Repeat once per year as preventive maintenance. BG 44K is the stronger option for symptoms \u2014 use it if Techron doesn't resolve rough idle after one tank.\n\n**What this does:** Techron's detergent package dissolves light deposits in the injectors, fuel rail, and combustion chambers. It does not clean intake valves \u2014 those are upstream of the injectors in a GDI engine.\n\n### Intake valve cleaning (GDI-specific)\n\nDirect injection engines build carbon on the back of intake valves because fuel is never sprayed across them to wash them clean. The 3.6L is no exception. By 60,000\u201380,000 miles, valves accumulate enough carbon to affect flow and cause rough cold starts.\n\n**Spray-in method (partial cleaning):**\n1. Locate the PCV vacuum line on the intake manifold. On the JL 3.6L, this is typically on the rear of the intake manifold near the firewall.\n2. Disconnect the vacuum line and insert the spray tube from CRC GDI IVD as directed.\n3. With the engine warm and idling, spray in short bursts per the product instructions. The solvent enters through the vacuum circuit and contacts the intake valves.\n4. Rev the engine gently to help distribute the solvent. Expect rough running and some white smoke from the exhaust \u2014 this is normal.\n\n**Walnut blasting (thorough):**\nFor significant carbon buildup, the definitive fix is walnut blasting \u2014 removing the intake manifold and blasting the valves with crushed walnut shells via a vacuum attachment. Dealers and specialized shops do this for $200\u2013$400. At-home walnut blasting kits exist but require a shop vac with good filtration and some patience.\n\n**When to do it:** Inspect at 60,000 miles. If your cold starts are consistently rough and Techron hasn't helped, walnut blasting is the correct answer.\n\n### Off-car injector cleaning\n\nIf misfire codes or rough idle persist after intake valve cleaning and in-tank treatment, the injectors themselves may need service. This requires removing them:\n\n1. Relieve fuel pressure \u2014 pull the fuel pump fuse, start the engine, and let it stall. Crank once more to bleed residual pressure.\n2. Disconnect the fuel rail. On the 3.6L this is accessed from the top of the engine after removing the intake manifold or coil packs (depending on year).\n3. Remove injectors and send to a cleaning service, or use a bench test kit if you have one.\n\nMost shops charge $20\u2013$30 per injector for ultrasonic cleaning and flow testing. A set of six runs $120\u2013$180 \u2014 cheaper than replacement if they're within spec.\n\n- **GDI valve buildup vs. injector fouling**: Most symptoms blamed on injectors on the JL 3.6L are actually carbon buildup on intake valves. Address that first before pulling injectors.\n- **Injector O-rings**: If you remove injectors, replace the O-rings. They cost under $15 for the set and a leaking injector O-ring causes fuel smell and potential fire risk.\n- **Fuel quality**: The Pentastar runs on 87 octane but Top Tier fuel (higher detergent content) reduces intake valve deposits. Using a gas station with Top Tier certification makes a measurable difference at high mileage.\n\n- Techron Concentrate Plus (12 oz): $10\u2013$15 (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000BUECI4?tag=trailmanual0b-20)\n- BG 44K fuel system cleaner: $25\u2013$35\n- CRC GDI IVD intake cleaner: $15\u2013$20 (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B007ZHKP8G?tag=trailmanual0b-20)\n- Walnut blasting service: $200\u2013$400 at a dealer or import shop\n- Injector O-ring set: $12\u2013$18\n\nPrevention is cheaper than treatment. Top Tier fuel and an annual Techron treatment push intake valve maintenance out past 80,000 miles for most driving styles."},{"id":"jl-fuel-system-inspection","title":"Fuel System Inspection \u2014 JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"fuel","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":80},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner","flashlight","fuel pressure gauge (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic diagnostics"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["fuel","fuel-system","inspection","maintenance","diagnostic"],"summary":"The JL 3.6L and 2.0T both use returnless direct-injection fuel systems with no serviceable inline fuel filter. Inspection focuses on the pump, pressure sensor, injectors, and evap system. Fuel pump replacement is the most common fuel system job on higher-mileage JLs.","description":"# Fuel System Inspection \u2014 JL Wrangler\n\nThe JL does not have a serviceable inline fuel filter \u2014 the filter is integrated into the pump assembly inside the tank. There is no routine filter replacement interval. Fuel system maintenance means inspection, monitoring for symptoms, and replacing the pump assembly when it fails.\n\nThe 3.6L runs a port injection system combined with direct injection (SPDE \u2014 port injection for cold start, direct injection at operating temp). The 2.0T is direct injection only. Both systems run high fuel rail pressure (2,175 PSI at the high-pressure pump on the 2.0T) and are sensitive to fuel quality.\n\n- OBD2 scanner (live data capability \u2014 not just code reading)\n- Flashlight or borescope for visual inspection\n- Fuel pressure gauge (optional \u2014 for confirming low pressure codes)\n\n**Step 1: Pull codes and live data**\nConnect your OBD2 scanner and read stored codes. Relevant fuel codes for the JL:\n- P0087 \u2014 Fuel rail pressure too low (the most common fuel pump warning)\n- P0191 \u2014 Fuel rail pressure sensor performance\n- P0087 + P0016 together \u2014 possible crank/cam sensor issue masquerading as fuel problem; check both\n- P2291 (2.0T) \u2014 Fuel injection quantity too low\n\nCheck live data for fuel rail pressure at idle. 3.6L should idle at 56\u201366 PSI on the low-pressure side. If it's reading 40 PSI or below, the pump is starting to fail.\n\n**Step 2: Visual inspection of the fuel system**\n1. With the engine off and key out, inspect:\n   - Fuel filler neck and cap for cracking or torn seal. A failed cap sets P0456 (evap small leak).\n   - Fuel lines running along the frame rail \u2014 look for chafing, cracking, or road damage.\n   - Evap canister (mounted near the rear axle on most JL trims) \u2014 check for physical damage.\n2. Smell for fuel in the cabin or near the rear cargo area with the tailgate down. Fuel smell without visible leak may indicate a loose in-tank connection.\n\n**Step 3: Monitor for pump symptoms**\nThe JL fuel pump is an in-tank unit accessed by removing the rear seat and a floor panel. Signs it's trending toward failure:\n- Long crank times before start (especially cold or hot)\n- Stumble or hesitation at 60+ MPH under load\n- P0087 appearing intermittently under heavy throttle (hills, towing, wheeling)\n\n**Step 4: Fuel injector health check**\nDirect injection injectors on the 2.0T (and the DI side of the 3.6L SPDE system) accumulate carbon deposits on the intake valves because fuel never washes them. At 60,000\u201380,000 miles, a walnut shell blast of the intake ports is worth considering. Symptoms of injector fouling: rough idle, poor cold-start fuel trims (check STFT/LTFT in live data \u2014 should be within \u00b15%).\n\n**Fuel pump assembly (when replacement is needed):**\n- OEM Mopar pump assembly: $280\u2013$350 (includes filter)\n- Delphi FG1215 (compatible aftermarket): $150\u2013$220\n- Labor: 2\u20133 hours; rear seat removal required\n\n**Fuel cap (if leaking):**\n- Mopar OEM cap: $20\u2013$30\n- Stant 10834 (compatible): $12\u2013$18\n\n- **Ethanol in fuel**: E85 and high-ethanol blends are not JL-approved unless you have a flex-fuel model (rare in JL trim). Running high-ethanol blends long-term degrades fuel system seals.\n- **Off-road fuel pickup**: Hard off-camber situations or steep descents can temporarily starve the fuel pickup \u2014 this is normal and does not indicate pump failure. Persistent issues on trail suggest the pump sock is partially clogged.\n- **Low fuel = thermal damage**: Running consistently below 1/4 tank on direct-injection vehicles stresses the pump \u2014 the fuel cools it. Keep it above 1/4 for daily driving; above 1/2 for extended trail runs.\n\n- OBD2 scanner with live data (Autel AL319 or BlueDriver): $30\u2013$120\n- Fuel pressure gauge kit: $25\u2013$45\n- Mopar fuel pump assembly: $280\u2013$350 at the dealer\n- Delphi FG1215: $150\u2013$220 from RockAuto or Amazon ([link](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000C28MUS?tag=trailmanual0b-20))"},{"id":"jl-grab-handle-upgrade","title":"JL Wrangler Grab Handle Upgrade and Installation","vehicle":"jl","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":200},"tools_required":["T40 Torx socket","ratchet","trim panel tool","1/2\" socket (for some mounts)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["interior","grab-handles","trail","passenger","rollbar"],"summary":"Aftermarket grab handles bolt to the JL roll bar using the factory mounting holes. The stock handles are functional but thin \u2014 paracord and aluminum tube versions provide a more substantial grip for passengers on rough trails.","description":"# JL Wrangler Grab Handle Upgrade and Installation\n\nThe factory JL grab handles do the job, but they're designed around liability, not grip. The plastic housing can flex under load, and the cord diameter is narrow enough that a tight trail section with a white-knuckle passenger isn't comfortable. Aftermarket handles \u2014 most commonly paracord or solid aluminum tube versions \u2014 are a low-cost fix that makes a real difference for anyone riding in the back on technical terrain.\n\n- Replacement grab handles (set of 4 or just the positions you care about)\n- T40 Torx socket and ratchet\n- Trim panel tool (for removing the factory handles cleanly)\n- Thread-locker (optional but recommended for rollbar mounts)\n\n**Handle options:**\n\n| Type | Price (set of 4) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Paracord woven (Savvy, Rugged Ridge) | $35\u2013$70 | Lightweight, comfortable grip, wide color selection |\n| Solid aluminum tube | $60\u2013$120 | Most durable, zero flex, heavier |\n| Tubular nylon strap | $30\u2013$50 | Budget option, functional |\n| Mopar OEM replacement | $50\u2013$80 | Same as factory, useful if one breaks |\n\n**Remove factory handles**\n1. The factory grab handles bolt to the roll bar via T40 Torx fasteners \u2014 typically two bolts per handle.\n2. On the front passenger A-pillar handle, there may be a plastic shroud that pulls off first before accessing the bolts. Use a trim panel tool at the seam to avoid scratching the pillar.\n3. Unthread both T40 bolts. The factory handle and its rubber mounting pads will come off as a unit.\n4. Clean the roll bar contact area \u2014 the factory pads leave residue.\n\n**Install new handles**\n5. Position the new handle over the factory mounting holes. Most aftermarket handles use the same hole pattern.\n6. Thread in the mounting hardware included with the kit. Hand-tighten first to confirm alignment.\n7. Apply a drop of medium-strength thread-locker to each fastener if the manufacturer doesn't specify a thread treatment. Roll bar vibration on rough trails backs out dry fasteners.\n8. Torque to manufacturer spec, or approximately 15\u201318 ft-lbs for most M8-threaded roll bar mounts.\n\n**Front vs. rear handles**\nThe front A-pillar handles serve as door grab handles as well as trail handles \u2014 they see the most use. The rear roll bar handles are primarily for passengers bracing on trail. If budget is limited, prioritize the rear handles; front passengers have the door handle and dash.\n\n- **Thread-locker**: This step matters. A grab handle that spins freely on a rough trail section is useless. Apply and give it a cure time before wheeling.\n- **Hardware compatibility**: Most kits include hardware sized for the factory bolt pattern. If they don't fit, verify the bolt diameter (typically M8) and pick up the right fasteners \u2014 don't use shorter hardware than the factory length.\n- **Paracord maintenance**: Woven paracord handles absorb trail dust and mud. They're machine washable on a gentle cycle in a mesh bag. Replace if the cord shows significant fraying at the anchor points.\n- **Weight limit**: Most aftermarket handles are rated 300\u2013500 lbs. Don't use them as climbing holds to haul into the Jeep \u2014 they're bracing handles, not structural mounting points.\n\n- Savvy Off-Road paracord handles (set of 4): $45\u2013$55 (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07ZQXMHDB?tag=trailmanual0b-20)\n- Rugged Ridge grab handle set: $40\u2013$60\n- Solid aluminum tube handles (Rugged Ridge, Poison Spyder): $80\u2013$120\n\nThe paracord versions are the best value. They're lighter than aluminum, look better than stock, and the grip is noticeably more comfortable for passengers bracing on ledgy trails."},{"id":"jl-seat-cover-installation","title":"Seat Cover Selection and Installation \u2014 JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":450},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver","trim panel tool","hog ring pliers (for OEM-fit covers)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["interior","seat-covers","upholstery","off-road","waterproof"],"summary":"For a JL that sees trail use, waterproof neoprene covers (Rough Country, Coverking) are the most functional choice at $120\u2013$200. Exact-fit seat covers install in 60\u201390 minutes; universal-fit covers are faster but look cheaper. Side airbag compatibility is not optional \u2014 verify before buying.","description":"# Seat Cover Selection and Installation \u2014 JL Wrangler\n\nJL seat covers divide into two real categories: exact-fit (custom-cut for your specific seat contour, installs over the OEM foam, uses the factory seat attachment points) and universal-fit (slips over the seat, uses elastic and straps, fits most bucket seats). The price gap is real, and so is the quality gap.\n\nFor a Jeep that goes off-road, the three properties that matter: waterproof/water-resistant (mud and rain happen), durable enough for gear contact (straps, tools, wet gear), and side-airbag compatible (the JL has seat-mounted side airbags in most trims \u2014 non-compatible covers are a safety hazard).\n\n- Seat covers (see recommendations below)\n- Flathead screwdriver or trim panel tool\n- Hog ring pliers (for exact-fit covers that attach to the foam hooks \u2014 not needed for all covers)\n- 30\u201345 minutes per seat\n\n**Neoprene (best for wet/trail use):**\n- Coverking Neoprene Custom Fit: $200\u2013$250 per row, exact-fit, side-airbag compatible seam placement, full coverage including headrest. Best balance of fit and durability.\n- Rough Country Neoprene: $120\u2013$160 per row, exact-fit for JL, solid construction. Slightly stiffer material than Coverking.\n- Smittybilt Gear Seat Cover: $70\u2013$90 per seat (bucket), universal-fit, MOLLE-compatible exterior (actually useful for trail snacks, maps, small gear). Not waterproof but water-resistant.\n\n**Leather alternative (for street-focus builds):**\n- Katzkin leather replacement upholstery: $500\u2013$800 per vehicle, professional installation required. Genuine leather replacement, not a cover. Overkill for trail use.\n\n**Budget options:**\n- BDK universal waterproof covers: $25\u2013$40, fit is mediocre, look cheap. Fine for a work truck or beater Jeep; not worth it on a build you're investing in.\n\n**Exact-fit neoprene covers (Coverking or Rough Country)**\n\nFront seats:\n1. Slide the front seat fully forward. Remove the headrest \u2014 pull up while pressing the release buttons on each post.\n2. Slide the headrest cover over the headrest foam. It will be snug \u2014 work it from the center outward.\n3. Place the seat back cover over the seatback from the top. Work it down, pulling the cover's bottom edge forward and underneath. The cover hooks into the seat foam channels using hog rings or tension clips (varies by brand).\n4. Place the seat cushion cover over the cushion. Tuck the perimeter edge under the seat cushion pan. Many exact-fit covers route a strap beneath the seat and attach with a clip \u2014 route it before tucking the edges.\n5. Reinstall the headrest into its adjusted cover.\n\nRear seats (JL has folding rear bench or 40/20/40 split):\n6. Fold the seat down if present. Covers typically come in sections \u2014 a cushion section, an upper seatback section, and sometimes a center armrest section.\n7. Work the cover onto the seatback from the top, routing all attachment straps. Tuck edges under the seat frame.\n8. Install the cushion cover, route under-seat straps.\n\n**Verify headrest position**: After re-installation, confirm the headrests move and lock correctly \u2014 a cover that binds the headrest adjuster is an installation error.\n\n- **Side airbag clearance**: The JL's seat-mounted side curtain airbags are in the outer seatback bolster. Covers must have deployment seams at the OEM airbag location or leave the area unobstructed. Coverking and Rough Country mark this explicitly on their JL covers. Generic covers that fully wrap the outer bolster are dangerous.\n- **Seat heater compatibility**: If your JL has heated seats (available on Sahara, Rubicon, High Altitude), make sure the cover material is thin enough to conduct heat. Thick neoprene reduces effectiveness noticeably.\n- **Seat sensor interference**: The JL has occupancy sensors under the passenger seat cushion. Covers that press on the seat cushion differently than the factory foam can trigger airbag warning lights in rare cases. If a warning appears after installation, the cover is likely too tight over the sensor area.\n\n- Coverking Neoprene (full vehicle kit): $350\u2013$450 ([Coverking.com](https://coverking.com) or Amazon)\n- Rough Country neoprene front row: ~$130 ([Amazon](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07L8K8G88?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- Smittybilt Gear bucket covers: ~$80 per seat\n- Hog ring pliers (if needed): $12\u2013$20\n\nInstallation labor at an upholstery shop: $60\u2013$120 if you supply the covers. Worth it if you want a factory-tight fit on an exact-fit cover."},{"id":"jl-air-filter-jl","title":"Engine Air Filter Replacement \u2014 3.6L","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":18,"high":35},"tools_required":["flathead screwdriver","hands"],"skills_required":["none"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["fluids-filters","maintenance","preventive"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM Air Filter (3.6L Pentastar)","part_number":"68257030AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"K&N Drop-In Performance Filter","part_number":"33-5079","vendor":"K&N","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Mopar Performance High-Flow Filter","part_number":"P5160245AA","vendor":"Mopar Performance","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar 68257030AA Air Filter \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/mopar/air-filter-3.6l-jeep-wrangler-jl-68257030aa"},{"title":"Jeep Air Filter Change Wrangler JL 3.6L \u2014 CheaperJeeperTV","url":"https://cheaperjeepertv.com/jeep-air-filter-change/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Pop four clips, swap the filter, done. The JL 3.6L Pentastar uses Mopar 68257030AA \u2014 replace it every 30,000 miles or sooner if you run dusty trails.","description":"The JL's airbox sits at the front passenger corner of the engine bay with four metal spring clips holding the lid down. No tools required for a stock replacement \u2014 though a flathead screwdriver helps pry the clips if they've never been opened. The whole job runs about 10 minutes and the OEM Mopar filter is $20\u201325 from any dealer or Quadratec. Aftermarket drop-ins from K&N or aFe are double the price; the genuine Mopar paper element flows plenty for a stock 3.6L and traps finer dust than oiled cotton-gauze filters.\n\nOpen the hood and look at the front passenger side. The airbox is the big black plastic shroud with the intake snorkel running up to the cowl. Run a finger along the seam between the lid and the lower box \u2014 you'll feel the four spring clips, two front and two rear. Pop each clip outward with your thumb or a screwdriver tip. Lift the lid up and toward the front of the Jeep; the intake tube on the back stays connected, the lid just hinges.\n\nThe old filter pulls straight up. Note its orientation \u2014 the rubber seal sits on top, the pleats face down. Wipe out any leaves or dirt in the lower airbox with a shop rag before dropping the new filter in. Don't blow it out with compressed air unless you've removed the MAF sensor tube \u2014 debris can fire backward through the system and contaminate the mass airflow sensor.\n\nDrop the new filter in with the seal facing up and pleats facing down. Press the lid back down \u2014 make sure the front edge tucks under its lip \u2014 and snap the four clips back into place. Pull on the lid once to confirm it's seated. If you feel air whistle through the seal when the engine runs, the lid isn't fully clipped.\n\nMost JL owners running pavement-only can stretch this to 40,000 miles. Phoenix dust, Moab trips, or sand washes drop the interval to 15,000\u201320,000. Hold the old filter up to a shop light; if you can't see light through it, it's overdue.","steps":["Open the hood and locate the airbox at the front passenger corner.","Pop the four spring clips on the airbox lid (thumb or flathead screwdriver).","Lift the lid up and toward the front; the intake tube stays connected.","Lift the old filter straight up out of the box.","Wipe out any debris in the lower airbox with a rag \u2014 do not use compressed air.","Set the new filter in with seal facing up, pleats facing down.","Tuck the front edge of the lid under its lip and press the lid down.","Snap all four spring clips back into place.","Tug the lid to confirm it's seated, close the hood."],"pros":["10-minute job with no tools required","OEM Mopar filter is $20\u201325 and fits perfectly","No risk of over-oiling and contaminating the MAF (a known issue with some oiled cotton filters)"],"cons":["Performance gains from aftermarket filters are mostly marketing on a stock 3.6L","Spring clips can pinch fingers if they snap shut on you","Hot-rodded JLs running tunes may still want a full intake system, not just a drop-in"]},{"id":"jl-battery-replacement","title":"Battery Replacement \u2014 Start-Stop and eTorque Systems","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"electrical","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":450},"tools_required":["10mm socket","13mm socket","ratchet","battery terminal brush","memory saver (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["electrical","battery","start-stop","etorque"],"parts":[{"name":"Main AGM H7 battery (94R, 80Ah, 850 CCA)","part_number":"MTX-94R / H7","vendor":"Interstate / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Mopar OEM eTorque main battery","part_number":"56029720AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Auxiliary AGM battery (group 400, 200 CCA)","part_number":"AUX14 / AUX14-1","vendor":"DieHard / Super Start","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Odyssey AGM94R H7","part_number":"AGM94R","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Main and Aux Battery Size","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/main-battery-aux-battery-size.123643/"},{"title":"2021 3.6 eTorque OEM Battery Specs","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/2021-3-6-etorque-oem-battery-specs.122345/"},{"title":"Wrangler Forum \u2014 Auxiliary Battery AGM Discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/auxiliary-battery-is-it-an-agm-battery.2442093/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL uses an AGM H7 main battery \u2014 replace it with AGM, not a flooded lead-acid, or the start-stop system will fail and the BCM will log faults.","description":"The 2018+ Wrangler JL ships with an AGM H7 (BCI group 94R) main battery \u2014 typically 80 Ah and 850 CCA. eTorque trims (2.0L turbo and 3.6L with the 48V mild hybrid) add a small 12V AUX14 auxiliary AGM battery rated around 200 CCA, plus the separate 48V lithium-ion pack that handles the mild hybrid functions. The 48V pack carries an 8-year warranty and is not a DIY part. The auxiliary 12V AGM and the main H7, however, are both owner-serviceable.\n\nUse AGM only. The JL's body control module is configured for AGM charging voltage and current profiles. Drop in a flooded lead-acid battery and the BCM will overcharge it on highway runs and undercharge it during ESS stop events, which kills the battery in months and throws \"Service Stop/Start System\" warnings. Quality H7 AGM options include Mopar OEM (Interstate MTX-94R/H7), Odyssey AGM94R, Optima YELLOWTOP DH7, and the OEM eTorque part 56029720AA. Expect to pay $250\u2013$350 for a quality H7 AGM.\n\nThe auxiliary battery on start-stop and eTorque trims lives on the passenger side firewall under a small cover. It powers electronics during the ESS engine-off pause so cabin power, infotainment, and HVAC don't drop out. When it fails, ESS stops working and you'll see a service message \u2014 but the truck still runs fine on the main battery alone. Replacement AUX14 batteries run $90\u2013$140.\n\nAfter replacing either battery, the BCM needs to register the new battery so it can manage charging correctly. On non-eTorque JLs the system usually self-relearns within a few drive cycles. On eTorque trims, a Mopar dealer or aftermarket scan tool (Topdon, Autel, wiTECH) is recommended to perform a battery registration. Without registration, the alternator may not properly charge the new battery, shortening its life by half.","steps":["Park on level ground and turn off all accessories. Open the driver door so the BCM stays awake briefly.","Optional: plug a memory saver into the OBD-II port to preserve radio presets and adaptive transmission learning.","Open the hood. Loosen the 10mm negative (black) terminal clamp on the main battery first. Tuck the cable aside.","Loosen the 10mm positive (red) terminal clamp and remove the plastic cover. Tuck the cable aside.","Remove the 13mm battery hold-down bracket bolt at the front base of the battery.","Lift the battery straight up \u2014 it weighs roughly 50 pounds.","Clean the tray and terminals with a battery brush. Inspect for corrosion or moisture damage.","Set the new AGM battery in place with terminals matching the original orientation.","Reinstall the hold-down bracket and torque the 13mm bolt snug \u2014 do not overtighten.","Connect the positive terminal first, then the negative. Tighten the 10mm clamps until snug.","For eTorque or start-stop trims, perform a battery registration with a scan tool that supports BCM coding.","For the auxiliary battery: open the passenger-side firewall cover, disconnect the small terminals (10mm), remove the strap, and swap. Reverse to install."],"pros":["30\u201360 minute job in the driveway","$250 DIY vs. $500+ at the dealer","Restores start-stop function and clears service messages","AGM batteries handle deep-cycle abuse better than flooded"],"cons":["Battery registration may require a scan tool on eTorque trims","AGM costs roughly 2x a flooded H7","The 48V lithium pack on eTorque is dealer-only","AUX14 auxiliary battery is buried behind a firewall cover"]},{"id":"jl-differential-fluid-jl","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Dana 44 Front and Rear","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":45,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm socket","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench","RTV gasket maker"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024","2025-2026"],"tags":["axles","gear-oil","service-interval","trac-lok"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil (1 quart)","part_number":"68218057AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Mopar Trac-Lok friction modifier (4 oz)","part_number":"04318060AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Mopar differential cover gasket / RTV","part_number":"68003557AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Permatex Ultra Black RTV","part_number":"82180","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Motobilt \u2014 Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Rubicon Rear Differential Gear Oil Capacity Surprise","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/rubicon-rear-differential-gear-oil-capacity-surprise.16811/"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 Differential Fluid Discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/diff-fluid-wrangler-jl.97387/page-3"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL Dana 44 axles take less fluid than the JK they replaced \u2014 about 1.5 quarts per axle \u2014 and Mopar specifies 75W-85 GL-5 gear oil, with 4 oz of friction modifier added to any Trac-Lok differential.","description":"The JL inherited the Dana 44 designation but the axles themselves are new \u2014 they're called AdvanTEK and they hold noticeably less fluid than the JK Dana 44. Plan on 1.4 to 1.6 quarts per axle (front and rear), not the 2.3 quarts you'd put in a JK. Overfilling is a real risk because the fill plug is positioned higher than the actual fill line on some axle covers \u2014 fluid coming out of the hole doesn't always mean the diff is full to spec. Trust the manual capacity and measure what goes in.\n\nMopar's recommended fluid is 75W-85 GL-5 hypoid gear oil (part #68218057AA). The owner's manual for some JL configurations also lists 75W-140 for severe trailer-towing use. For 99% of owners running street and trail, 75W-85 is correct. Any name-brand GL-5 in that viscosity works \u2014 Mobil 1 Synthetic 75W-90 is a common and acceptable substitute, despite being a half-step heavier.\n\nTrac-Lok limited-slip rear axles (standard on Sahara, optional on Sport S) require friction modifier. The Mopar additive is part #04318060AC, 4 ounces per axle. Without it, the clutches will chatter on slow corners \u2014 you'll hear it as a series of clunks from the rear end when turning. Lockers (Rubicon front and rear) do not need friction modifier.\n\nThe first fluid change matters most. Factory fill carries break-in metal from the ring and pinion. Mopar's service interval is 67,500 miles for normal use, 30,000 for severe service \u2014 but pulling the cover at 5,000\u20137,500 miles after delivery and replacing the fluid is cheap insurance. After the first change, every 30,000 miles is reasonable; sooner if you submerge the axles or wheel in mud often.\n\nThere is no drain plug on the AdvanTEK covers \u2014 you remove the cover to drain. That makes this a bigger job than it sounds, because you'll need to clean the cover, the magnet, and the housing mating surface, then re-seal with RTV. Bolts are 13mm, torque to 30 ft-lb in a cross pattern. Wait at least an hour for the RTV to skin before adding fluid.","steps":["Park on level ground. Warm the axles by driving a few miles \u2014 warm fluid drains cleaner.","Place a wide drain pan under the diff cover. Remove the fill plug FIRST (1/2\" ratchet drive). If it won't break loose, stop \u2014 you don't want to drain a diff you can't refill.","Crack the bottom cover bolts, then loosen the upper bolts. Leave the top two snug and let the cover hang open at the bottom to drain.","Once drained, remove all bolts and pull the cover. Inspect the magnet \u2014 fine fuzz is normal, chunks or chips are not.","Clean the cover, magnet, and housing mating surface with brake cleaner. Both surfaces must be bare metal and dry.","Apply a 1/8\" bead of Ultra Black RTV around the cover, encircling each bolt hole. Install cover and torque bolts to 30 ft-lb in a cross pattern.","Wait one hour for RTV to set.","Use a fluid pump to add 1.5 quarts of 75W-85 GL-5 through the fill hole. Add 4 oz friction modifier first if Trac-Lok.","Reinstall fill plug, torque to 25 ft-lb. Clean any drips."],"pros":["Cheap insurance against ring-and-pinion wear","Catches early bearing failure (metal on magnet)","30,000-mile interval is reasonable for a trail-driven JL","Lockers don't need friction modifier \u2014 simpler than older Trac-Lok-only platforms"],"cons":["No drain plug \u2014 full cover removal required","RTV cure time blocks the job for an hour","Fill plug position can fool you into overfilling \u2014 measure what goes in","Trac-Lok chatter is the symptom of missed friction modifier, and the fix is another fluid change"]},{"id":"jl-oil-change-jl","title":"Oil Change \u2014 Pentastar 3.6L and eTorque","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":95},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter cap socket (76mm, 14-flute)","drain pan (6+ quart)","torque wrench","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-present"],"tags":["oil-change","pentastar","3.6l","etorque","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"0W-20 full synthetic oil (5\u20136 quarts depending on year)","part_number":"MS-12633","vendor":"Mopar / API SP rated equivalent","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (cartridge)","part_number":"68191349AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Drain plug crush washer","part_number":"6104787AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"2018+ JL Wrangler owner's manual \u2014 3.6L oil spec 0W-20","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/manuals.html"},{"title":"JL 3.6L Pentastar oil capacity (5 qt non-eTorque, 5.5 qt eTorque)","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com"},{"title":"Pentastar cartridge filter housing torque 25 Nm","url":"https://www.moparrepairconnect.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The JL Pentastar moved from 5W-20 (JK) to 0W-20 full synthetic. Use a cartridge filter \u2014 there's no spin-on. Drain plug torques to 20 ft-lb, filter cap to 18 ft-lb. eTorque models hold half a quart more oil than non-eTorque.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar in the JL is the same basic engine that was in the late JK, but FCA changed the oil spec from 5W-20 to 0W-20 for fuel economy. Either viscosity will run the engine; the 0W-20 reduces cold-start friction and helps the start-stop and eTorque systems behave smoothly. Use what the manual calls for and the OBD-II long-term fuel trims will stay where they should.\n\n**Capacity.** 5.0 quarts on non-eTorque (2018+) and 5.5 quarts on eTorque mild-hybrid models with filter change. Always verify with the dipstick \u2014 overfilling causes the same intake-valve-coking issue the Pentastar is already prone to from direct injection.\n\n**Filter.** Cartridge type, same plastic housing on top of the engine as the JK. Mopar 68191349AC is the OEM part. The plastic cap cracks under abuse \u2014 a 76mm 14-flute cap socket is the only correct tool. If a previous shop used channel locks and the cap is gouged, replace it before it splits in service (Mopar 68191344AB, ~$30).\n\n**eTorque caveat.** eTorque adds a 48V belt-driven starter/generator (BSG) on the front of the engine. It doesn't change the oil change procedure, but it does mean the engine may auto-start during the change if you don't disconnect the 48V battery or follow the service mode procedure (turn ignition to RUN, then to OFF, then immediately disconnect 12V negative). Don't skip this on eTorque trucks.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to thin the oil. Shut off.","**eTorque only:** Cycle key to RUN then OFF, then immediately disconnect 12V negative terminal. This prevents auto-start during the change.","Pull the engine cover (friction fit).","Loosen the filter cap one turn to let oil drain into the housing before pulling the drain plug.","Position drain pan under oil pan. Remove 13mm drain plug. Drain 5+ minutes.","Remove filter cap completely. Lift out old cartridge. Wipe housing.","Replace cap O-ring. Lubricate new O-ring with fresh oil. Snap new cartridge into cap. Reinstall, torque to 25 Nm (18 ft-lb).","Install drain plug with new crush washer. Torque to 20 ft-lb.","Add 5 qt (non-eTorque) or 5.5 qt (eTorque) of 0W-20 full synthetic through the cap.","Reconnect 12V if disconnected. Start engine; verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds.","Wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed \u2014 do not overfill."],"pros":["Top-mount filter, no crawling","30\u201360 minute job","Common 0W-20 synthetic at any parts store","Same procedure regardless of door count or transmission"],"cons":["Plastic filter cap requires care","eTorque adds a battery-disconnect step","Wrong viscosity (especially old 5W-30 inventory) will trigger fuel trim drift on long-term monitors","Pentastar still loves to coke intake valves \u2014 frequent oil changes help but don't solve it"]},{"id":"jl-spark-plugs-jl","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 3.6L Pentastar","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":90},"tools_required":["3/8\" ratchet","6-inch extension","5/8\" spark plug socket","torque wrench (in-lb capable)","T20 Torx"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["ignition","preventative"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK Laser Iridium ILKAR7L11 (set of 6)","part_number":"94124","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Mopar OEM spark plug (set of 6)","part_number":"SP149875AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Dielectric grease (small tube)","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":4}],"citations":[{"title":"iFixit \u2014 Spark plug replacement, 2018-2024 JL 3.6L","url":"https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/How+To+Replace+the+Spark+Plugs+In+Your+2018%E2%80%932024+Jeep+Wrangler+JL+(3.6L)/185982"},{"title":"WAYALIFE JL torque specs reference","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/torque-specs-jl-wrangler-reference-guide.52799/"},{"title":"JLwranglerforums \u2014 spark plug change on 3.6 Pentastar","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/spark-plug-change-on-3-6-penstar.122868/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Replace all six iridium plugs every 100,000 miles. Torque to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m) \u2014 the 12mm Pentastar plugs strip if you over-tighten. Plan on an hour in the driveway.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar in the JL uses 12mm spark plugs \u2014 smaller than the 14mm plugs in older Jeep engines. That matters for two reasons. First, the spark plug socket is 5/8\" (16mm), not the 13/16\" that fits the 4.0L. Second, the thinner shell strips its threads at much lower torque, so a regular click-type torque wrench set to \"feel\" is not good enough. Use a wrench that reads in inch-pounds or one that goes down to single-digit foot-pounds.\n\nNGK ILKAR7L11 is the direct factory equivalent and runs about $55 for a set of six. Mopar's branded plug is the same NGK part with a Chrysler box and a markup. There is no benefit to going up a heat range or to a one-step-colder plug on a stock motor \u2014 the OEM iridium plugs are correct for stock and lightly modified Pentastars. Skip the anti-seize; NGK explicitly says not to use it on iridium plugs, and the plated threads do not need it.\n\nAccess on the JL is straightforward. The coil packs sit on top of each cylinder, held by a single 10mm bolt. Remove the bolt, twist the coil to break the rubber boot seal, lift it off, and you are looking at the plug well. Use a 6-inch extension and the 5/8\" plug socket. The plug well is deep \u2014 a magnetic or rubber-insert socket keeps the old plug from dropping into the head when you back it out.\n\nReinstall each new plug by hand first to confirm thread engagement \u2014 never start a plug with a ratchet. Torque to 13 ft-lb (17.5 N\u00b7m). A dab of dielectric grease inside the coil boot helps it pull off cleanly the next time. Reinstall the coil with the 10mm bolt, repeat for the remaining five cylinders. Total job time is about an hour the first time, 30 minutes after that.\n\nSymptoms that mean it is time: rough idle at startup, misfire code (P0301\u2013P0306), or a noticeable loss of low-end response. Most JLs make it to the 100,000-mile interval cleanly. Track-driven or extreme cold-climate Jeeps benefit from inspecting at 75,000.","steps":["Cold engine. Disconnect the negative battery cable (10mm).","Remove the engine cover (pulls straight up off four rubber grommets).","Pick a coil pack. Remove the 10mm coil hold-down bolt.","Unclip the coil electrical connector. Twist and lift the coil out.","Drop the 5/8\" plug socket on a 6-inch extension into the well. Back the plug out counterclockwise.","Verify the gap on the new plug (factory pre-gap is correct \u2014 do not regap iridium).","Hand-thread the new plug. Then torque to 13 ft-lb.","Apply dielectric grease inside the coil boot. Reinstall coil, bolt, and connector.","Repeat for the remaining five cylinders.","Reinstall engine cover, reconnect battery, start, listen for smooth idle."],"pros":["100,000-mile interval \u2014 set-and-forget maintenance","Job runs about $60 in parts vs. $200+ at a shop","Restores throttle response and cold-start smoothness","Direct access \u2014 no manifold removal needed"],"cons":["12mm plugs strip easily if you over-torque","Coil boots can tear if the rubber has aged","Plug socket must be the deep 5/8\" type with a retainer","Skipping torque spec is the #1 way to crack a coil"]},{"id":"jl-transfer-case-fluid","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Change \u2014 MPT/FCA 8HP Considerations","vehicle":"jl","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":70},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","13mm hex bit (fill)","13mm hex bit or square drive (drain)","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2025"],"tags":["transfer-case","fluids","mp3022","atf+4"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 Automatic Transmission Fluid (quart)","part_number":"68218057AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife ATF (multi-vehicle, MS-9602 compatible)","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Fluid transfer pump (quart bottle adapter)","vendor":"Amazon","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Trail Wrangler \u2014 Jeep JL Transfer Case fluid change","url":"https://trailwrangler.com/2021/05/13/jeep-jl-transfer-case-fluid-change-guide/"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Forums \u2014 MP3022C fluid change","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/transfer-case-mp3022c-fluid-change.58861/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Use Mopar ATF+4 (or an MS-9602-rated multi-vehicle ATF) in every JL transfer case \u2014 MP3022 on Rubicon, NV241 on Sport/Sahara, MP1522 on 4xe \u2014 and budget 1.7 to 2.0 quarts for the refill.","description":"The JL's transfer case requires ATF, not gear oil \u2014 this trips up owners coming from XJ or TJ platforms where the t-case ran the same 75W-90 as the axles. The Mopar spec is MS-9602, which is the same fluid family as ATF+4. Any name-brand ATF+4 or a multi-vehicle ATF that lists MS-9602 compatibility (Valvoline MaxLife, Castrol Transmax) will hold up. Mopar's official position is ATF+4 only. The aftermarket position is that MS-9602-compatible fluids work fine; pick your comfort level.\n\nCapacity depends on the case. The MP3022 (Rubicon two-speed with 4LO) shows around 1.7\u20131.85 quarts on a proper drain and refill. The NV241 on Sport/Sahara is similar. The MP1522 found on 4xe takes about 2.0 quarts. Service manuals occasionally underreport \u2014 buy two quarts and bring a third if you have it.\n\nBoth plugs use a 3/8\" square drive or a 13mm hex on most years \u2014 pop them open before you crawl under to verify. Open the fill plug first; same rule as a diff \u2014 if you can't open the fill, don't drain. Position a low-profile pan under the case, open the drain plug, let it run. Inspect what comes out. ATF should look reddish; if it's brown or smells burnt, the case has been working hard and you may want to plan a second drain in 5,000 miles to flush remaining contamination.\n\nReinstall the drain plug at 20 ft-lbs. Pump new ATF into the fill hole until it overflows \u2014 that's the level, do not try to add more. Reinstall the fill plug at 20 ft-lbs. Run the rig through the 2H/4H/N/4L positions a few times in a parking lot to circulate the new fluid through the chain.","steps":["Drive the rig 10 minutes to warm the fluid.","Park level, set the parking brake, chock front tires.","Locate the transfer case behind the transmission, between the front and rear driveshafts.","Loosen the fill plug first to verify it opens.","Place a drain pan, open the drain plug, let drain fully.","Inspect drained fluid color and smell.","Reinstall drain plug at 20 ft-lbs with fresh crush washer if equipped.","Pump fresh ATF+4 (or MS-9602 ATF) into the fill hole until it overflows.","Reinstall the fill plug at 20 ft-lbs.","Cycle through 2H/4H/4L positions to circulate."],"pros":["One of the cheapest scheduled-service items on a JL","Catches water intrusion that ruins chain and bearings","1 hour with the rig on the ground (no lift required)","Fluid is the same as transmission and power steering \u2014 easy to stock"],"cons":["Mopar restricts the official spec to ATF+4, limiting bulk purchase options","Service position is awkward without ramps","Two stripped plug heads (square-drive 3/8) are common \u2014 chase down a fresh tool if yours is rounded"]},{"id":"jl-rooftop-tent","title":"Rooftop Tent and Awning Setup for the JL","vehicle":"jl","category":"overland","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":1100,"high":3500},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","measuring tape","helper for lifting tent assembly"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","hardware installation"],"build_tiers":["overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["overland","rooftop-tent","camping","awning"],"parts":[{"name":"iKamper Skycamp Mini 2.0 Rooftop Tent","vendor":"iKamper","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"ARB Simpson III Hardshell Rooftop Tent","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1699},{"name":"CVT Mt. Rainier Hard Shell Rooftop Tent","vendor":"CVT","price_estimate_usd":1799},{"name":"Tepui Autana 3 Softshell Tent","vendor":"Tepui by Thule","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"ARB 2500 Awning 8.2' x 8.2'","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"iKamper Skycamp Mini Product Page","url":"https://www.ikamper.com/en/products/skycamp-mini-2-0"},{"title":"ARB Simpson III Tent Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/arb-simpson-iii-rooftop-tent"},{"title":"Tepui by Thule Autana Softshell Tent","url":"https://www.thule.com/en-us/us/tents/roof-top-tents/tepui-autana-3-_-901800"}],"alternatives":["jl-rear-bumper"],"summary":"The JL Wrangler's factory roof is rated at 165 lbs static load \u2014 that's not the tent's weight limit, it's the roof's structural limit, and the right rack and mounting setup is what keeps you sleeping soundly instead of lying under rubble.","description":"The 165 lbs static / 150 lbs dynamic rating is the number that governs your tent and rack choices on the JL. Static load is the tent plus two adults lying in it, measured when the vehicle is stationary. Dynamic load is everything minus roughly 10\u201315%, accounting for driving-induced forces. Most hardshell tents fall between 100\u2013140 lbs \u2014 that's the tent shell, the platform, and the mattress. Two adults in the 150\u2013200 lb combined range push the dynamic load limit at highway speeds. The solution is not to avoid the tent \u2014 it's to use a proper rack system designed for the JL's roof structure, which distributes that load across multiple points rather than concentrating it on the factory cross-bar mounts.\n\n**Rack requirement:** The factory JL roof rails are not sufficient for a rooftop tent without additional support. You need a JL-specific roof rack or cross-bar system from Rhino-Rack, Yakima, or Thule \u2014 all of which offer JL-specific fitments that clamp to the factory tracks without drilling. These racks spread the load across the roof structure rather than loading the factory T-slots directly. Budget $300\u2013$600 for a quality rack system before adding the tent.\n\nThe iKamper Skycamp Mini 2.0 at $2,499 is the premium choice and for good reason. The Mini's clamshell hardshell design opens in 20 seconds and provides a sleeping platform for two adults in a compact footprint that's shorter than most full-size RTTs. The 3.5\" high-density foam mattress is genuinely comfortable, and the tent folds down to a low-profile 7\" height on the roof. The Mini also comes in under 110 lbs, which is the most favorable weight-to-roof-rating ratio of the premium options.\n\nThe ARB Simpson III at $1,699 is the value hardshell choice with proven trail credibility. ARB builds equipment for serious expeditions and the Simpson III's seam quality and zipper durability reflect that \u2014 it's spec'd for sustained outback use, not weekend glamping. At 94 lbs, it's the lightest hardshell on this list, which matters on a JL.\n\nThe CVT Mt. Rainier at $1,799 offers a slightly larger sleep platform than the ARB at a competitive price. CVT is a Utah-based brand with a strong reputation in the overlanding community, and their quality control is consistent. The Mt. Rainier's 4\" mattress is notably comfortable compared to thinner options.\n\nThe Tepui Autana at $1,099 is the softshell entry \u2014 lower cost, lighter when folded (62 lbs), but requires a longer setup time (2\u20135 minutes) and the nylon shell accumulates condensation in ways hardshell designs don't. The Autana is a functional choice for seasonal fair-weather use or builds where budget is a hard constraint.\n\nThe ARB 2500 Awning at $299 is the single highest-value-per-dollar addition to an overlanding JL \u2014 a shaded cooking and relaxing area deployed in 60 seconds. The 8.2' x 8.2' footprint provides genuine shade. It mounts to the roof rack side rail and is compatible with the same racks as the tent options above.","steps":["Install the roof rack system first. Follow the JL-specific instructions for your rack brand (Rhino-Rack, Yakima, or Thule). Confirm the rack is torqued to the mounting specs \u2014 under-torqued racks shift under tent load.","Position the tent on the rack. Most RTTs mount with 4 or 6 bolts through the tent's base platform into the rack's cross bars. Measure the mounting bolt pattern before positioning.","With a helper, lift the tent assembly onto the rack. Hardshell tents weigh 90\u2013120 lbs \u2014 this is a two-person job. Use a step ladder or work on a level surface.","Insert mounting hardware through the base platform and into the rack T-slot nuts or cross-bar channel. Start all bolts finger-tight before torquing.","Confirm the tent is centered and square on the roof before final torquing. Off-center tent placement affects roof load distribution.","Torque mounting hardware to the tent manufacturer's spec (typically 35\u201350 ft-lb on the main mount bolts).","Open the tent fully and confirm the hinge mechanism operates correctly and the ladder deploys and locks.","For the ARB awning: mount the awning bracket to the side of the rack rail, route the awning roll correctly, and test deployment before the first camp.","Drive 50 miles on highway and recheck all mounting torque before first overnight use."],"pros":["Hardshell designs (iKamper, ARB, CVT) set up in under 60 seconds \u2014 faster than a ground tent","iKamper Skycamp Mini's 110 lb weight is the most favorable for JL roof load ratings","ARB's expedition-grade construction is field-proven on vehicles that cross the Australian outback","The ARB awning at $299 delivers outsized utility relative to cost \u2014 shade and wind break for cooking, eating, and gear organization","RTT elevation keeps you off cold or wet ground and away from ground-level wildlife concerns"],"cons":["Factory JL roof rails require a supplementary rack system before any RTT can be safely mounted \u2014 add $300\u2013$600 to tent cost","165 lb static roof limit requires careful attention to tent weight plus occupant weight \u2014 understand the rating before purchasing a heavy tent","Hardshell tents add significant aerodynamic drag and wind noise at highway speed \u2014 fuel economy drops noticeably","RTT setup requires highway stops to open/close the tent for each camp \u2014 not suited to itineraries with multiple camp moves per day","Softshell (Tepui) condensation management requires ventilation habit changes vs sleeping inside a hardshell","The iKamper Skycamp Mini is premium-priced \u2014 if cost is a primary constraint, the ARB Simpson III delivers the core functionality at $800 less"]},{"id":"jl-recovery-points-guide","title":"JL Wrangler Recovery Points: What's Rated and What Isn't","vehicle":"jl","category":"recovery","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":50,"high":300},"tools_required":["22mm or 7/8\" wrench (for D-rings)","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["recovery","recovery_points","tow-hooks","d-rings","trail","safety"],"summary":"The JL has two factory-rated recovery points at the front frame horns \u2014 the painted tow hooks are anchored directly to the frame. The rear tow hooks are also rated. Aftermarket front bumpers often include shackle receivers or D-ring tabs that are rated higher. The factory tie-down loops in the cargo area are NOT rated for vehicle recovery.","description":"# JL Wrangler Recovery Points: What's Rated and What Isn't\n\nUsing the wrong recovery point is how you pull a bumper off a Jeep \u2014 or worse, send a snatch block back through a windshield under load. The JL has real recovery points from the factory. Knowing exactly where they are and what they can handle takes two minutes and could save someone's day on trail.\n\n**Front tow hooks (frame-mounted):**\nThe JL ships with two front tow hooks \u2014 the angled hooks visible on the front frame horns. These are welded to mounting brackets that bolt directly to the frame. They are rated recovery points. Most JL front tow hooks are rated at the vehicle's GVWR or higher.\n\n**How to use them:** Attach a shackle (3/4\" or 7/8\" screw-pin D-ring or bow shackle) to the hook. Run your recovery strap, snatch strap, or kinetic rope to the shackle. Never loop a strap directly around the hook without a shackle \u2014 it can slip and shock load in an unexpected direction.\n\n**Rear tow hooks (frame-mounted):**\nThe JL has rear tow hook mounts integrated into the rear frame rails. On most trims, there are two rear hooks visible at the bottom corners of the rear bumper area. These are also frame-mounted and rated.\n\n**What's NOT a recovery point:**\n- Tie-down loops in the cargo area (rated for cargo, not towing)\n- Stabilizer bar links or track bars\n- Trailer hitch receivers (unless rated \u2014 most factory JL hitches are rated for towing, but verify before using as a recovery anchor)\n- Aftermarket accessories bolted to body panels rather than frame\n\n**D-ring mounts (shackle receivers):**\nMost aftermarket front and rear bumpers include integrated shackle receivers \u2014 threaded or bolted mount points that accept a D-ring directly. These are cleaner than hooks: the D-ring sits flush, doesn't catch brush, and swivels to the load angle.\n\nStandalone shackle receiver mounts can also be bolted directly to the factory tow hook mounting points without replacing the bumper.\n\n**Recovery hitches:**\nA 2\" receiver-to-D-ring adapter ($25\u2013$50) converts the factory trailer hitch receiver to a rear recovery point. Verify the factory hitch's tow rating first \u2014 JL Sport factory hitches are typically rated at 3,500 lbs. Match the shackle and strap rating to the lowest rated component.\n\n| Shackle size | Working load limit | Common use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3/4\" D-ring | 4.75 tons | Sufficient for JL recovery |\n| 7/8\" D-ring | 6.5 tons | Better for snatch block rigging |\n| 1\" D-ring | 8.5 tons | Overbuilt for most JL use but fine |\n\nThe JL's GVW is approximately 5,500\u20136,000 lbs depending on trim. A 3/4\" D-ring is technically sufficient but the 7/8\" is the standard for trail recovery \u2014 the cost difference is minimal and the margin matters when you're running a kinetic rope at full stretch.\n\nAmazon: https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000CJNKQE?tag=trailmanual0b-20 (Bubba Rope 7/8\" D-rings, set of 2)\n\n- Always use a strap dampener (a floor mat, heavy jacket, or purpose-made strap dampener) over the recovery strap midpoint. If a fitting fails under load, the dampener reduces snap-back energy significantly.\n- Screw-pin shackles: tighten the pin fully, then back off 1/4 turn to prevent seizing. Tape the pin after seating.\n- Never stand in line with a recovery strap under load \u2014 the energy release direction if anything fails is along the strap axis.\n- Check the recovery point anchor bolts after every recovery. Frame mounting bolts can loosen under shock load.\n\n- 7/8\" D-ring shackles (pair): $25\u2013$45 (rated, with safety pin)\n- Shackle receiver mounts for factory tow hook locations: $50\u2013$120 (Warn, Rugged Ridge)\n- Recovery hitch adapter (2\" receiver to D-ring): $25\u2013$50\n\nYou don't need to spend a lot here. Two quality 7/8\" D-rings and an understanding of which points are rated covers most JL recovery situations. The expensive part of recovery is the rope and the strap \u2014 get those right first."},{"id":"jl-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit for the JL","vehicle":"jl","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":650},"tools_required":["none \u2014 kit assembly only"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["recovery","gear","trail-prep","self-recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Recovery Kit (RK11B)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Bubba Rope 20' Gator-Jaw Synthetic Kinetic Recovery Rope","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":119},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Boards (pair)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink Rope-to-Receiver Shackle","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Rhino USA Heavy Duty 3/4\" D-Ring Shackles (pair)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Kit RK11B Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/recovery-kit-rkb11"},{"title":"Bubba Rope Kinetic vs Static Strap Guide","url":"https://www.bubbarope.com/kinetic-vs-static-recovery"},{"title":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Board Instructions","url":"https://www.maxtrax.com.au/products/mkii"}],"alternatives":["jl-winch"],"summary":"Before you go anywhere technical in a JL, you need a recovery kit \u2014 not because the Wrangler gets stuck often, but because when it does, the tools to get yourself out should be on the rig, not at home.","description":"Recovery gear is split into two functional categories: things that pull you out (straps, shackles, snatch blocks) and things that get you unstuck without needing another vehicle (traction boards, traction mats, a high-lift jack). A complete starter kit covers both. You don't need every item on day one, but you should understand what each does before you need it.\n\nThe ARB Recovery Kit ($499) is the fastest way to check off most of the list at once. The RK11B includes a kinetic recovery strap, tree saver strap, snatch block, shackles, and a recovery damper \u2014 all in a gear bag. ARB's quality control on strap strength ratings is consistent, and the kit is assembled by people who understand recovery rigging rather than marketing. The $499 price is higher than sourcing individual budget parts, but you're getting components with verified strength ratings and a known source.\n\nIf you're building your kit piece by piece, the Bubba Rope Gator-Jaw at $119 is the correct kinetic strap choice. Kinetic ropes stretch under load and use stored elastic energy to help break a vehicle free \u2014 they're dramatically more effective than static tow straps for a stuck vehicle and create less violent loading on both vehicles in the recovery. The 20' length is appropriate for most JL-to-JL or JL-to-recovery-vehicle distances. Do not substitute a static tow strap for a kinetic rope on a hard pull.\n\nMaxtrax MKII traction boards ($349/pair) are for self-recovery without another vehicle. The ridged plastic boards wedge under a spinning tire and give it something to grip. They work on sand, mud, and loose terrain. The MKII design is the current iteration \u2014 the ribs are sized to work with 32\"\u201337\" tires. At $349, the pair is an investment, but cheaper alternatives sacrifice the ridge geometry that makes MKII effective. Budget alternatives at $80\u2013$120/pair exist, but the recovery rate drops.\n\nThe Factor 55 FlatLink ($65) is for winch-equipped JLs. It replaces the hook at the end of your winch rope with a flat, low-profile shackle mount that's compatible with soft shackles and eliminates the dangerous projectile risk of a steel hook in a failed recovery. If you have a winch, the FlatLink should be on the rig.","steps":["Identify the storage location in your JL for the recovery kit \u2014 most JL builds use a dedicated cargo organizer, a Pelican case, or a soft bag stored in the cargo area behind the rear seat.","Inspect all items in the ARB kit before the first trail day \u2014 verify strap condition, shackle ratings, and snatch block operation.","For traction boards: practice insertion and extraction before you need them in a real situation. The correct technique is to push them under the spinning tire perpendicular to the axle with the ridged side up, then drive forward or backward slowly.","For kinetic rope: inspect for fraying before each use. Do not use a damaged kinetic rope \u2014 failure under load is violent.","Brief any trail partners on your recovery setup before you're in the mud figuring it out. Know where the recovery points are on your rig (the tow hooks at front and rear bumper on the JL are the primary attach points for soft shackles and hook connections).","Store recovery damper (sometimes called a recovery blanket) in an accessible location \u2014 it drapes over the strap between vehicles and reduces projectile risk if the strap parts under load."],"pros":["ARB RK11B covers the critical recovery scenarios in one purchase at a known quality level","Kinetic rope (Bubba Rope or ARB's included strap) is dramatically safer and more effective than static tow straps for unstuck recoveries","Maxtrax traction boards enable solo self-recovery \u2014 you don't need another vehicle on the trail to use them","Factor 55 FlatLink eliminates the steel hook projectile risk on a winch \u2014 mandatory upgrade for winch-equipped JLs","The full kit at $650 all-in is modest relative to the cost of a damaged rig or a tow truck"],"cons":["Maxtrax MKII at $349/pair is the meaningful cost item in the kit; budget boards are available but less effective","ARB kit is comprehensive but includes some items you may not use (tree saver is only useful in wooded terrain) \u2014 piece-by-piece sourcing can optimize to your terrain type","Recovery skill matters more than hardware quality \u2014 invest time in learning proper recovery technique, not just collecting gear","Kinetic ropes must be kept clean and free of UV exposure during storage \u2014 degraded elasticity reduces effectiveness significantly","None of this gear replaces a winch for technical recovery situations; plan the kit as a foundation, not a substitute"]},{"id":"jl-recovery-points-install","title":"Recovery Point Hardware \u2014 Rated Shackles and D-Ring Mounts for the JL","vehicle":"jl","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":220},"tools_required":["22mm socket or wrench","torque wrench","thread locker (blue)"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["recovery-points","recovery","d-ring","shackle","tow-hook","soft-shackle"],"parts":[{"name":"Factor 55 ProLink Shackle Mount (fits JL factory receiver)","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":139},{"name":"ARB Rated Recovery Points \u2014 Front Pair (JL Wrangler)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":119},{"name":"Warn Clevis Shackle Kit (3/4\" rated, 4.75 ton WLL)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":69},{"name":"Bubba Rope Soft Shackle (7/16\", 35,000 lb MBS)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"Factor 55 ProLink Product Page","url":"https://factor55.com/products/prolink"},{"title":"ARB Recovery Points JL Application","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"}],"alternatives":["jl-winch","jl-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The factory JL tow hooks are tested for towing, not rated for recovery loads. Replace them or supplement them with rated recovery point hardware before you need to be pulled out of something.","description":"The JL Wrangler comes with factory tow hooks front and rear on most trims. Mopar does publish pull ratings for the OEM hooks, but the brackets and welded mounting points are sized for towing dynamics \u2014 low-speed sustained load \u2014 not the shock loads and odd angles common in a vehicle recovery. Before attaching a strap to a tow hook and having another vehicle yank you out of a creek bed, understand what you're attached to.\n\nThe right answer is hardware that's rated for recovery: products with a published Working Load Limit (WLL) and a minimum break strength (MBS) that matches your recovery gear. The hardware listed here is rated for recovery use, sold by vendors that stake their reputation on these products, and compatible with standard recovery straps, kinetic ropes, and winch lines.\n\n**Rated D-rings / clevis shackles:** The traditional solution. A 3/4\" screw-pin clevis rated at 4.75 ton WLL is the minimum spec for a Wrangler-class vehicle. Warn and ARB both make reliable options. Thread the pin closed and secure with a cotter pin or safety wire so it can't unscrew under load. D-rings are bulky but inexpensive and nearly indestructible.\n\n**Soft shackles:** Dyneema or similar HMPE-fiber loops that serve the same role at a fraction of the weight. Rated options from Bubba Rope, Yankum, or Warn handle the load of any JL recovery and collapse safely if they fail \u2014 no metal hardware flying through the air. The only downside is UV degradation and abrasion: inspect the sheath before every trail run.\n\n**Factor 55 ProLink / dedicated receiver mounts:** If your bumper or factory tow hook receiver accepts a 2\" insert, the Factor 55 ProLink replaces it with a rated hook that accepts soft shackles or clevis hardware cleanly. Eliminates improvised attachment points and keeps the recovery hardware purpose-built. The cleanest option for a steel bumper with a receiver tube.\n\nAt minimum: swap the factory tow hook D-ring (if stock D-rings are included) for rated 3/4\" clevis hardware, and keep at least one front and one rear recovery point accessible at all times. The front recovery point matters when you're nose-in to something; the rear is for the more common situation of needing a straight pull backward off a stuck approach.","steps":["Identify your recovery point attachment locations: factory tow hooks, bumper receiver inserts, or purpose-built tabs on aftermarket bumpers.","For D-ring / clevis install at a tow hook: remove the factory hook bolt (typically 18mm), clean the mounting hole threads, apply blue thread locker, torque the D-ring mounting bolt to the bumper manufacturer spec or 40\u201350 ft-lb minimum.","For receiver-style mounts (Factor 55 ProLink): insert into the receiver tube, insert the pin through the receiver cross-hole, secure with the included hardware.","Verify the shackle pin is fully threaded and backed off 1/4 turn (to prevent galling under load). Safety wire or tape the pin.","Confirm the attachment point is accessible and visible \u2014 recovery points buried behind brush guards defeat the purpose.","Keep at least one soft shackle in the recovery bag to bridge between differently-sized hardware in the field."],"pros":["Rated hardware gives a real WLL number \u2014 no guessing whether the tow hook will hold","Soft shackles eliminate the \"metal projectile\" failure mode of wire or D-rings under snap loads","Factor 55 / ARB dedicated mounts are the cleanest integration with purpose-built bumpers","D-rings and soft shackles are inexpensive enough to run multiples \u2014 both ends, plus one spare"],"cons":["Factory tow hook threads may not accept aftermarket D-ring bolts without an adapter \u2014 measure before ordering","Soft shackles degrade from UV and abrasion \u2014 must be inspected before use; replace at first sign of sheath damage","Dedicated mount systems (Factor 55) require a compatible receiver tube or bumper cutout \u2014 not universal","Recovery points are only as strong as their mount \u2014 a D-ring on a flimsy bumper tab is still a weak link"]},{"id":"jl-winch","title":"Winch Selection for the JL Wrangler \u2014 10,000 lb Minimum","vehicle":"jl","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire crimper","heat shrink and lighter","drill (for some bumper installs)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 Gen2 10,000 lb Winch","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Warn M8000-S Winch (8,000 lb) \u2014 minimum for JLU","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com/zeon-10-s"},{"title":"Warn Winch Selection Guide","url":"https://www.warn.com/winch-selector"},{"title":"Smittybilt X20 Gen2 Winch Overview","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com/recovery/winches/x20-gen2-winch.html"}],"alternatives":["jl-recovery-gear-kit","jl-front-bumper"],"summary":"A 10,000 lb winch is the correct floor for the JL \u2014 the JLU 4-door has a GVWR up to 5,300 lbs, and a winch rated at only 1.5x your vehicle weight is inadequate under real recovery conditions.","description":"The general rule is a winch rated at at least 1.5x the gross vehicle weight. The JL 2-door GVWR is approximately 4,500 lbs; the JLU 4-door sits at 4,900\u20135,300 lbs depending on trim and options. At those numbers, 8,000 lbs is technically marginal on a JLU and genuinely inadequate on a loaded Sahara with armor, a full tank, and gear. A 10,000 lb winch is the right answer for any JL build, and the cost difference between the Smittybilt X20 10K and an 8K unit is minimal.\n\nThe Warn VR EVO 10-S at $449 is the sweet spot for most JL builds. Warn's engineering reputation is earned \u2014 the EVO is Warn's entry-level sealed unit, which means the motor and control pack are protected from water and mud ingestion. Warn's customer support and parts availability are significantly better than budget brands, and the EVO's wireless remote is reliable. This is the first winch recommendation for anyone who hasn't winched before and wants a unit that behaves predictably.\n\nThe Smittybilt X20 Gen2 at $349 is the budget option. The X20 Gen2 is a notable improvement over the original X20 in build quality \u2014 sealed motor, IP67 rating, and wireless remote included. For a rig that uses the winch occasionally, the X20 Gen2 performs adequately. It won't hold up to heavy repeated use (multiple pulls per day in sustained mud) as well as the Warn, but for a JL that hits trails a few times a year, it's a functional choice.\n\nThe Warn Zeon 10-S at $1,099 is the premium choice for builds where the winch is a primary tool rather than a recovery backup. The Zeon uses a faster line speed, better motor cooling, and a free-spooling clutch mechanism that is noticeably smoother than the EVO. If you're running technical trails regularly and expect to use the winch as an active recovery tool \u2014 not just an emergency backup \u2014 the Zeon is worth the premium.\n\n**Synthetic vs steel rope:** Every winch listed here ships with steel wire rope by default unless the model number ends in -S, which denotes synthetic. Synthetic rope (Dyneema or equivalent) is the correct choice for trail use \u2014 it doesn't store kinetic energy like steel, won't create a lethal snap-back if it parts under load, and is safe to touch with bare hands during rigging. The -S variants cost slightly more but the safety case for synthetic is not debatable. If your winch ships with steel, budget $80\u2013$150 for a synthetic line upgrade.","steps":["Confirm your front bumper has a winch-ready plate with mounting holes on the correct hole pattern for your winch model. Most JL aftermarket bumpers use a standard multi-pattern plate.","Thread the power leads from the winch drum along the mounting plate before bolting the winch down \u2014 much easier than fishing them after.","Mount the winch to the bumper plate. Torque mounting hardware to manufacturer spec \u2014 typically 40\u201350 ft-lb.","Route the positive and negative cables from the winch to the battery. Use the cable routing path that avoids the radiator and accessory belts.","Connect the positive lead directly to the battery positive post (with the inline fuse/fusible link that ships with the winch). Connect the negative lead to the battery negative post.","Install the fairlead (roller or Hawse \u2014 Hawse for synthetic rope, roller for steel) onto the bumper fairlead mount. Hawse fairleads require smooth Dyneema; roller fairleads are designed for steel.","Feed the rope through the fairlead, attach the hook or soft shackle to the drum end, and spool the rope onto the drum under light tension (have a helper pull the free end while you run the winch in).","Test in both directions before the trail. Confirm wireless remote function."],"pros":["Warn VR EVO 10-S delivers genuinely reliable performance at an accessible price point \u2014 the best value in this category","10,000 lb rating covers all JL and JLU GVWR variants with meaningful headroom","Synthetic rope (on -S models) eliminates the danger of steel snap-back under load","Wireless remote on all options listed is a meaningful safety upgrade \u2014 you can stay off the direct pull line while operating","Winch-ready bumpers make installation straightforward; no fabrication required"],"cons":["Smittybilt X20 Gen2 is not the choice for rigs that winch regularly or in sustained heavy conditions \u2014 the motor will overheat faster than Warn units","Steel rope on non-S variants is a safety downgrade \u2014 budget for synthetic line upgrade if your winch ships with steel","Winch weight (35\u201370 lbs) adds to front-end nose weight; relevant on already-heavy builds with armor","The Warn M8000-S listed here is included for reference but is below the recommended minimum for a fully-loaded JLU \u2014 use 10K as the floor","Electrical connections must be properly fused and routed \u2014 a poorly done winch install is a fire risk; take the time to do the wiring correctly"]},{"id":"jl-alignment-jl","title":"Alignment After Lift \u2014 JL-Specific Specs","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":100,"high":180},"tools_required":["tape measure","alignment shop or string-line setup","tie rod wrench"],"skills_required":["measurement","tape-measure toe check"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2026"],"tags":["caster","toe","death-wobble-prevention","lift-aftercare"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy adjustable front lower control arms (pair)","part_number":"8062","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":540},{"name":"Rough Country adjustable lower arms","part_number":"11700","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Teraflex adjustable front lower arms","part_number":"1815110","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":470}],"citations":[{"title":"JL Wrangler Alignment Specs Discussion","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/jl-wrangler-wheel-alignment-specs-toe-caster-camber.17832/"},{"title":"Basic DIY JL Front End Alignment \u2014 WAYALIFE","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/write-up-basic-diy-jeep-jl-wrangler-front-end-alignment.56571/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A 2\"+ lift on a JL drops caster below the 4\u00b0 safety floor; you need adjustable lower control arms to bring it back to 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0 or the front end will wander at highway speed.","description":"The JL runs more positive caster from the factory than the JK did \u2014 Sport models target 5.35\u00b0, Sahara 5.05\u00b0, Rubicon 4.80\u00b0, with toe at 0.20\u00b0 \u00b1 0.15\u00b0 total and camber at -0.25\u00b0. That extra caster is what makes the JL feel less twitchy than the JK at highway speed. The problem: lift kits rotate the axle pinion up, and on a solid-axle Jeep that means caster goes down by roughly 1\u00b0 per inch of lift. Two inches of lift and you're at 3\u00b0\u20134\u00b0 caster, below the Jeep service manual's 4.0\u00b0 minimum. That's when the wandering and floaty steering complaints start.\n\nWhat can and can't be adjusted: toe is the only spec you can change with stock parts \u2014 loosen the tie rod adjuster sleeve clamps, rotate the sleeve, retighten. Camber on a solid axle is fixed by the axle housing; there is no adjustment. Caster requires changing the angle of the axle, which means adjustable lower control arms (cheapest path) or adjustable upper and lower arms (best path for big lifts). The lowers reposition the axle fore-aft; the uppers rotate the pinion. With just lowers you can claw back about 2\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 of caster. With both, you can fully restore stock geometry.\n\nTarget settings for a lifted JL: caster 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0, total toe 0.15\u00b0\u20130.20\u00b0 toe-in, camber whatever the axle gives (don't worry about it). Higher caster up to 6\u00b0 feels tighter but eats tires faster and adds steering effort. Below 4\u00b0 you risk wander and death-wobble vulnerability.\n\nThe tape-measure toe check works at home: roll the Jeep forward 6 feet to settle the suspension, measure between the front of the tires at hub height, then between the rear of the tires at the same height. The front number should be 1/16\" to 1/8\" smaller than the rear. Adjust the tie rod sleeve to dial it in. A proper alignment shop will charge $100\u2013$140 to do this with a rack and verify caster, which is worth doing after any lift.","steps":["Confirm the lift is fully installed, all control arm bolts torqued at ride height.","Drive the Jeep 20\u201350 miles to settle bushings.","Bring to alignment shop with a printout of target specs (caster 4.5\u00b0\u20135.5\u00b0, total toe 0.15\u00b0\u20130.20\u00b0 toe-in).","If caster is below 4\u00b0, do not road-test at highway speed \u2014 install adjustable lower control arms first.","With adjustable lowers, lengthen them to push axle rearward, increasing caster.","With adjustable uppers, shorten them to rotate pinion up.","Have shop re-measure after each adjustment.","Set total toe to 0.15\"\u20130.20\" toe-in.","Center the steering wheel by adjusting equal turns on each tie rod end.","Test drive \u2014 straight steering, no pull, no wander at 65 mph."],"pros":["Restores correct caster after a lift","Eliminates highway wander","Reduces death wobble risk by keeping caster above 4\u00b0","Adjustable arms also re-center axle fore-aft"],"cons":["Caster correction requires aftermarket arms","Camber is non-adjustable \u2014 you live with what the axle gives you","Big lifts (4\"+) need both upper and lower adjustable arms","Setting caster too high eats tires and adds steering effort"]},{"id":"jl-death-wobble-jl","title":"Death Wobble on JL \u2014 Different from JK but Same Root Causes","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","subcategory":"diagnostics","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","pry bar","torque wrench","metric sockets"],"skills_required":["front-end inspection","recall lookup","torque to spec"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2024"],"tags":["death-wobble","safety-critical","track-bar","recall","TSB","ball-joints"],"parts":[{"name":"OEM JL front track bar (post-recall)","part_number":"68398018AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":245},{"name":"Synergy JL HD adjustable track bar","part_number":"8861-15","vendor":"Synergy Mfg","price_estimate_usd":419},{"name":"Steel steering gear box (TSB 08-074-20)","part_number":"68250506AE","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":980},{"name":"Dynatrac HD ball joints JL (pair)","part_number":"DA60-2X1052-J","vendor":"Dynatrac","price_estimate_usd":480}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 18V-675 \u2014 JL track bar bracket weld","url":"https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2018/RCRIT-18V675-4839.pdf"},{"title":"JLWranglerForums \u2014 Track Bar Bracket Weld Recall","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/track-bar-bracket-weld-issue-recall-announced/"},{"title":"4xeForums \u2014 Lower ball joint TSB 02-004-21 build dates 9/16/20\u20138/4/21","url":"https://www.4xeforums.com/threads/if-your-jeep-wrangler-jl-was-built-between-9-16-20-and-8-4-21-your-lower-ball-joints-may-loose-causing-death-wobble.3631/"}],"alternatives":["jl-track-bar-jl","jl-ball-joints-jl"],"summary":"Death wobble on a JL has the same root cause as the JK \u2014 loose or worn front-end components triggered by a bump \u2014 but the JL adds three platform-specific suspects: the recalled 2018 track bar bracket weld (NHTSA 18V-675), the aluminum steering gear box (TSB 08-074-20), and the lower ball joint TSB on trucks built between September 2020 and August 2021. Check those three first, then inspect the rest of the front end like a JK.","description":"The JL inherited the JK's solid front axle with a linked drag-link-and-tie-rod setup. Same geometry, same failure mode. What changed is what Jeep tried to do to fix the JK's reputation \u2014 and some of those changes created new failure points.\n\n**The 2018 track bar bracket recall.** Early JLs (built before December 2018) had a frame-side track bar bracket where the weld was placed off the seam. Under load the bracket separated from the frame, and the axle could shift freely. NHTSA recall 18V-675 covers the fix. If your JL is a 2018 build, run the VIN through NHTSA's recall lookup before doing anything else \u2014 if the recall has not been performed, the dealer fixes it free.\n\n**The aluminum steering gear box.** 2018 JLs left the factory with an aluminum steering gear box that flexed under high steering input. TSB 08-074-20 replaces it with a steel unit. Symptom is vague on-center steering and wobble that returns after every alignment. Dealer handles this under warranty for affected VINs.\n\n**Lower ball joints, builds 9/16/20 to 8/4/21.** TSB 02-004-21-REV A covers lower front ball joints that loosen prematurely and trigger death wobble. If your JL was built in that window, the dealer inspects and replaces them free of charge.\n\n**Then it's a JK inspection.** With recalls and TSBs cleared, run the same systematic front-end inspection you would on a JK. Get the wheels off the ground, rock each tire 12/6 and 9/3 for ball joint and tie rod end play, watch every joint while a helper turns the steering wheel, and check track bar bolt torque (110 ft-lb axle / 125 ft-lb frame on JL). Re-torque after any wheeling trip. A stabilizer cannot cure death wobble on a JL any more than it can on a JK \u2014 fix the root cause.\n\n**A note on aftermarket fixes.** Some owners install a dual-pin track bar bracket (Synergy, Metalcloak) when the frame-side bracket has wallowed even after a recall fix. Adjustable track bars from Synergy and RockJock allow re-centering the axle after a lift, which matters because off-center geometry amplifies wobble. Heavy-duty tie rod kits (RockJock Currectlync JL CE-9701JL, Steer Smarts Yeti XD) replace the factory bent rod with a straight DOM tube that resists impact-bend on rocks.","steps":["Run your VIN through NHTSA's recall lookup at nhtsa.gov/recalls. Address recall 18V-675 (track bar bracket) if it shows open.","Ask the dealer to check TSB 08-074-20 (steering gear box) and TSB 02-004-21 (ball joints) against your build date. Both are dealer-paid if eligible.","With recalls cleared, put the front wheels on jack stands. Rock each tire at 12/6 and 9/3 \u2014 diagnose worn ball joints vs. tie rod ends.","Have a helper turn the wheel slowly while you watch each joint. Independent slop is a worn part.","Verify track bar bolt torque: 110 ft-lb at the axle, 125 ft-lb at the frame.","Inspect track bar bushings and bracket. Pry-test the bar at the axle bracket for any movement.","Replace worn components, re-torque, alignment shop afterward.","Road test gradually toward the speed wobble occurred at, with bump tests in between."],"pros":["Recalls and TSBs are free at the dealer","Solves the wobble at the source rather than masking it","Catches related wear before it becomes a roadside breakdown"],"cons":["Diagnosis still takes a couple of hours","Aluminum gear box replacement is dealer-only on out-of-warranty trucks ($900+)","Lifted JLs with 35\"+ tires need geometry correction in addition to part replacement","Some owners report repeat wobble even after full front-end replacement on early 2018 trucks \u2014 track bar bracket reinforcement may be required"]},{"id":"jl-tie-rods-jl","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":500,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","tie rod separator","metric sockets","torque wrench","tape measure","grease gun"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["steering","death-wobble","recall"],"parts":[{"name":"Apex Chassis 2.5 Ton ProLock Tie Rod & Drag Link Kit (Steel)","part_number":"KIT115","vendor":"Apex Chassis","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Synergy Heavy Duty Chromoly Tie Rod (JL)","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":525},{"name":"Steer Smarts YETI XD JL Drag Link","vendor":"Steer Smarts","price_estimate_usd":750}],"citations":[{"title":"Apex Chassis KIT115 JL Steering Kit","url":"https://apexchassis.com/products/kit115-jeep-jl-steering-kit-steel"},{"title":"JL Wrangler Tie Rod & Drag Link Guide","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/guide-to-replacing-stubborn-steering-components-tie-rod-and-drag-link/"}],"alternatives":["jl-alignment-jl","jl-steering-stabilizer-jl"],"summary":"The JL's factory steering was the subject of a 2019 service campaign and is still the weakest link on lifted trucks \u2014 a heavy-duty tie rod and matching drag link tighten the front end and stop the wandering most owners complain about.","description":"Jeep updated the JL tie rod under service bulletin W30 because the original two-piece design developed play that drove an epidemic of loose, vague steering. Even the updated factory tie rod is fine for stock trucks on stock tires, but it's the first thing to fail once you add 35\"+ tires, a lift, or any meaningful trail use. The drag link uses the same forged-end design and tends to develop the same play a few thousand miles later.\n\nThree configurations cover most builds. A complete steel kit like Apex Chassis KIT115 ($900) gets you a 2.5-ton tie rod and drag link with greasable forged ends in one box \u2014 that's the simplest upgrade if you want everything done in a single afternoon. Synergy and Steer Smarts both sell standalone chromoly tie rods around $500\u2013$550, which is the right call if your drag link is still tight and only the tie rod has play. For the loaded overland or rockcrawler build, Steer Smarts YETI XD covers both pieces with 30 mm ball joints at the top of the price range.\n\nAluminum tie rods are sold for the JL but corrosion at the sleeve threads and stripped jam nuts have shown up enough times in the forums that most experienced JL builders steer clear unless weight is the absolute priority.\n\nTorque specs matter on this truck. The knuckle nuts are 47 ft-lb, the pitman arm nut is 77 ft-lb, and the steering stabilizer bolt/nut are 50/55 ft-lb. Jam nuts on 3/4-16 sleeves start at 180 ft-lb \u2014 don't guess them.","steps":["Park level, chock rear wheels, loosen front lug nuts, jack the front axle and put it on stands.","Remove both front wheels for clearance.","Measure the tie rod and drag link center-to-center lengths and write them down.","Remove the steering stabilizer to give yourself room to swing.","Pop the cotter pins, remove the knuckle nuts (tie rod) and pitman arm nut (drag link), and separate with a proper tie rod separator. Avoid pickle forks here \u2014 they wreck the boots if you plan to keep any factory pieces as backups.","Pre-assemble the new tie rod and drag link to match the recorded lengths.","Install the tie rod first. Torque knuckle nuts to 47 ft-lb, then continue tightening to align cotter pin slots. Install new cotter pins.","Install the drag link. Torque the pitman arm nut to 77 ft-lb and the knuckle end to 47 ft-lb.","Tighten all sleeve clamps or jam nuts to the manufacturer's spec (180 ft-lb minimum for 3/4-16). Grease zerks.","Reinstall the steering stabilizer (50 ft-lb bolt, 55 ft-lb nut), wheels (95 ft-lb lugs), and get an alignment."],"pros":["Cures the post-W30 wandering on lifted trucks","Greasable rebuildable ends","Works with the factory steering damper"],"cons":["Best kits run nearly $1,000","Alignment required","Aluminum bargain options can corrode and strip"]},{"id":"jl-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit \u2014 Spacers and Entry Springs (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","spring compressor"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","spacer","budget"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Lift Kit N3 Shocks (JL)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Teraflex 1.5\" Lift Spacer Kit (JL)","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Daystar 2\" Coil Spacers KJ09128BK (JL)","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":129}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex JL Suspension Spacer Kit Product Page","url":"https://teraflex.com/products/15-jl-wrangler-lift-spacer-kit"},{"title":"Rough Country Jeep JL Lift Kits","url":"https://www.roughcountry.com/jeep-wrangler-jl-suspension-lift-kits.html"}],"alternatives":["jl-mid-lift","jl-expedition-lift"],"summary":"A budget lift on the JL clears 33s with room to spare and lifts the stance without touching the geometry \u2014 but you are making trade-offs, and you should know what they are before you buy.","description":"Coil spacers and entry-level spring kits work on the JL because the factory suspension geometry is already reasonably dialed for street and mild trail use. Up to 1.5\u20132 inches, you can add height without significant caster correction or control arm adjustment \u2014 the JL's geometry tolerates that range better than the JK did. The Teraflex 1.5\" spacer kit is the cleanest execution of this approach: it uses a proper spacer that mounts between the coil and the perch rather than a puck shoved in the spring, which limits bind. Daystar's 2\" coil spacers are the same idea at a lower price point but ship as raw spacers with no additional hardware \u2014 fine for overland rigs that don't see aggressive flexing.\n\nThe Rough Country 2\" kit steps up to replacement springs and includes N3 shocks, which is a meaningfully better outcome for the money than spacers alone. The shocks are not premium, but they are better valved for the lifted ride height than the factory shocks pushed beyond their intended range. If you're shopping this tier because budget is a hard constraint, the Rough Country kit is the most complete option under $250. Just know that the control arm angles are not corrected at 2\" \u2014 you will notice mild vibration at highway speed in some rigs, and death wobble risk increases if your front-end components (tie rod, track bar, ball joints) are anything less than perfect.\n\nWhat is not included at this price point: adjustable control arms, caster correction, geometry-matched sway bar end links, or track bar correction. Those items matter more as lift height increases and as trail use intensifies. A 1.5\"\u20132\" spacer lift on a mild-trail or overlanding JL is a defensible choice. A 2\" spacer lift on a rig that sees rocky terrain regularly is asking for accelerated wear on your front-end components.","steps":["Chock rear wheels, loosen front lug nuts, then lift the front of the vehicle and support on jack stands under the frame rails.","Remove front wheels and set aside.","Support the front axle with the floor jack \u2014 don't let it hang on the CV axles.","Remove the front sway bar end links (or disconnect them if using detachable links).","Slowly lower the axle to relieve spring tension. Pull the factory coil springs.","Install spacers per manufacturer instructions. For Daystar puck-style spacers, seat them fully on the upper spring perch before seating the spring beneath.","Reinstall springs, raise the axle to seat the spring, reconnect sway bar end links.","Reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to 100 ft-lb.","Repeat for rear axle: support frame, lower axle, swap rear coils or install rear spacers.","Lower vehicle fully to ground, then torque all suspension fasteners to spec with the vehicle at ride height (critical \u2014 torquing at full droop will bind the bushings).","Drive to an alignment shop within 50 miles. A 2\" lift on the JL will require at minimum a front toe alignment check."],"pros":["Lowest cost entry into a lifted JL \u2014 Daystar spacers at $129 are hard to beat for a mild stance lift","Bolt-on installation with basic tools; no specialty equipment beyond a spring compressor","Retains factory ride quality at the 1.5\" range","Rough Country kit includes shocks that are better matched to the new ride height than factory units","Reversible \u2014 you can return to stock by removing spacers"],"cons":["No caster correction or geometry adjustment \u2014 factory control arm angles are not changed","Increased highway vibration risk, especially on JLs with any front-end wear","Spacer lifts stress the CV axles at full droop \u2014 more aggressive trail use will accelerate axle wear","Rough Country shocks are adequate but not trail-capable; they will not hold up to repeated hard hits on rocky trails","Does not clear 35\" tires without fender trimming on non-Rubicon trims","Not suitable as a foundation for future lift height increases \u2014 spacers compound angle problems already present"]},{"id":"jl-coil-replacement","title":"JL Coil Spring Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (13mm, 15mm, 17mm, 18mm, 21mm)","torque wrench","pry bar","spring compressor (if going taller)"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","suspension disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2023","2024-2026"],"tags":["lift","coils","ride-quality","geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front coil 2-door Sport","part_number":"68250203AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" coil set (front + rear)","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" coil-only kit JL","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Metalcloak 3.5\" Game-Changer coils JL","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":540}],"citations":[{"title":"TORQUE SPECS \u2014 JL Wrangler Reference Guide","url":"https://www.wayalife.com/threads/torque-specs-jl-wrangler-reference-guide.52799/"},{"title":"Jeep JL Wrangler Torque Specs \u2014 Metalcloak","url":"https://metalcloak.com/blog/jeep-jl-wrangler-torque-specs"},{"title":"Complete Torque Values for Jeep JL Wrangler","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/complete-torque-values-for-jeep-jl-wrangler/"}],"alternatives":["jl-shock-replacement","jl-lift-options","jl-control-arms"],"summary":"JL coil swaps are bolt-in if you drop the axle far enough to clear the coils \u2014 the catch is torquing control arm and track bar bolts at ride height, not full droop, or you'll preload the bushings and chew through them.","description":"The JL inherited the JK's coil-sprung solid-axle layout but tightened the geometry \u2014 control arms are stiffer, bushings are different, and the front track bar mounts higher on the frame. The coil swap procedure itself is among the most approachable suspension jobs on the JL because nothing is captured: drop the axle, pull the spring out the top, swap, reassemble. No spring compressor needed for OEM-height or 2.5\" coils.\n\nThe mistake people make on JLs is final-torquing the control arm and track bar bolts with the axle drooping on jack stands. Polyurethane and rubber bushings establish their neutral position based on where the suspension was sitting when the bolt got torqued. Tighten them at full droop, and they're pre-loaded against the resting suspension position the entire time the Jeep is on the ground. The result is harsh ride, premature bushing wear, and rubber bushings that crack within 20K miles. The fix is to either set the axle at ride height on a drive-on lift or to set everything to snug, lower the Jeep, then crawl underneath with a torque wrench.\n\nCoil selection on the JL splits into three lanes. Stock-height replacement (Mopar OEM, $80\u2013$120 per spring) is for owners whose original coils sagged or who lost one to corrosion. 2.5\" leveling coils (Rough Country, Teraflex, Mopar 2\" lift kit) get you to 35\" tires with no other geometry tricks. 3.5\" coils (Metalcloak Game-Changer, Rock Krawler) start to need extended brake lines, adjustable track bar, and adjustable control arms to keep the front end pointed straight. Don't run 3.5\" coils with stock arms and the stock track bar \u2014 bump steer and pulling-to-one-side will follow.\n\nJL coils are progressive-wound on most aftermarket sets. The bottom coils are heavier so they support the rig's curb weight; the top coils are lighter so they compress earlier under small bumps. This is why a Rubicon front spring will sit different heights on a Sport versus a 4xe \u2014 same spring, different weight on it. Weigh your front axle if you're swapping models. The 4xe carries about 200 lb more on the front axle than a gas Sport.\n\nThe bumpstop retainer cup bolt at the top of the coil pocket is hand-tight only; don't crank it. The spring retainer clip at the lower coil seat takes 16 ft-lb. Top shock bolts are 17 ft-lb with a small 1/4\" wrench on the shaft to keep it from spinning. Front lower shock bolt is 70 ft-lb (M14). Track bar at axle: 125 ft-lb. Track bar at frame: 55 ft-lb. Control arm bolts at axle and frame: 125 ft-lb, torqued at ride height.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (21mm) on the corner you're working. Lift the front of the Jeep, support on stands under the frame rails.","Remove front wheels. Support the axle under the differential with a floor jack.","Disconnect both front sway bar end links at the axle (15mm). On Rubicons with electronic disconnect, cycle the disconnect first, then unbolt.","Disconnect the lower shock bolts (18mm) at the axle.","Disconnect the track bar at the axle bracket (21mm, 125 ft-lb to reinstall).","Slowly lower the floor jack to drop the axle. The coils will lose tension as the axle drops 5\u20136 inches.","Remove the spring retainer clip (13mm bolt, 16 ft-lb to reinstall).","Pull the coil out the top with the bumpstop still in the bumpstop cup.","Install the new coil \u2014 make sure it seats in the upper pocket isolator and the lower spring perch.","Reinstall the retainer clip (16 ft-lb).","Jack the axle back up. Reconnect shock, track bar, and sway bar links \u2014 all to \"snug\" only.","Lower the Jeep to the ground, drive 20 feet to settle bushings.","Final-torque control arms (125 ft-lb), track bar (125 ft-lb at axle, 55 at frame), sway bar links (70 ft-lb) at ride height.","Get an alignment. Caster spec: 4.5\u00b0\u20136.0\u00b0."],"pros":["No spring compressor needed for stock-height or 2.5\" coils","Bolt-in swap with common metric tools","Wide aftermarket options from $200 to $600","2.5\" coils clear 35s on a Rubicon with no other parts"],"cons":["Torquing at full droop wrecks bushings \u2014 easy mistake to make","4xe weights front axle differently than gas; coil choice matters","3.5\"+ lifts need adjustable track bar, adjustable control arms, longer brake lines","Always plan an alignment after the swap"]},{"id":"jl-expedition-lift","title":"Expedition and Rock Lift \u2014 Long-Arm Full System ($2,000+)","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":16,"high":24},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":5500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","angle grinder","welder or fab shop access","alignment equipment"],"skills_required":["advanced suspension work","fabrication basics","alignment","drivetrain familiarity"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","long-arm","full-system","rock-crawler"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2 Lift System (JL)","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":3876},{"name":"EVO Mfg Enforcer 3.5\" Lift System (JL)","vendor":"EVO Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":2899},{"name":"Synergy Manufacturing 4\" Long-Arm Upgrade System (JL)","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":4299}],"citations":[{"title":"Rock Krawler JL Wrangler Suspension Systems","url":"https://www.rockkrawler.com/jeep/wrangler-jl-suspension.html"},{"title":"EVO Manufacturing JL Enforcer Lift System","url":"https://www.evomfg.com/jeep-jl-wrangler-suspension-lift-kits"},{"title":"Synergy Manufacturing JL Long-Arm Suspension","url":"https://synergymfg.com/jeep/jl-wrangler-suspension"}],"alternatives":["jl-mid-lift","jl-budget-lift"],"summary":"A long-arm or full expedition system on the JL is a major build commitment \u2014 you're changing the fundamental geometry of the rig, and the labor bill alone can approach the kit cost if you don't do it yourself.","description":"Long-arm suspension replaces the factory short control arms with longer arms that attach further outboard on the axle. The result is dramatically better flex, more stable geometry at full droop, and the ability to run 37\"\u201340\" tires without fighting the suspension for clearance. The JL responds exceptionally well to this treatment because the factory trackbar and control arm mount positions were already better designed than the JK's \u2014 you're not correcting as many fundamentally compromised factory choices, you're extending a good foundation.\n\nThe Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2 is the benchmark in this category. Rock Krawler's JL system uses a mid-arm geometry \u2014 longer than the factory short arms but shorter than a full long-arm \u2014 that delivers 80% of the flex benefit without requiring the crossmember relocation that true long-arm kits demand. The result is a system that a capable home builder can install without frame modification. At $3,876, you get replacement coils, Fox 2.0 Performance shocks, adjustable control arms, and all geometry correction hardware.\n\nThe EVO Mfg Enforcer is the value entry into full-system territory. EVO's approach is aggressive fitment spec and domestic manufacturing, and the Enforcer is genuinely a rock-build kit \u2014 the sway bar end links and front coils are spec'd for heavy hits and axle articulation, not highway comfort. If your JL is primarily a trail rig and you don't care about plush road manners, the Enforcer at $2,899 is money well spent. The Synergy 4\" system is the premium build choice \u2014 Synergy's fitment tolerances and hardware quality are exceptional, and the 4\" height opens up 40\" tire territory on a Rubicon with portal axles.\n\n**4xe owners:** The rear battery pack on the Wrangler 4xe changes the rear suspension geometry and limits available rear lift. Most full systems are not 4xe compatible without modification \u2014 EVO Mfg explicitly lists 4xe compatibility; Rock Krawler and Synergy require direct confirmation with tech support before purchasing. Do not assume a standard JL kit works on a 4xe.\n\n**Warranty:** Any lift over 2\" installed without factory authorization voids the factory powertrain warranty. This is not a scare tactic \u2014 it's documented in Jeep's warranty policy. On a rig this heavily modified, most owners budget for extended aftermarket coverage or accept the trade-off.","steps":["Read the full installation manual before buying any additional parts \u2014 full systems often require supplemental hardware (specific bump stops, brake line extensions, front diff drop if running a non-Rubicon Dana 30) that ships separately.","Plan for a full weekend minimum. 16\u201324 hours is realistic for a first-time installation without professional help.","Lift and support the vehicle safely. Long-arm installs often require full body clearance \u2014 use tall jack stands or a lift.","Remove wheels, disconnect shocks, and disconnect all factory control arms front and rear.","Remove factory coil springs. If the kit requires crossmember modification or skid plate removal, complete that work before reinstalling anything.","Install new front lower and upper control arms per kit sequence. Torque in the correct order \u2014 wrong sequence will preload bushings incorrectly.","Install replacement coil springs front and rear.","Install new shocks. If the kit includes external reservoir shocks, route the reservoir lines before buttoning up.","Install geometry correction hardware: trackbar brackets, caster correction plates, sway bar brackets.","Install new sway bar end links (adjustable or quick-disconnect per kit).","Route any extended brake lines if included \u2014 do not reuse factory lines if kit specifies new ones.","Reinstall skid plates and crossmembers.","Reinstall wheels, lower vehicle to ground.","Torque all fasteners to spec at ride height \u2014 this step is non-negotiable on a full system.","Drive to a reputable alignment shop with lift experience. Request caster adjustment be set to kit spec, not factory spec."],"pros":["Maximum flex and articulation \u2014 rock crawling capability approaches dedicated trail rig levels","Clears 37\"\u201340\" tires depending on kit and axle configuration","Premium shocks included (Fox 2.0 or equivalent) are genuinely trail-capable, not entry-level","Rock Krawler mid-arm approach delivers most flex benefits without requiring frame modification","Well-engineered geometry correction from these brands means predictable, stable handling on-road","These kits are designed as final build platforms, not starting points \u2014 you don't need to upgrade further"],"cons":["Cost is significant \u2014 $2,900\u2013$4,300 for hardware alone, add $1,000\u2013$2,000 labor if shop-installed","Install complexity is high; this is a two-day project for an experienced home builder, longer for first-timers","Voids factory warranty on any JL not past the warranty period","4xe compatibility is not universal \u2014 verify directly with the manufacturer before purchasing","Long-arm kits require a dedicated alignment shop visit and alignment to kit specs, not factory specs","At 3.5\"\u20134\" lift, steering geometry changes are significant enough that an SAS (steering angle sensor) calibration may be required depending on trim level","Heavier and stiffer springs reduce ride compliance compared to factory \u2014 the JL will feel truck-like on pavement"]},{"id":"jl-lift-options-jl","title":"JL Lift Kit Options \u2014 What Changed from JK","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":4000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","spring compressor","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["suspension work","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["lift","geometry-correction"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit with Fox shocks","vendor":"Mopar / Quadratec","price_estimate_usd":1599},{"name":"AEV 2.5\" Dualsport RT","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":1379},{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":3876},{"name":"Geometry correction brackets (JL)","vendor":"Teraflex / JKS / Synergy","price_estimate_usd":200}],"citations":[{"title":"Aftermarket JL Lift Kit suspension options master list","url":"https://www.jlwranglerforums.com/forum/threads/aftermarket-jl-lift-kit-suspension-options-master-list.3690/"},{"title":"The Ultimate Jeep Wrangler Lift Kit Comparison","url":"https://trailrecon.com/blogs/news/jeep-wrangler-lift-kit-comparison-aev-rock-krawler-jock-pro-teraflex"},{"title":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit Fox Series for JLU","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/mopar/2-inch-lift-kit-fox-series-shocks-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited"}],"alternatives":["jl-coil-replacement-jl","jl-alignment-jl"],"summary":"The JL keeps the JK's coil-sprung five-link layout but uses different spring rates, a wider front track bar angle, and a more expensive geometry-correction price tag \u2014 budget about $200 extra over a JK build for the same lift height.","description":"The JL chassis carries forward the five-link front and rear coil suspension from the JK. So you can still pick from spacer lifts, spring lifts, mid-arm kits, and long-arm kits. What changed is the geometry-correction tax. Caster on the JL drops faster per inch of lift, and the OEM geometry-correction bracket from Mopar/aftermarket costs around $200 for a JL versus about $90 for the JK. Plan for that.\n\nSpacer lifts (1.25\"\u20132\") are bolt-on coil spacers. Cheap, fast, and they keep the OEM ride. Mopar's own 2\" kit with Fox shocks at $1,599 is a popular middle path because it's covered if you also lease the Jeep. AEV's 2.5\" Dualsport RT ($1,379) is the expedition-grade spring lift for owners running heavy bumpers and a roof rack. Rock Krawler's 3.5\" Mid-Arm Stage 2 ($3,876) goes furthest with adjustable lower arms, adjustable track bars, and corrected sway bar links \u2014 that's a 37\" tire build.\n\nThe 4xe complicates lift selection: the rear battery pack changes ride height, and lifts under 2\" usually keep the factory shocks; lifts at 2.5\"+ require longer shocks specced for 4xe rear hardware. Confirm any kit you buy lists 4xe compatibility on the box.\n\nSway bar links changed in 2024 \u2014 Jeep updated hardware specs, so a link from a 2018 kit may not fit a 2024 build correctly. Check year-specific fitment before ordering.\n\nTire fitment guide: 33\" tires fit a stock JL with no lift if you trim the front fender liner. 35s need 2\"\u20132.5\" of lift plus 17\"+ wheels with proper backspacing. 37s need 3.5\"+ of lift on a non-Rubicon, or a Dynatrac/AEV kit on a Rubicon designed for that height.","steps":["Decide tire size first \u2014 that drives lift height, not the other way around.","Pick a kit type: spacer (cheap), spring lift (OEM-style ride), or mid-arm/long-arm (best geometry).","Verify 4xe and model-year compatibility with the kit vendor.","Order geometry correction (brackets or adjustable arms) for any lift over 1.5\".","Pull stock springs, install new components per kit instructions.","Re-center axle with the front track bar, then set toe to 1/16\"\u20131/8\" toe-in.","Get a rack alignment \u2014 JL alignment specs are tighter than JK."],"pros":["Wide range of OEM-blessed and aftermarket options","4xe-specific kits available","Backed by stronger factory steering than late JK"],"cons":["Geometry correction costs more than equivalent JK parts","4xe lifts can void some factory warranty coverage","Sway bar link hardware varies by year \u2014 verify before buying"]},{"id":"jl-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift Kit \u2014 Spring Lift with Geometry Correction ($600\u2013$2,000)","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":2000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","pry bar","breaker bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","alignment awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","geometry","spring-lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit with Fox Shocks (JL)","vendor":"Mopar/Jeep Performance Parts","price_estimate_usd":1599},{"name":"JKS 2.5\" Quicker Disconnect Lift System (JL)","vendor":"JKS Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" Performance Spacer Lift System (JL)","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"JKS Manufacturing JL Wrangler Lift Systems","url":"https://www.jksmfg.com/jeep-wrangler-jl-lifts.html"},{"title":"Mopar Jeep Performance Parts \u2014 JL Lift Kit","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/jeep-performance-parts.html"},{"title":"Teraflex JL Performance Spacer Lift System","url":"https://teraflex.com/products/25-jl-wrangler-performance-spacer-lift-system"}],"alternatives":["jl-budget-lift","jl-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The mid-tier lift is where the JL gets genuinely capable \u2014 real geometry correction, 35s without drama, and a ride that holds up on trail \u2014 but the JL costs more to do right than the JK did, and that gap matters when you're budgeting.","description":"The JL's suspension geometry is more complex than the JK's. The trackbar mount positions, control arm angles, and coil spring rates were all updated, and aftermarket lift manufacturers had to start from scratch rather than adapt JK geometry. That's why a properly executed 2.5\" lift on a JL typically runs $200\u2013$400 more than the equivalent JK kit from the same brand. It's not padding \u2014 the geometry correction hardware is different and the spring rates are re-engineered for the JL's heavier body-on-frame structure.\n\nThe Mopar 2\" Fox lift kit is the standout option if warranty coverage matters to you. It's the only JL lift kit that preserves the factory bumper-to-bumper warranty when dealer-installed, and the Fox shocks included are legitimate trail shocks \u2014 not baseline fill valves. The tradeoff is price: at $1,599 installed, you're paying for peace of mind. It also tops out at 2\" with this kit, which gets you to 35s on Rubicon or near there on non-Rubicon with trimming.\n\nThe JKS 2.5\" system is the value choice for a trail-focused build. JKS includes their quicker-disconnect sway bar links, which are a genuine upgrade over stock \u2014 you can disconnect in under 30 seconds trailside without tools. At 2.5\", this kit clears 35s comfortably across all JL trims with minor fender liner trimming. The Teraflex 2.5\" system is the budget-ceiling option in this tier: it's a spacer-based approach rather than a full spring replacement, so you get the height and some geometry correction, but the ride quality doesn't improve as much as it does with the JKS or Mopar kits.\n\nNote on the 2024 JL: updated sway bar link hardware was introduced. Verify kit compatibility with your specific year before purchasing \u2014 some pre-2024 kits require updated hardware for 2024 fitment.","steps":["Stage all hardware and read the full instruction sheet before picking up a wrench \u2014 JL lift kits have a non-obvious sequence for control arm adjustments that varies by brand.","Loosen all lug nuts with the vehicle on the ground.","Lift the vehicle at the frame rails and support securely on jack stands. Lift front and rear separately, do not attempt to lift all four corners at once.","Support the front axle with a floor jack.","Remove front wheels.","Disconnect sway bar end links.","Remove front shocks \u2014 mark factory ride height before removal if adjustable replacement shocks are included.","Compress or relieve tension on front coil springs and remove. Install replacement springs per kit instructions.","Install new front shocks.","Install trackbar drop bracket if included (critical for maintaining steering geometry at 2\"+ lift on the JL).","Install adjustable control arms if included, or verify factory control arm length is within kit spec \u2014 do not assume factory length is acceptable without checking the kit instructions.","Reinstall sway bar end links (new links from kit, or JKS quick-disconnects if included).","Reinstall wheels. Lower front to ground.","Repeat process for rear: support axle, remove rear shocks, swap rear springs, install rear shocks. Install any rear control arm adjusters or trackbar corrections per kit.","Lower vehicle fully to ground.","Torque all suspension fasteners to spec with the vehicle at ride height \u2014 not at full droop.","Drive straight to an alignment shop. A full four-wheel alignment is required, not optional."],"pros":["Clears 35s on all JL trims with minor trimming; 37s possible on Rubicon with additional front-end clearance work","Geometry correction hardware included means much lower vibration and steering wander risk vs spacer lifts","Mopar kit preserves factory warranty when dealer-installed \u2014 only lift option that does","JKS quick-disconnect sway bar links are a trail-day quality-of-life upgrade included in the kit","Full spring replacement (JKS/Mopar) improves overall ride quality compared to factory springs at lifted height","Solid foundation for future upgrades \u2014 lockers, armor, heavier tires"],"cons":["Substantially more expensive than JK equivalent kits due to JL-specific geometry engineering","Trackbar drop bracket required at 2\"+ adds hardware and potential long-term rust/fatigue point","Professional alignment is mandatory and adds $80\u2013$120 to the total cost","Teraflex spacer system in this tier doesn't improve ride quality as much as spring-replacement alternatives","4xe owners: rear lift height interacts with the rear battery pack \u2014 verify 4xe compatibility before purchasing; not all mid-tier kits explicitly support 4xe","Full install is a long day \u2014 plan for 8\u201310 hours if this is your first JL lift"]},{"id":"jl-shock-replacement","title":"Shock Absorber Replacement","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets (18mm, 21mm)","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque sequencing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2026"],"tags":["shocks","ride-quality","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Front Shock (0-2\" lift, JL Unlimited)","part_number":"33-292625","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Rear Shock (2-3\" lift, JL Unlimited)","part_number":"33-292632","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Bilstein B8 5100 Rear Shock (3-4.5\" lift, JL)","part_number":"33-292977","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP Rear Shock (JL)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"OEM Mopar shock (replacement)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Front JL Unlimited 33-292625","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/bilstein/front-5100-series-monotube-shock-absorber-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited-33-292625"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 Rear JL 2-3\" lift 33-292632","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/bilstein/rear-5100-series-monotube-shock-absorber-jeep-wrangler-jl-unlimited-2-3-inch-lift-33-292632"},{"title":"Fox 2.0 Performance Rear IFP Shock JL","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/p/fox-racing-shox/rear-20-performance-series-ifp-shock-jeep-wrangler-jl"}],"alternatives":["jl-coil-replacement","jl-lift-options"],"summary":"Replace JL shocks when they leak, fade on long trails, or no longer settle after a bump \u2014 match shock length to your current lift height, not your future plans.","description":"Stock JL shocks are tuned soft for on-road comfort. They fade quickly on washboard, body-control gets sloppy after 60,000 miles, and the rear shocks are usually the first to leak. If you've added a lift, the stock shocks are likely topped out before full droop \u2014 meaning you've got internal damage even if they aren't visibly leaking.\n\nThe decision tree is short. Stock height and want a better ride? Bilstein 5100 monotubes (33-292625 front, 33-316321 rear for 0-1.5\" lift, 33-292632 rear for 2-3\" lift) are bolt-in upgrades at around $110 per corner. Lifted 2-3\"? Same Bilstein 5100s in the lifted length. Lifted 3\"+? Step up to remote-reservoir shocks like Fox 2.0 Performance Series IFP, Bilstein 8112, or King 2.5 \u2014 your shocks will see more heat and travel.\n\nAvoid the temptation to put a 4\" lift shock on a stock vehicle. The shock will top out at full extension, which damages the internal valving and gives you a harsh ride at any extension.\n\nInstall is the simplest suspension job on the JL. Unlike spring or control arm work, shock replacement doesn't touch alignment geometry. One side at a time, no spring tension released. Stock JL shock mounting uses 18mm and 21mm fasteners. Mopar service spec for shock-to-axle bolt is 75 ft-lb, shock-to-frame upper bolt is 60 ft-lb \u2014 confirm against the included instructions for your specific shock as some aftermarket units use different hardware.\n\nPre-extend new shocks before installing them upright (most monotubes ship gas-charged at full extension, but pre-cycling them by hand helps seat the valving). Don't install a shock upside down \u2014 the IFP gas charge needs to be at the top for proper damping.","steps":["Park on level ground; chock the opposite wheel.","Lift one corner; place jack stand under the frame, leaving the axle hanging.","Remove the wheel.","Spray penetrating oil on both shock mounting bolts; let sit 5 minutes.","Unbolt the lower shock bolt (at the axle bracket).","Unbolt the upper shock bolt (at the frame mount).","Compress the old shock by hand and remove.","Cycle the new shock 3\u20135 times by hand to seat valving.","Install upper bolt first (loose), then lower bolt.","Torque to spec \u2014 typically 75 ft-lb lower, 60 ft-lb upper (verify with your shock's instructions).","Reinstall wheel; torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lb.","Repeat for remaining corners.","Drive 50 miles, then re-check shock bolt torque (rubber bushings settle)."],"pros":["Cheapest meaningful ride upgrade","No alignment needed after","Bolt-in, no spring compressor required","Bilstein 5100s last 60,000+ miles on daily-driver JLs"],"cons":["Wrong-length shocks for your lift cause internal damage","Stock shock mount bolts can seize after Phoenix heat cycles \u2014 penetrant first","Remote-reservoir shocks need clearance routing for the reservoir hose","Rear shocks on Unlimited 4-doors are tight to the gas tank \u2014 work from below"]},{"id":"jl-shocks-upgrade","title":"Shock Absorber Upgrade \u2014 Bilstein 5100, Fox 2.0, and King Options","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","wrench set","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","bilstein","fox","king"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5100 Series Shock Set (JL, set of 4)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":675},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series Shock Set (JL, set of 4)","vendor":"Fox Shocks","price_estimate_usd":850},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Elite Series Shock Set (JL, set of 4)","vendor":"Fox Shocks","price_estimate_usd":1050},{"name":"King OEM Performance Series Shock Set (JL, set of 4)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":1325}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5100 JL Wrangler Shocks Product Page","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/en-us/products/jeep-wrangler-jl-shocks.html"},{"title":"Fox 2.0 Performance JL Shock Overview","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/jeep-wrangler-jl-shocks"},{"title":"King Shocks OEM Performance Series JL","url":"https://www.kingshocks.com/jeep-wrangler-jl"}],"alternatives":["jl-mid-lift","jl-budget-lift","jl-expedition-lift"],"summary":"Shock absorber quality is the single most important determinant of ride quality on a lifted JL \u2014 the springs set the height, but the shocks control everything else, and factory shocks are not built to the standard these vehicles deserve on trail.","description":"The JL Wrangler's factory shocks are adequate for the street-use case the platform was originally calibrated for. The moment you lift the rig, run heavier tires, or take it onto washboard roads and rocky terrain, the factory shocks are out of their designed range. They overheat on sustained trail use, blow through their travel on repeated hits, and provide a choppy ride that fatigues the driver and stresses the rest of the suspension. Aftermarket shocks are not a luxury upgrade \u2014 on a trail-going JL, they're a functional requirement.\n\n**Shock length matters at lift height.** Factory shocks are compressed at lifted ride height because they weren't designed for it. Running factory shocks at 2\" of lift means they spend most of their travel compressed \u2014 you lose bump travel, which is the travel direction that absorbs hits. Aftermarket shocks for lifted JLs are spec'd for the lifted ride height and provide correct travel distribution. When ordering, specify your lift height. Most brands offer shocks in multiple length ranges to match 0\"\u20131.5\", 1.5\"\u20133\", and 3\"+ lift applications.\n\nThe Bilstein 5100 at $675 for a set of four is the benchmark value option. Bilstein's monotube design provides more consistent damping than twin-tube designs (the factory shocks are twin-tube) and the 5100 is a proven choice with extensive real-world use on JL platforms. The 5100 is not adjustable, which is a limitation for builds that want to tune the ride between street and trail configurations.\n\nThe Fox 2.0 Performance at $850 moves up to Fox's internal floating piston (IFP) monotube design. The IFP separates the oil from the nitrogen charge, which maintains consistent damping across temperature ranges better than non-IFP designs. On sustained trail use \u2014 long washboard sections, repeated rocky hits \u2014 the Fox 2.0 holds its tune longer than the Bilstein 5100. The Performance Elite at $1,050 adds remote reservoir capability (on some variants) or an externally adjustable compression knob, which lets you dial the front shocks for street or trail on the fly.\n\nKing OEM Performance at $1,325 for a set of four is the premium choice for builds that prioritize shock performance above all else. King's quality is exceptional and their valving is notably better than Fox or Bilstein at managing high-frequency small hits (the kind that cause driver fatigue on long trail runs) while still handling big impacts. If your JL is primarily a trail rig and you spend significant time in the vehicle on technical terrain, King shocks deliver a noticeably different experience.\n\n**4xe rear shocks:** The Wrangler 4xe's rear battery pack changes the rear spring and shock mounting geometry. The rear shocks on the 4xe are different lengths than the standard JL rear units. When ordering shocks for a 4xe, specify 4xe rear fitment explicitly \u2014 most brands offer 4xe-specific rear shocks, but the front shocks are the same as the standard JL.","steps":["Lift and support the vehicle on jack stands at the frame rails. Support the axle with a floor jack.","For each shock: remove the upper mount bolt/nut first, then the lower.","Note the factory shock mounting orientation before removal \u2014 some JL shocks are not fully symmetrical and must be installed in a specific direction.","Compare old shock length to new shock extended and collapsed length. Confirm the new shock is the correct length for your lift height.","Install new shocks. Start upper mount first, then lower.","Torque upper mount to manufacturer spec (typically 55\u201365 ft-lb). Torque lower mount to spec (typically 70\u201380 ft-lb on the lower axle pin).","For adjustable shocks (Fox Performance Elite, some King variants): set the adjuster to the starting position recommended by the manufacturer before the first trail run.","After installation, cycle the suspension through full travel to confirm no binding and correct range of motion.","Re-torque all shock hardware after the first 500 miles."],"pros":["Bilstein 5100 delivers a significant ride improvement over factory shocks at an accessible price point","Fox 2.0 Performance maintains consistent damping on sustained trail use better than the Bilstein 5100","King OEM Performance shocks are the clear winner for managing high-frequency trail feedback and driver fatigue","Correct-length aftermarket shocks restore proper travel distribution at lifted ride height \u2014 factory shocks can't do this","Shock upgrade is one of the most accessible technical installs on the JL \u2014 straightforward bolt-on with basic tools"],"cons":["Bilstein 5100 is not adjustable \u2014 no ability to tune between street and trail configurations","Fox Performance Elite adjustability is meaningful but requires understanding of what the adjustment does; incorrect settings can degrade performance","King shocks at $1,325 are a significant investment \u2014 prioritize against other build items unless shocks are the bottleneck","4xe rear fitment is different from standard JL \u2014 must specify 4xe compatibility when ordering rear shocks","All aftermarket shocks require correct length spec matched to your lift height \u2014 ordering the wrong length results in incorrect travel distribution"]},{"id":"jl-track-bar","title":"Front Track Bar Upgrade \u2014 Lifted JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"suspension","subcategory":"track_bar","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":95,"high":375},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","measuring tape"],"skills_required":["basic suspension work","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["track-bar","suspension","lift","steering","handling","front-axle"],"parts":[{"name":"TeraFlex Front Adjustable Track Bar (2018\u20132024 JL)","vendor":"TeraFlex","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Synergy Mfg JL Front Track Bar (Heavy Duty Adjustable)","vendor":"Synergy","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rough Country Adjustable Front Track Bar (JL Wrangler)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Metalcloak Front Track Bar (JL, 2\u20136\" Lift Compatible)","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":375}],"citations":[{"title":"TeraFlex JL Track Bar Product Page","url":"https://www.teraflex.com"},{"title":"Synergy JL Suspension Line","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com"}],"alternatives":["jl-budget-lift","jl-mid-lift","jl-shocks-upgrade"],"summary":"Lifting the JL without correcting the front track bar length shifts the front axle toward the passenger side \u2014 you'll see it in the steering wheel offset and feel it in the highway stability. An adjustable replacement takes about an hour to install and corrects the geometry properly.","description":"The JL Wrangler's front suspension uses a track bar (panhard bar) to locate the front axle laterally. At stock height the geometry is correct. Lift the JL by 2\" or more and the arc the track bar traces no longer keeps the axle centered \u2014 it pulls slightly toward the passenger side. The visual tell is a steering wheel that needs to sit a few degrees off-center to drive straight. The mechanical consequence is uneven steering input, additional stress on the front end components, and a contributing factor to steering wander at highway speeds.\n\nThe JL's front end differs from the JK in ways that matter here: the JL uses an independent high-steer geometry on some builds, and the track bar bracket geometry changed between generations. JL-specific track bars are not interchangeable with JK parts, so confirm vehicle fitment before ordering.\n\nA fixed-length \"heavy duty\" replacement that doesn't match your lift height just trades one geometry problem for another at a different height. Every option listed here is adjustable \u2014 set the length to center your axle at whatever lift you're running, and re-adjust if you change lift height later. This is the correct approach.\n\nTeraFlex's JL-specific adjustable track bar is a well-documented choice for 2\"\u20134.5\" lifts. Synergy's heavy-duty unit runs slightly larger diameter tubing and is the better buy for serious trail rigs that articulate aggressively. Rough Country's unit is the budget entry and does the job for mild-to-moderate builds. Metalcloak's unit is the premium option built for high-articulation rock crawling setups.\n\nThe JL front track bar has one end at the frame (driver side) and one end at the axle bracket (passenger side). Both ends use bolts, not press-fit joints. Torque the frame end to 65\u201370 ft-lb and the axle end to 55\u201360 ft-lb \u2014 but torque these only after the vehicle is at full ride height (weight on the suspension), not with the axle hanging free.\n\nAfter install, measure axle centering: tape from the inside of each front tire to the nearest frame reference point at equal height. Equal measurements mean the axle is centered. If the steering wheel still sits off-center after axle centering, a tie rod or drag link adjustment may be needed in addition to the track bar.","steps":["Loosen and remove the stock track bar at both ends with the vehicle on jack stands. Note that the axle shifts when the bar comes off.","Thread the adjustable track bar to approximately stock length as a starting point.","Install at frame end first (snug, not torqued), then axle end (snug).","Lower the vehicle to full ride height \u2014 wheels on ground, weight on suspension.","Measure axle centering from inside front tires to a frame reference. Adjust track bar length as needed.","Torque frame-end bolt to 65 ft-lb. Torque axle-end bolt to 55 ft-lb. Torque jam nut on adjustable section.","Check steering wheel centering on a level surface. If off, a minor tie rod adjustment will correct it.","Road test. Re-check torque after 100 miles."],"pros":["Corrects front axle offset from lift \u2014 addresses a root cause of highway wander","Adjustable design accommodates future lift changes without buying another bar","1\u20132 hour install with basic hand tools \u2014 high return on time invested","Eliminates uneven steering input from an off-center axle"],"cons":["Does not correct other front-end geometry issues \u2014 worn or bent tie rods need separate attention","Adjustable rod ends require re-torque checks after trail cycles \u2014 not a set-and-forget item","Lifts over 5\" may require a track bar relocation bracket in addition to the adjustable bar","Jam nut must be torqued to spec; loose jam nuts allow the adjustment to walk during trail use"]},{"id":"jl-compression-test","title":"JL Wrangler Compression Test (3.6L Pentastar)","vehicle":"jl","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":80},"tools_required":["compression tester","spark plug socket (5/8\")","ratchet and extension","12mm socket for coil packs","OBD2 scanner","helper or remote starter (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","multimeter or scanner use"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["diagnostic","compression","engine-health","pentastar","tools_diagnostic"],"summary":"A compression test on the 3.6L Pentastar takes about 90 minutes including coil pack removal. Healthy numbers are 170\u2013210 PSI per cylinder with no more than 15 PSI variation between cylinders. Anything below 150 PSI or a large spread warrants a wet test and further diagnosis.","description":"# JL Wrangler Compression Test (3.6L Pentastar)\n\nA compression test tells you whether the engine's fundamental mechanical health is intact \u2014 rings, valves, head gasket. It's the right tool when you're diagnosing misfires that don't respond to ignition fixes, buying a used JL, or investigating unexplained oil consumption. The 3.6L has 6 cylinders in a V configuration, so you're pulling 6 spark plugs and testing both banks.\n\n- Compression tester with a 14mm spark plug port adapter (most test kits include this)\n- 5/8\" spark plug socket (deep, with rubber insert)\n- 12mm socket for coil packs\n- 3\" and 6\" extensions\n- Ratchet\n- OBD2 scanner to disable fuel and ignition (see below)\n- Helper to crank the engine, OR a remote starter switch\n- Pen and paper to log each cylinder\n\n**Compression spec (3.6L Pentastar):**\n- Target: 170\u2013210 PSI per cylinder\n- Acceptable minimum: 150 PSI\n- Max variation between cylinders: 15 PSI\n\n**Prepare the engine**\n1. Run the engine until fully warm. Compression tests done on a cold engine read slightly low and can mask borderline cylinders.\n2. Disable the fuel system: pull the fuel pump relay from the under-hood PDC. Location is labeled on the cover \u2014 it's typically labeled FP RELAY or FUEL PUMP.\n3. Disable the ignition coils: you can unplug each coil individually, or some prefer to use a scanner to disable injection. Cranking without disabling both fuel and ignition can hydrolock if fuel sprays into a cylinder while you're cranking.\n\n**Remove coil packs and spark plugs**\n4. The coil packs are individual units \u2014 one per cylinder. Unplug the electrical connector on each, then remove the single 12mm bolt holding each coil. Pull the coil straight up and out.\n5. Remove all 6 spark plugs using the 5/8\" plug socket. Keep them organized \u2014 you'll want to examine each for deposits, oil fouling, or unusual coloration.\n\n**Test each cylinder**\n6. Thread the compression tester adapter into the first cylinder's spark plug hole (hand-tight \u2014 don't torque it).\n7. Open the throttle fully \u2014 either hold it by hand or wedge it open. Closed throttle restricts airflow and gives falsely low readings.\n8. Crank the engine for 4\u20136 complete revolutions (3\u20135 seconds of cranking). Watch the tester gauge \u2014 it will climb and then stop climbing. Record the number.\n9. Press the release valve to bleed pressure, then move to the next cylinder. Test all six.\n\n**Wet test (if a cylinder reads low)**\n10. If any cylinder reads below 150 PSI or is more than 15 PSI below its neighbors, do a wet test: add a tablespoon of clean engine oil through the spark plug hole, then retest that cylinder.\n- **Compression increases significantly**: Worn or damaged piston rings \u2014 oil temporarily seals the ring-to-wall gap.\n- **Compression unchanged**: Valve problem (burnt valve, worn valve seat) or head gasket issue \u2014 oil can't seal those leaks.\n\n| Result | Likely cause |\n|---|---|\n| All cylinders within spec | Engine mechanically healthy |\n| One or two cylinders low, wet test improves | Ring wear \u2014 evaluate by consumption, blow-by, and age |\n| One cylinder consistently low, wet test no change | Burnt valve or head gasket \u2014 further testing needed |\n| Two adjacent cylinders low | Head gasket between those cylinders |\n| All cylinders uniformly low | Timing chain stretch or timing off \u2014 not ring or valve wear |\n\n- **Cranking speed matters**: A discharged battery or slow cranking gives falsely low readings. Test with a fully charged battery.\n- **Throttle position**: Partially closed throttle is the most common reason for low readings that don't match any failure pattern. Hold it fully open.\n- **Re-installing spark plugs**: Install new plugs if the old ones are at or beyond their service interval (60,000\u2013100,000 miles depending on plug type). Plugs cost $5\u2013$12 each \u2014 if you're this deep into the job, replace them.\n- **Coil pack inspection**: While the coils are out, inspect the boots for cracking and check the firing tips. Crack the boots and feel for carbon tracking. Replace any that show damage.\n\n- Compression tester (Actron, OTC): $30\u2013$60 (https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000IXPBOA?tag=trailmanual0b-20)\n- OEM spark plugs (set of 6, NGK for JL): $35\u2013$55\n- Coil pack (individual, if failed): $25\u2013$45 each (Mopar or NGK)\n\nIf a compression test reveals worn rings on a high-mileage JL, get a leakdown test before deciding on repairs \u2014 it gives more precise information about which component is failing and how badly."},{"id":"jl-obd2-diagnostics","title":"OBD2 Diagnostics for the JL Wrangler \u2014 Common Codes and What They Mean","vehicle":"jl","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":120},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner"],"skills_required":["basic diagnostics"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["diagnostic","obd2","check-engine-light","tools_diagnostic","fault-codes"],"summary":"A Bluetooth OBD2 adapter and a phone app gives you live data, fault codes, and the ability to clear the check-engine light on your JL for $30\u2013$50. The JL speaks CAN bus \u2014 most modern scanners work. The codes that matter most: P0300 (misfire), P0087 (fuel pressure), P0016/17 (cam/crank correlation), and U-codes (CAN bus communication faults).","description":"# OBD2 Diagnostics for the JL Wrangler \u2014 Common Codes and What They Mean\n\nEvery JL Wrangler since 2018 has an OBD2 port under the driver's side dash \u2014 it's the 16-pin trapezoid connector. A scanner plugged in here gives you access to DTCs (diagnostic trouble codes), live sensor data, and the ability to clear warning lights.\n\nThe JL uses FCA's CAN bus architecture. Standard OBD2 codes (P, B, C, U) all work. Enhanced codes that go deeper into Jeep-specific systems require a scanner with FCA support \u2014 Autel scanners, FORScan (with a paid license for Chrysler), or Mopar's own DRB-III successor tool.\n\n- OBD2 scanner or Bluetooth adapter:\n  - Entry level: Ancel AD310 or Autel AL319 ($30\u2013$50) \u2014 reads/clears codes, basic live data\n  - Mid-tier: BlueDriver Bluetooth + app ($120) \u2014 reads codes, live data, enhanced FCA codes, pending codes\n  - App-based (Bluetooth): OBD Auto Doctor or Torque Pro + ELM327 Bluetooth adapter ($20\u2013$30) \u2014 code reading and live data, no enhanced Jeep codes without an upgraded adapter\n\n**Engine and drivetrain:**\n\n| Code | Description | Common cause on JL |\n|---|---|---|\n| P0300\u2013P0306 | Random/cylinder misfire | Worn spark plugs, bad coil, carbon-fouled injector |\n| P0087 | Fuel rail pressure low | Failing fuel pump, clogged filter sock |\n| P0016 / P0017 | Cam-crank correlation (bank 1 or 2) | Oil sludge on cam phasers, low oil level, stretched timing chain |\n| P0128 | Coolant temp below thermostat | Stuck-open thermostat |\n| P0191 | Fuel rail pressure sensor | Sensor failure or wiring issue |\n| P0420 | Catalyst efficiency low | Failing O2 sensor or aging catalytic converter |\n| P219A / P219B | Air-fuel ratio imbalance | Vacuum leak, injector fouling (common on DI engines) |\n\n**Electrical and network:**\n\n| Code | Description | Common cause on JL |\n|---|---|---|\n| U0001 | High-speed CAN bus fault | Wiring damage, aftermarket accessories interfering |\n| U0100 | Lost communication with ECM | Module failure or power supply issue |\n| B1000 | Airbag control unit fault | Common after battery disconnect \u2014 clears itself |\n\n**Evap and emissions:**\n\n| Code | Description | Common cause on JL |\n|---|---|---|\n| P0456 | Evap small leak | Loose or cracked gas cap (check cap first) |\n| P0440 / P0442 | Evap system leak | Cracked hose, damaged charcoal canister |\n\nBeyond codes, live data is where OBD2 gets useful. Key PIDs to monitor on a JL:\n\n- **Fuel trims** (STFT, LTFT): Should be within \u00b15% at idle. Values higher than +10% mean the engine is running lean (vacuum leak, injector issue). Values lower than -10% mean rich (O2 sensor, MAF reading high).\n- **Engine coolant temp**: Should reach 192\u2013210\u00b0F at normal operating temp and stabilize. If it never gets there \u2192 stuck-open thermostat. If it climbs past 230\u00b0F \u2192 cooling system issue.\n- **Fuel rail pressure** (3.6L at idle): 56\u201366 PSI. If consistently below 50 PSI \u2192 pump trending toward failure.\n- **O2 sensor voltage** (3.6L): Should cycle between 0.1\u20130.9V at idle after warmup. A sensor stuck at one value is failing.\n\nClearing a code does not fix the problem \u2014 it resets the monitor. If the condition still exists, the code returns within one drive cycle. Clear codes after a confirmed repair, not as a way to make the light disappear temporarily.\n\nAfter clearing, the JL's emissions monitors need one or more complete drive cycles to reset. An emissions inspection will fail if monitors show \"Not Ready.\" Give it 2\u20133 days of normal driving before an inspection after clearing.\n\n- **Enhanced codes vs. standard codes**: P, B, C, U codes from the standard OBD2 spec are readable by any scanner. Jeep-specific enhanced codes (often in the P1xxx and C1xxx range) require FCA-licensed software.\n- **Aftermarket accessory interference**: Auxiliary lights wired directly to the battery (not through a proper relay/fuse circuit) can generate CAN bus faults. If U-codes appear after installing accessories, audit your wiring first.\n- **Battery disconnect resets**: JL electronics are sensitive to battery disconnects. After any battery work, expect temporarily cleared settings (radio, windows, TPMS) and possibly some module faults that clear after a drive cycle.\n\n- Ancel AD310 entry OBD2 scanner: ~$30 ([Amazon](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B01G5EA74I?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- BlueDriver Bluetooth OBD2 (best all-around for DIY): ~$120 ([Amazon](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B00652G4TS?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- ELM327 Bluetooth adapter (for Torque Pro): $15\u2013$25"},{"id":"jl-tire-rotation-balancing","title":"Tire Rotation and Balancing \u2014 JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"wheels_tires","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":80},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","lug wrench or impact"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["tires","wheels_tires","maintenance","rotation","balancing"],"summary":"JL Wranglers wear tires unevenly \u2014 the front end is heavy and the off-road use pattern accelerates edge wear on larger tires. Rotate every 5,000\u20137,500 miles. If you carry a full-size spare (most trail builds do), include it in a 5-tire rotation pattern.","description":"# Tire Rotation and Balancing \u2014 JL Wrangler\n\nJeep spec calls for tire rotation every 6,000 miles (or at every oil change). Off-road use adds irregular wear patterns from aired-down running, side-hilling, and hard braking on trail descents \u2014 rotate more frequently if you trail ride regularly.\n\nThe JL's weight distribution puts more load on the front tires (the 3.6L engine is heavy) and the front axle does all of the steering. Front tires wear faster and wear differently \u2014 usually more on the outer edges due to camber effects from lift. A proper rotation extends total tire life by 15\u201325%, which matters when your 35s cost $200 each.\n\n- Floor jack and jack stands (4 stands for a full lift)\n- Torque wrench\n- Impact wrench or breaker bar for lug nuts\n- Tire chalk or paint marker (to track rotation history)\n- Balancing: this part requires a shop \u2014 you cannot balance tires in a home garage without a balancing machine\n\n**Without a spare in the rotation (4-tire pattern):**\nThe JL's non-directional tires (most all-terrain tires are non-directional) rotate front-to-rear on the same side. The simpler \"X pattern\" (cross front to rear, crossing sides) evens wear more aggressively and is preferred for lifted JLs.\n\nX-pattern: RF \u2192 LR, LF \u2192 RR, RR \u2192 LF, LR \u2192 RF\n\n**With full-size spare (5-tire rotation \u2014 recommended for serious trail rigs):**\nIncluding the spare distributes wear across all five tires, extending the set life by ~20%. Use the forward cross-5 pattern:\nLF \u2192 spare, RF \u2192 LF, spare \u2192 RR, LR \u2192 RF, RR \u2192 LR\n\nMark the spare position each rotation so you can track where each tire has been.\n\n**Directional tires (arrow on sidewall):** Must stay on the same side of the vehicle \u2014 only front-to-rear rotation is allowed. No X-pattern. Many aggressive mud tires are directional.\n\n1. Chock the wheels that will stay on the ground.\n2. Loosen all lug nuts one turn before lifting.\n3. Lift the vehicle and support on jack stands \u2014 use the frame rails, not the axle differential pumpkins.\n4. Remove wheels and tires. Lay them out in their new positions before moving anything.\n5. Inspect each tire: look for uneven wear patterns, cracking in the sidewalls, embedded rocks or nails, and damage to the bead area.\n6. Move each tire to its new position. Thread lug nuts by hand first.\n7. Lower the vehicle partially onto the tires (enough weight to prevent spinning) and torque in a star pattern:\n   - JL lug nut torque: **130 ft-lb**\n8. Lower fully, do a final torque pass, reinstall the spare cover if applicable.\n9. Check tire pressure on all five tires after rotation \u2014 they often bleed air during a trail run and the rotation is a good time to normalize.\n\nBalance the tires whenever:\n- You notice steering wheel vibration at highway speed (55\u201370 MPH typically)\n- You install a new tire\n- You remove a weight from a wheel (they fall off on trail sometimes)\n\nBalance at a shop \u2014 $15\u2013$25 per tire is normal. Tell them it's an off-road vehicle and ask about heavy-duty balancing weights \u2014 the plastic clip-on weights often fall off on trail vibration. Stick-on or bolt-on weights hold better.\n\n**Air-down and reinflation:** Airing down on trail can cause beads to shift slightly, which throws off balance. If you're airing down to 15 PSI regularly, expect to rebalance more frequently \u2014 every 2\u20133 rotations rather than every 5.\n\n- **One-side wear on lifted JLs**: If your front tires are consistently wearing on the outer edge, you need an alignment check \u2014 specifically caster correction and camber adjustment. Rotation delays the symptom but doesn't fix the root cause.\n- **Lug nut torque matters**: JL hub spigots are alloy \u2014 undertorqued lug nuts cause wheel wobble and can damage the studs. 130 ft-lb, star pattern, always.\n- **Mud-packed wheels**: Trail mud packed behind the wheel unbalances it. Rinse the wheels clean before balancing.\n\n- Tire rotation at a shop: $20\u2013$50 (often free with tire purchase)\n- Balancing per tire: $15\u2013$25\n- Torque wrench (if you don't have one): $35\u2013$80 \u2014 worth owning; you'll use it constantly on a Jeep ([example](https://www.amazon.com/dp/B000BVEKET?tag=trailmanual0b-20))\n- Tire pressure gauge: $10\u2013$20 (get one that reads down to 5 PSI for trail use)"},{"id":"jl-tire-fitment-37s","title":"Running 37\" Tires on the JL \u2014 Rubicon vs Non-Rubicon Fitment","vehicle":"jl","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":1200,"high":2000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","lug wrench","TPMS programming tool (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","tire mounting awareness"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["tires","37s","fitment","wheels","regear"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 37x12.5R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Nitto Trail Grappler M/T 37x12.5R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Toyo Open Country M/T 37x12.5R17","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":285}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep JL Wrangler Tire Fitment Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/article/jeep-wrangler-jl-tire-fitment"},{"title":"BFGoodrich KM3 Tire Sizes and Specs","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com/tires/mud-terrain-ta-km3"},{"title":"Nitto Trail Grappler Product Page","url":"https://www.nittotire.com/tires/trail-grappler-m-t"}],"alternatives":["jl-wheels","jl-mid-lift","jl-regear"],"summary":"The JL Rubicon is the only Wrangler that fits 37s from the factory without modification \u2014 and even on the Rubicon you will get occasional flex rubbing. On every other JL trim, 37s require a lift and a plan.","description":"Jeep was deliberate about the JL Rubicon's tire clearance. The Rubicon comes with a 1\" front lift, wider fender flares, and 33\" tires from the factory \u2014 but the geometry allows for 37s with minimal clearance work at full flex. In practice, 37s on a stock Rubicon rub at full steering lock combined with full bump travel. The rub points are the front upper control arm at the coil bucket and the rear fender liner at full droop. Most Rubicon owners address this with a 1\"\u20131.5\" spacer lift or minor fender liner trimming without a full kit.\n\nNon-Rubicon JL trim levels (Sport, Sport S, Sahara) are a different situation. The factory fender openings are narrower, the suspension geometry is lower, and the tires are 31\"\u201333\" from the factory. Fitting 37s without a lift results in rubbing at every steering and suspension extreme \u2014 you need 2.5\" of lift minimum, and 3.5\" is the more comfortable clearance baseline. At 2.5\" you will still need fender liner removal (not cutting, just pulling the liner) at the rear, and the front upper control arm area needs attention at full steering lock. At 3.5\", 37s fit cleanly across all four corners.\n\nThe tire choices matter for fitment as much as the size. A 37x12.5R17 is the most common spec, and all three recommended options here are in that range. Note that actual measured width varies by brand \u2014 the BFG KM3 at 37x12.5 measures close to nominal, the Nitto Trail Grappler runs slightly narrower, and the Toyo Open Country MT runs slightly wider depending on the wheel backspacing. On a 17x8.5 or 17x9 wheel with 4.5\"\u20135\" backspace, all three mount cleanly.\n\n**Regear is not optional at 37s.** Stock gear ratios on the Sahara (3.45) and Sport (3.73) were engineered around 31\"\u201333\" tires. Adding 37s effectively drops your RPM at highway speed and loads the drivetrain heavily off-road. The Sahara with 3.45 gears and 37s is genuinely painful to drive \u2014 low-end torque evaporates, the transmission hunts gears on grades, and trail crawl ratios are inadequate. Plan for 4.56 gears with 35s, 4.88 gears with 37s on Sahara/Sport. Rubicon owners with the 4.10 stock ratio can run 37s tolerable, but 4.56 is the right answer for serious trail use.\n\n**TPMS:** The JL uses a direct TPMS system \u2014 sensors in each wheel. When you move to aftermarket wheels, the sensors must either be transferred from factory wheels or new sensors purchased and programmed. TPMS sensors for the JL are vehicle-specific; verify compatibility before ordering.","steps":["If installing new wheels alongside new tires, mount tires on new wheels at a tire shop \u2014 spec 4.5\"\u20135\" backspacing for lifted JL builds.","Verify TPMS sensors are transferred or new compatible sensors are installed before mounting.","Lift the vehicle on jack stands and remove factory wheels.","Install new wheel/tire assemblies. Torque lug nuts to 100 ft-lb in a star pattern.","Lower the vehicle and re-torque after 50 miles.","Turn steering wheel to full lock in both directions and articulate suspension through full travel (ramp or spotter needed for this) to check for rub points.","If rubbing is present: identify the location and address \u2014 rear fender liner removal is a 20-minute task, front upper control arm rub may require trim cutting or a minor lift correction.","If no regear is yet planned, reset tire size in the vehicle's computer (speedometer calibration). JL uses the Uconnect system; some builds use a programmer or dealer reflash for this."],"pros":["37s are the meaningful capability threshold for serious trail work \u2014 more traction footprint, more ground clearance, better over-rock rollover","On the Rubicon, the modification scope is genuinely minor \u2014 the vehicle was designed to accommodate them","BFG KM3, Nitto Trail Grappler, and Toyo OPMT are all proven tires with extensive JL trail-use history","Proper 37s make the JL look proportional \u2014 the body was designed with large tires in mind"],"cons":["Non-Rubicon fitment requires 2.5\"+ lift \u2014 that cost is additive to the tire cost","Regear is functionally required on Sport (3.73) and Sahara (3.45) \u2014 budget an additional $1,800\u2013$2,400 for a proper gear change","37s are heavy \u2014 rotating mass increase affects acceleration, braking, and fuel economy noticeably","TPMS sensor compatibility requires verification for aftermarket wheels; factory sensors may need reprogramming","A full set of 37s plus a quality wheel set plus regear is a $4,000\u2013$5,000 package total \u2014 budget realistically"]},{"id":"jl-wheel-spacers","title":"Wheel Spacers \u2014 1.25\" to 1.5\" for the JL Wrangler","vehicle":"jl","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheel_spacers","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":220},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","wire brush (lug seat cleanup)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["wheel-spacers","spacer","wheels","tires","fitment","5x5","5x127"],"parts":[{"name":"Spidertrax 1.25\" Wheel Spacers (5x5, 1/2\"\u201320 stud) \u2014 pair","vendor":"Spidertrax","price_estimate_usd":149},{"name":"BORA 1.5\" Hub-Centric Spacers (5x127mm) \u2014 pair","vendor":"BORA","price_estimate_usd":119},{"name":"Rugged Ridge 1.5\" Wheel Spacers (JL, 5x5) \u2014 pair","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"TeraFlex 1.25\" Wheel Spacer Kit (5x5, JL Wrangler) \u2014 pair","vendor":"TeraFlex","price_estimate_usd":119}],"citations":[{"title":"Spidertrax JL Wheel Spacer Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.spidertrax.com"},{"title":"BORA Wheel Spacers JL Application","url":"https://www.borawheelspacers.com"}],"alternatives":["jl-wheels","jl-tire-fitment-37s"],"summary":"A 1.25\"\u20131.5\" hub-centric spacer on the JL pushes the wheel out enough to clear inner fender interference on larger tires and fills the wheel wells visually without committing to an aftermarket wheel purchase.","description":"The JL Wrangler runs a 5\u00d75 (5\u00d7127mm) bolt pattern with a 71.5mm center bore. Most trims come with modest stock offsets \u2014 the Sport and Sahara sit around +44mm, the Rubicon at a slightly more aggressive +30mm. When you move to 35\" or larger tires, particularly on the Sport and Sahara, the stock offset can leave tire contact with the lower inner fender on full articulation. A 1.25\"\u20131.5\" spacer resolves that without requiring a wheel swap.\n\nHub-centric fit is the non-negotiable specification here. A hub-centric spacer seats on the hub's center bore, keeping the wheel centered by the hub rather than relying on lug torque alone. Lug-centric spacers exist and are cheaper \u2014 they also allow microscopic hub runout to become vibration. Buy hub-centric.\n\nThe 1.25\" spacer is the sweet spot for a JL running 35s on a mild-to-moderate lift. It resolves common inner fender contact and adds visual stance without pushing the wheel to the outer edge of the fender flare. The 1.5\" spacer works for builds with wider aftermarket fenders or for owners running 37s who want maximum clearance on articulation.\n\nSpidertrax is the long-standing premium choice in this category \u2014 their bolt-on spacers use extended studs, which are mechanically superior to a spacer with its own stud pattern that stacks studs. BORA makes a quality hub-centric unit at a lower price point. Rugged Ridge's OEM-style spacers are the budget option and perform the task without drama.\n\nDo not run spacers over 1.5\" on stock JL wheel studs without upgrading to extended studs \u2014 the engagement length on the lug nut drops to marginal beyond that thickness.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose on the wheel before jacking. Jack the vehicle, remove the wheel.","Clean the hub face and the spacer mating surfaces with a wire brush \u2014 rust or debris causes runout.","Install the spacer onto the hub, aligning the center bore. Thread the included extended studs or lug bolts.","Torque the spacer mounting hardware to 95\u2013100 ft-lb in a star pattern.","Reinstall the wheel over the spacer studs. Torque lug nuts to 100 ft-lb in a star pattern.","Lower the vehicle and perform a final torque check on the lug nuts with the vehicle at full weight.","Re-torque after the first 50 miles."],"pros":["Resolves inner fender contact on 35s and 37s without a wheel purchase","Hub-centric design eliminates vibration from runout \u2014 fits like a proper part","1.25\"\u20131.5\" stays within the JL's fender flare footprint \u2014 no additional cutting needed","Reversible if you later switch to offset wheels that don't need the spacer"],"cons":["Extended studs (or included stud hardware) must be torqued to spec \u2014 undertorquing causes loosening on trail","Spacers over 1.5\" require upgraded studs for safe lug engagement \u2014 do not skip this step","Adds a maintenance item: re-torque after first 50 miles and check periodically on trail rigs","Budget slip-on (lug-centric) spacers are not acceptable for off-road use \u2014 pay for hub-centric"]},{"id":"jl-wheels","title":"Wheel Selection for the JL \u2014 Steel, Alloy, and Beadlock","vehicle":"jl","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":1480},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","TPMS awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2018-2024"],"tags":["wheels","rims","beadlock","fitment"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Series 31 Steel Wheel 17x9 -6mm offset","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 305 NV 17x8.5 0mm offset","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Fuel Boost D533 17x9 -12mm offset","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"KMC Machete Crawl Beadlock 17x9 -12mm offset","vendor":"KMC Wheels","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels 305 NV Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/collections/jeep-wrangler-jl-wheels"},{"title":"Fuel Off-Road D533 Boost Wheel Specs","url":"https://fuelmfg.com/wheels/d533-boost.html"},{"title":"KMC Machete Crawl Beadlock Overview","url":"https://www.kmcwheels.com/products/machete-crawl-beadlock"}],"alternatives":["jl-tire-fitment-37s"],"summary":"The JL uses a 5x127 (5x5\") bolt pattern and needs 4.5\"\u20135\" of backspacing on most lifted builds \u2014 get those two numbers right and the wheel selection becomes straightforward.","description":"Wheel selection for the JL is primarily a function of bolt pattern, backspacing, and use case. The bolt pattern is fixed: 5x127mm (often called 5x5\"). Backspacing determines how far the wheel face sits inside the fender relative to the wheel centerline \u2014 too much backspace and the tire tucks too far under the fender, creating clearance issues on a lifted rig; too little and the tire pokes out beyond the fender flares, requiring flare extensions or risking legality issues in some states. For a lifted JL running 35\"\u201337\" tires, 4.5\"\u20135\" backspacing puts the tire face at the fender edge on most builds.\n\nSteel wheels (Pro Comp Series 31 at $95 each) are the right call for builds where rock contact is expected. Steel deforms on impact rather than cracking, which means a bent steel wheel is usually fixable trailside with a hammer and a tire iron while a cracked alloy wheel is not. The Pro Comp Series 31 is a functional, no-frills 17x9 in the correct backspace for JL trail use. At $95 per wheel, replacing a badly damaged one isn't catastrophic.\n\nThe Method 305 NV at $175 per wheel is the benchmark alloy option. Method's NV (No Void) design eliminates spokes entirely, which means no stress concentrations at spoke roots \u2014 the common failure point on spoke-design alloy wheels. The face is a machined flat disc. It's heavier than spoked alloys but more impact-resistant. Method's quality control is consistent and their 17x8.5 0mm offset works well on lifted JL builds. This is a popular choice because it's functional on the trail and looks reasonable on the street.\n\nThe Fuel Boost D533 at $185 per wheel is the value alloy option with a more aggressive aesthetic. The offset (-12mm) provides slightly more tire clearance than the Method on non-Rubicon fenders. Fuel's construction quality is solid for the price point, and the D533 is widely available.\n\nThe KMC Machete Crawl Beadlock at $320 per wheel is the choice for builds that run reduced tire pressure on technical terrain. Beadlock wheels clamp the tire bead mechanically, preventing tire de-beading at low pressures (12\u201320 psi range). The KMC unit is a two-piece design with an inner ring that can be retorqued periodically \u2014 correct maintenance is required. DOT-approved beadlocks don't exist in the traditional sense; the Machete Crawl is labeled for off-road use only, which has legal implications for street use in some states.\n\n**TPMS:** The JL uses a direct TPMS system. When changing to aftermarket wheels, your factory TPMS sensors must be transferred by a tire shop (requires proper tools) or replaced with aftermarket sensors verified for JL compatibility. Budget $15\u2013$20 per sensor for new ones if not reusing factory sensors.","steps":["Transfer or install TPMS sensors onto new wheels before mounting tires. This is done at a tire shop with a sensor programming tool.","Mount tires onto new wheels at the tire shop, specifying the correct mounting torque for bead seating.","For beadlock wheels (KMC Machete Crawl): the outer ring must be installed with the tire, with ring bolt torque set to spec and re-checked at first install \u2014 consult the KMC installation instructions for bolt pattern and torque spec.","Lift the vehicle on jack stands, remove factory wheels.","Install new wheel/tire assemblies. Torque lug nuts to 100 ft-lb in a star pattern.","Lower vehicle and re-torque lug nuts after 50 miles.","Confirm backspacing clearance at full steering lock and full flex \u2014 look for rubbing at the inner fender liner, upper control arm, and spring perch."],"pros":["Method 305 NV's no-void design is the most impact-resistant alloy option in this price range","Steel wheels (Pro Comp) are the practical choice for builds that regularly contact rocks \u2014 cheaper to replace than alloys","KMC Machete Crawl beadlocks enable low-pressure trail operation that dramatically improves rock-crawl traction","Correct backspacing on all listed options for lifted JL builds \u2014 no wheel spacers required","All options listed cover the full 5x127 bolt pattern JL requirement"],"cons":["TPMS sensor transfer or replacement adds $60\u2013$100 to the wheel cost and requires a shop visit","Beadlock wheels (KMC) require periodic ring bolt torque checks \u2014 not a set-and-forget install","Alloy wheels (Method, Fuel) crack rather than deform on severe impact \u2014 a cracked alloy wheel ends the trail day","Steel wheels are heavy \u2014 rotating mass from steel wheels is measurably higher than alloys, affecting acceleration and fuel economy","Backspacing should be confirmed against your specific lift height and tire width before ordering \u2014 published specs are starting points, not guarantees"]}];